window.DISASTER_DASHBOARD_DATA.narrativeChunks["2007_000"]=[[60480,"While the federal appropriation for WTC disaster relief allows an allocation of up to 10 percent of the $700 million award for administration and planning, Empire State Development Corporation, in cooperation with the NYC Economic Development Corporation, has allocated $14 million for administration. These activities provide for the coordination and administration of business assistance programs funded through the federal appropriation, centralization of application processing and disbursement of funds, access to assistance information, and records-keeping. Coordination of these activities greatly improves the efficiency of the delivery of disaster assistance, reduces the risk of abuse, and provides the opportunity to link resources to further maximize their benefits."],[20679,"130 LIBERTY BUILDING DECONSTRUCTION-- On October 3, 2006 the New York City Department of Buildings approved the plan for installation of the Liberty Street Bridge at the northern edge of the site, to permit the reconfiguration and stacking of the construction trailers. On November 13, 2006, Liberty Street bridge erection began and as of December 31, 2006, bridge erection was complete and power and utilities were being run to the structure. Regulators reviewed and approved the conditions on the roof and the 39th and 38th floors on November 10, 2006. Regulators also reviewed and approved conditions on floors 35 through 37 on November 14, 2006. As a result, the five floor interior abatement buffer between deconstruction and abatement was complete. Final abatement treatment called encapsulation (the spraying on of a binding matrix to all building components) started on floors 35 through 39 on November 17, 2006. On November 27, 2006, exterior façade removal of aluminum metal fascias and column covers commenced on the roof, floor 39 and floor 38. 10,762 square feet of material had been removed as of December 31, 2006. Pre-abatement work, including setting up of decontamination units and achieving negative air pressure commenced on floors 26 and 27 on November 20, 2006, by December 31, 2006, environmental work on these floors was approximately 15% complete. Pre-abatement work also began on floors 28 and 29 on November 7, By December 31, environmental work was approximately 28 % complete on these floors. An average of four (4) 100 yard containers of porous materials bagged and to be disposed of as ACM (Asbestos Containing Material) were removed from the site each week day between November 15, 2006 and December 31, 2006. In the quarter ending December 31, 2006, approximately..."],[60562,"LMDC GENERAL ADMINISTRATION --- LMDC maintains a staff and office space to carry out its planning and community development activities, including general management, oversight, monitoring, and coordination. In addition, LMDC provides local officials, businesses, and citizens with information about programs. The following provides information on activities occurring during the quarter ending December 31, 2006. PUBLIC PARTICIPATION --- ADVISORY COUNCIL MEETINGS-- LMDC holds Advisory Council meetings that allow for a broad spectrum of groups affected by the World Trade Center attacks (including victims&apos; family members, business owners, arts and cultural institutions, and downtown residents to consult with LMDC on issues of concern to their respective constituencies. --PUBLIC HEARINGS, MEETINGS, AND OTHER OUTRECH-- LMDC conducts additional outreach initiatives on a continuous basis to city, state, and federal elected officials, civic and community organizations, and victims families, participates in Community Board meetings, and meets regularly with community groups, civic organizations, and public officials to ensure that the opinions of those who were affected are taken into account. The LMDC website also solicits public feedback on all of its initiatives, including WTC site planning, transportation, Lower Manhattan neighborhoods, the WTC Site Memorial, and Partial Action Plans. --SECTION 106 COORDINATION AND OUTREACH-- A coordinated Determination of National Register Eligibility dated March 31, 2004 found the entire WTC Site eligible for listing on the National Register of Historic Places. LMDC continues to provide informational updates to consulting parties regarding the ongoing planning process at the WTC Site, consistent with the processes that brought about t..."],[61920,"AIRPORT ACCESS ALTERNATIVES ANALYSES: The alternatives and environmental impact analysis is jointly conducted by the LMDC, the Port Authority of New York and New Jersey, the Metropolitan Transportation Authority (MTA) and the City of New York. The Port Authority has programmed $60 million for planning and engineering services relating to this effort. The MTA is managing the contract with the consultants who are conducting the ongoing alternatives and environmental impact analysis. More information about this project is available at the Metropolitan Transportation Authority¿s web site at www.mta.info/mta/planning/lmlink. --In the fourth quarter of 2006, numerous alternatives were evaluated based upon the evaluation criteria that was presented to the public. These criteria include: travel time, number of commuter/ airport transfers, impacts to existing services, available capacity for peak services, capital costs, and future connectivity to other regional transportation links, service frequency, impacts to existing infrastructure, environmental impacts, and institutional impacts. When the analysis is completed, it will be shared with the public for their review and comments. --During the fourth quarter of 2006, LMDC amended Partial Action Plan 4 to reduce the amount allocated for this project to the amount already expended to date. In its Final Action Plan, LMDC allocated up to $31,000,000 to address a range of transportation initiatives that will benefit commuters, businesses, residents and pedestrians in Lower Manhattan. Projects expected to be funded include those that improve mobility and improve connectivity between Lower Manhattan, the rest of New York City and the region."],[64753,"LMDC is working on a subrecipent agreement with the City of New York."],[65348,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PROGRAM-- Funds provided for capital activities will support new construction of buildings, renovations, expansions into adjoining spaces, equipment purchases, and site acquisitions. LMDC continues to work with selected grant recipients to develop project scopes and to ensure compliance with all necessary and related federal laws and regulations, including compliance with HUD&apos;s environmental review procedures and required public notices. In November 2006, HUD approved an amended PAP and reduced the Cultural Enhancement Fund by $7,000,000 to $28,000,000. These funds were reallocated to the Final Action Plan for cultural uses. By the end of the fourth quarter, all 31 capital projects had gone to the board for approval. Six contracts have been signed including Art in General, Battery Dance Company, Home for Contemporary Theater dba HERE Art Center, Lower Manhattan Cultural Council, New Dance Alliance, , and New Museum of Contemporary Art. New Dance Alliance completed LMDC-funded activities, and Lower Manhattan Cultural Council successfully purchased new office space in Lower Manhattan."],[65389,"Funds provided will support educational programs, exhibitions, and developing promotional materials and initiatives. LMDC continues to work with selected grant recipients to further develop project scopes for the programs and events. In November 2006, HUD approved an amended PAP and reduced the Cultural Enhancement Fund by $7,000,000 to $28,000,000. These funds were reallocated to the Final Action Plan for cultural uses. As of December 31, 2006, all projects in this category had received final board approval. Seven contracts were signed and the associated activities successfully launched. New York Classical Theater, Battery Park City Parks Conservancy, and Skyscraper Museum were reimbursed for activities executed during Quarter 3. CultureNOW successfully redesigned, printed and distributed 100,000 DowntownNOW 5th edition maps and began work to redesign its website, to be launched in the Spring. Culture Project hosted its mid-October Impact Festival, including the LMDC funded dance performances by Rennie Harris and Liz Lerman. New York Scandia Symphony hosted the first of three LMDC-funded concerts at Trinity Church, with an audience of over 200 people."],[65418,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PROGRAM-- Funds for planning projects will support cultural organizations seeking new homes in Lower Manhattan, as well as several organizations that are in the early stages of a major expansion or early renovation. The funds will be used to assist these organizations with site search, project management, business plan development, and pre-design activities. LMDC continues to work with grant recipients to develop project planning scopes. In November 2006, HUD approved an amended PAP and reduced the Cultural Enhancement Fund by $7,000,000 to $28,000,000. These funds were reallocated to the Final Action Plan for cultural uses. As of December 31, 2006, all 13 planning grants had received final board approval and are in the contract development phase, with 6 fully executed."],[66785,"CHINATOWN LOCAL DEVELOPMENT CORPORATION (LDC)--Funding for this program will be used to spearhead community improvements in Chinatown and engage in a combination of short-term projects and long-term planning. CHINATOWN CLEEN STREETS PROGRAM--The Chinatown Partnership LDC, in conjunction with the New York City Department of Small Business Services (SBS), will implement and oversee a comprehensive, multi-year Clean Streets program. A maintenance subcontractor was selected and Clean Street workers are being deployed, power washing and daily sweeping and tidy work is executed everyday. This quarter two events were held in Chinatown; the Lunar Stages and the 5th Annual Taste of Chinatown. Clean Street workers helped set up, maintain the area during these events, and clean up after the events. A community forum was held on November 15, 2006 in which the CPLDC was able to obtain important feedback on the Clean Streets Program. Participants agreed that Chinatown has been much cleaner since the inception of the Clean Streets program. It is estimated that 500-600 bags of garbage are collected daily because of the Clean Streets Program . Painting of street furniture took place this quarter.; 147 fire hydrants, 102 mailboxes, 41 traffic boxes, 621 lampposts were painted. In Preparation for the winter season snow removal equipment has begun to be purchased CHINATOWN SHORT-TERM PROJECTS AND LONG-TERM PLANNING--In addition to the Clean Streets program, the Chinatown Partnership LDC will spearhead many community enhancement projects and initiatives. LMDC and the LDC are working on the scope of the project and expect to finalize a subrecipient agreement in the coming quarter."],[66843,"The planning phase of the project was completed in the quarter ending September 30, 2006. After the subrecipient agreement was finalized on September 12th; HPD announced the creation of the new Chinatown/Lower East Side Acquisition Program and issued an RFQ soliciting non-profit organizations to participate in the program. Three entities have been pre-qualified for participation in the Chinatown Lower East Side Acquisition Program: Asian Americans for Equality (AAFE), Cooper Square Mutual Housing Association in partnership with the Cooper Square Committee, and Settlement Housing Fund Inc. in partnership with Two Bridges Neighborhood Council. To date, HPD has approved one funding request: AAFE has been approved to receive $3.6 million in program funding for the acquisition of 36 units in two buildings on Henry Street. The funds are scheduled to be closed on this month."],[66348,"COLUMBUS PARK PAVILION -- In the fourth quarter of 2006, electrical work is nearing completion with the exception of new light fixtures, globes and replacement exterior fixtures that are awaiting delivery. All other construction work is complete, with the exception of gas connection. The Pavilion reconstruction is anticipated for completion during early winter 2007."],[77224,"In November 2006 HUD approved an amendment to the PAP reducing the Drawing Center¿s allocation to $2,000,000, and reallocating $8,000,000 to the Final Action Plan for cultural and community uses, with the Drawing Center preserved as an eligible recipient. A contract for $2,000,000 in progress between the LMDC and The Drawing Center."],[102446,"In December 2006, LMDC signed a subrecipieint agreement with the City of New York."],[102525,"In December 2006, LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the New York City Department of Education."],[103484,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT ACCESS--In the fourth quarter of 2006 the LMDC worked with a project team consisting of the New York City Departments of Parks and Recreation, City Planning, and Transportation, New York City Hall, environmental consultant AKRF, and environmental legal counsel Carter Ledyard & Milburne, LLP. During 4Q06, the scope of the environmental review was expanded to encompass associated street reconstruction in a related project by the Department of Transportation. The project was also divided into two sections; one requiring a more substantial environmental review due to the potential for archaeological disturbances, and the others requiring less review and approval. The Department of Parks and Recreation procured two designs and began design work of the improvements."],[103824,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT ESPLANADE AND PIERS--During the fourth quarter of 2006 the LMDC worked with a project team consisting of The New York City Departments of City Planning, and Transportation, the New York City Economic Development Corporation (EDC), New York City Hall, environmental consultant AKRF, and environmental legal counsel Carter Ledyard & Milburne, LLP. Working in consultation with appropriate city, state and federal agencies, the project team finalized a Draft Environmental Impact Statement (DEIS). The DEIS was not distributed during 4Q06 in order to coordinate with the Department of City Planning¿s schedule for associated ULURP actions. During this time EDC procured an economic impact consultant to review benefits of project improvements, and procured a design to advance designs for improvements."],[104120,"EAST SIDE K-8 SCHOOL-- LMDC completed an environmental assessment and is working with the School Construction Authority to finalize necessary agreements, in order to provide funds for this project. In the 4th quarter of 2006, HUD approved a Final Action Plan that included an additional $3,000,000 for this school."],[104328,"During the fourth quarter of 2006 the LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the New York City Department of Small Business Services on behalf of the New York City Economic Development Corporation in order to improve business signage and building facades through assistance grants and the development of design guidelines. During 4Q06 LMDC met with EDC¿s project team of consultants and associated business groups to develop design guidelines for retail improvements."],[104370,"During the fourth quarter of 2006 the LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the Department of Parks for improvements to open space along the Fulton Street Corridor. During 4Q06 LMDC facilitated the appraisal of under-utilized open space in order to improve an existing city park."],[104412,"During the fourth quarter of 2006 the LMDC signed two subrecipient agreements; one is with the New York City Department of Small Business Services on behalf of the New York City Economic Development Corporation in order to improve business signage and building facades through assistance grants and the development of design guidelines; the second agreement is with the Department of Parks and Recreation for improvements to open space along the Fulton Street Corridor. During 4Q06 LMDC facilitated the appraisal of under-utilized open space in order to improve an existing city park, and met with EDC¿s project team of consultants and associated business groups to develop design guidelines for retail improvements."],[104450,"During the 4th quarter of 2006, HUD approved an amendment to PAP 6 to eliminate the allocation of $8,000,000 for the Fulton Corridor Residential Rehabilitation and Conversion Program. The NYC Department of Housing Preservation and Development does not intend to implement this program as originally planned. These funds have been reallocated to affordable housing initiatives in LMDC&apos;s Final Action Plan."],[104460,"During the fourth quarter of 2006 the LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the New York City Department of Small Business Services on behalf of the New York City Economic Development Corporation in order to improve business signage and building facades through assistance grants and the development of design guidelines. During 4Q06 LMDC met with EDC¿s project team of consultants and associated business groups to develop design guidelines for retail improvements."],[104684,"FITERMAN HALL RECONSTRUCTION-- LMDC is working with the City University of New York to finalize necessary agreements in order to provide funds for this project."],[110036,"HISTORY AND HERITAGE IN DOWNTOWN NYC/Museums of Lower Manhattan (MOLM)-- In the fourth quarter of 2006 LMDC continued its ambitious marketing of the Museums of Lower Manhattan. In this quarter, the final versions of the Museums of Lower Manhattan films were uploaded on to the collective¿s website for visitors to view. CTM distributed 106,400 Museum of Lower Manhattan map/brochures. Bandujo, Donker and Brothers continued advertisement placements in various tourist publications, local newspapers, magazines and on internet websites. 637 Museums of Lower Manhattan radio spots aired on local radio during the quarter. The campaign was successful placing several stories on specific museums and exhibitions, including local television stories about events at the New York City Police Museum and National Museum of the American Indian. The Kids Passport Program continued during the fourth quarter, where kids who visit any three of ten participating museums can get their passport stamped and receive a Museums of Lower Manhattan t-shirt. The partnership with Duane Reade drugstores promoting the Museums and offering discounts to their customers. The campaign also ran a special offer with NYC Transit Buses offering 2 for 1 admission for any visitor with a Metro Card. Attendance figures for 2006 for the collective will be reported on in the next quarter."],[111898,"REHABILITATION/RECONSTRUCTION OF A PUBLIC IMPROVEMENT --- --HUDSON RIVER PARK SEGMENT 3--On February 28, 2006, HUD approved Partial Action Plan 10, which allocated up to $70 million for the completion of Hudson River Park Segment 3 in Tribeca. Within this area would be two public recreational piers (Piers 25 and 26), an ecological pier, and an adjacent upland park. HRPT¿s contractor has completed demolition of Piers 25 and 26."],[112274,"In December 2006, LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development"],[120400,"LMDC&apos;s commitment to this project has been fulfilled and during the fourth quarter the agency amended PAP 1 to reduce funding by $35,000 to cover actual program expenditures."],[123914,"The Job Creation and Retention Program (JCRP) provides discretionary incentives to companies making commitments to attract, create and/or retain at least 200 jobs in Lower Manhattan. The program will run though 2007. JCRP is managed by ESD in cooperation with NYC EDC in accordance with a Final Action Plan and an Amended Action Plan accepted and approved by HUD. JCRP assistance is in the form of job-based grants for companies making a minimum 7 year commitment to Lower Manhattan. The amount of assistance is based on several criteria including the number of jobs, economic impact, the creation of new employment in Lower Manhattan and the degree of risk of employment leaving Lower Manhattan. As of December 31, 2006, ESD has offered incentive grants to 126 companies. Seventy-seven companies have accepted grant offers worth $293.5 million to date. The accepted grant amount is $1,981,400 less than the previous quarter due to three companies not achieving their job growth grants. An additional company (LaBranche) terminated its accepted grant offer. Of those accepted offers, 73 active projects have received ESD¿s Board approval, representing 73,612 created and retained jobs in Lower Manhattan and an additional 15,742 jobs retained elsewhere in New York City. The value of the ESD Board approved grants is $289,246,097. This amount is $994,400 less than the previous quarter due to three companies not achieving their job growth grants. ESD has disbursed all of its original $170 million allocation for the program and has been drawing on LMDC¿s $150 million sub-allocation since August 2003. However, during the quarter ending September 30, 2006 ESD recaptured funds from Georgeson Shareholder who originally received its grant from ESD¿s allocation and had returned $48,222 to ESD as..."],[124126,"LMDC continues to work with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development to plan and develop the scope of the rehabilitation for the development. It is an anticipated that this scope planning phase will be completed in the following quarter and a subrecipient agreement finalized in the near term."],[125467,"LOWER MANHATTAN COMMUNICATIONS OUTREACH CAMPAIGN ---The campaign¿s three components include: the creation of informational kiosks at strategic locations downtown that will double as visitor centers; the development of branded marketing materials; and the distribution of LMDC printed materials and electronic updates. More than half of the campaign¿s funds have been allocated for the creation, design, construction, and maintenance of the two informational kiosks; the Downtown Rebuilding Information Kiosk which is located at the new World Trade Center Path Station and the Chinatown Kiosk which is located between Canal, Baxter, and Walker Streets. The campaign also allocated funds for staffing the WTC Path Station kiosk for two years. In 2006, the WTC Kiosk had 604,145 visitors and the Chinatown Kiosk served 198,180 visitors. Both kiosks are stocked with current information about Lower Manhattan and the rebuilding efforts. The campaign provided for the printing of marketing materials, including five Renew Downtown newsletters of which hundreds-of-thousands were printed and distributed to various locations all over Lower Manhattan. The LMDC developed and printed educational brochures on the [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]130 Liberty Street[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] deconstruction project. These brochures were distributed throughout the Lower Manhattan community. A total of 12 Palm Cards were developed, printed, and circulated at special events, the WTC kiosk, and various other locations and functions. The Palm Cards included: Greenmarket 2003, River to River 2003, the opening of Vesey Street Bridge, the opening of the PATH Station, the History & Heritage Campaign, Downtown for the Holidays, Tribeca Film Festival 2004, Chinatown (3 printings), Parks (two printings), the Greenmarket 2004, the..."],[126789,"LOWER MANHATTAN FERRY SERVICE PROGRAM-- In the quarter ending December 31, 2006, LMDC, Port Authority, New York State Department of Transportation, City of Yonkers, and New York Water Taxi continued planning for the new ferry service that is expected to start in the Spring of 2007."],[126835,"In the quarter ending December 31, 2006: 1. NYC DOT merged two computerized traffic models into one traffic model and also incorporated the Battery Park City area into the model. (At the end of the first phase of this project, NYC DOT had two separate traffic models that covered the east and western portions of Lower Manhattan. These models analyze the traffic effects of a lane closure, street closure or other type of vehicle restriction) --2. NYC DOT held extensive coordination efforts with the Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center (LMCCC). NYC DOT is looking to develop an interface between its traffic model and LMCCC¿s scheduling /GIS model. This will help LMCCC see impacts of specific street closures and how it affects traffic network. --3. NYC DOT began a bus study that will be completed in early 2007. The city will determine the existing supply and demand of bus parking in Lower Manhattan. The City will then determine the volumes and staging locations of buses entering Lower Manhattan. --4. NYC DOT began surveying and quantitatively analyzing the use and mis-use of ¿placard parking¿ in Lower Manhattan, in order to assess the impact of these patterns on the general user. The observational and quantitative results of the analysis may be used by DOT for the purpose of formulating a broad spectrum of policies with respect to the management of parking in Lower Manhattan."],[125159,"The Tribute in Light of September 11, 2006 was successfully and momentously displayed from the Battery Park Tunnel Garage, where it will remain housed through 2008. In November 2006 HUD approved an Amendment to Partial Action Plan 8 that reduced LMDC¿s allocation by $500,000 to $3,000,000. This amount reflects savings from lower than expected bids for some items. During this quarter, LMDC reconciled invoicing for the 2006 display."],[129386,"MASARYK TOWER REHABILITATION-- LMDC continues to work with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development to plan and develop the scope of the rehabilitation for the development. It is anticipated that this scope planning phase will be completed in the following quarter and a subrecipient agreement finalized in the near term."],[134415,"NYSE -- During the fourth quarter of 2006, design work began for the second phase of the project, studying improvements to security devices, and improvements to the streetscape. The project team began preparation, in conjunction with local stakeholders, to meet with the State Historic Preservation Commission."],[135868,"During the past quarter, invoices totaling $359,358 were paid for the East River Waterfront Planning Study and other planning projects. These invoices reflect work dating back to 2005. The East River Waterfront Planning Study has been completed and led to a second phase of work for the East River Esplanade/Piers and East River Access (see reports ERA-0240 and EREP-0240)."],[135813,"LMDC PLANNING - ECONOMIC ANALYSIS ¿ In the 4th quarter ending December 31, 2006, LMDC retained the Louis Berger Group to measure the economic benefits of LMDC¿s Cultural Enhancement Fund (CEF) Program. The Louis Berger Group will assist LMDC in the development of performance measures as well as a database for calculating and tracking these measures."],[135919,"LMDC PLANNING--ENVIRONMENTAL --- LMDC sought the advice and counsel of law firms and environmental consulting firms on retainer on environmental and land use requirements and matters relating to LMDC¿s planning and development initiatives in Lower Manhattan, including (1) the World Trade Center Memorial and Redevelopment Plan (WTC Plan), (2) the East River Waterfront Esplanade and Piers Project, (3) cultural enhancement and community improvement projects, and (4) public information, outreach, and participation efforts relating to the foregoing activities. With the assistance of the firms, LMDC reviewed technical issues relating to the April 2004 Programmatic Agreement pursuant to Section 106 of the National Historic Preservation Act; refinements to the WTC memorial and redevelopment (including design changes to the WTC Memorial recommended by Frank J. Sciame); and the deconstruction of [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]130 Liberty Street[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]. During the quarter LMDC completed a Draft Environmental Impact Statement for the East River Waterfront Esplanade and Piers Project and held a meeting with the Section 106 Consulting Parties regarding the status of the Memorial and Museum components of the WTC Plan and the Plan¿s impact on the Vesey Street Stair Remnants."],[135958,"LMDC PLANNING ¿ OFF-SITE INITIATIVES-- In the quarter ending December 31, 2006, Parsons Brinkerhoff concluded their feasibility study for the redesign of Chatham Square that was initially recommended in the Chinatown Access and Circulation Study. Public agency coordination on plan components continued for improvements to the Greenwich Street South Area. LMDC also worked with the NYC Department and Design and Construction regarding improvements to Louise Nevelson Plaza, which is expected to be a component of the street reconstruction of Liberty Street, Maiden Lane, and Pearl Street."],[135993,"All planning for West Street has been completed. In 2006, the New York State Department of Transportation opened the West Street Promenade South, a tree-lined pedestrian walkway extending along both sides of Route 9A (West Street) from West Thames Street to Battery Place in Lower Manhattan. Roadway and sidewalk improvements provide increased pedestrian space by adding recreational areas and lush greenery to the heavily trafficked roadway and complete a substantial portion of the Hudson River Park. Promenade South is the first phase of a two-phase State Department of Transportation (NYSDOT) project designed to repair damage sustained during the terrorist attacks on the World Trade Center on September 11, 2001 and provide an appropriately dignified and aesthetically graceful setting for the World Trade Center Memorial planned nearby. When both phases are complete, the full promenade project will improve pedestrian and vehicular circulation at Route 9A between Chambers Street and Battery Place in Lower Manhattan. The project will strengthen the connection among the future World Trade Center Memorial on the east side of Route 9A, the World Financial Center and Battery Park City on the west side of the roadway, and historic Battery Park, embarkation point for the Statue of Liberty and Ellis Island at the southern tip of Manhattan."],[136037,"WORLD TRADE CENTER PLANNING -- During the quarter ending December 31, 2006, LMDC¿s Board of Directors adopted proposed amendments to the General Project Plan that reflect and incorporate Frank Sciame¿s recommendations with respect to the Memorial and museum, as well as other proposed amendments to the plan. During the quarter, LMDC also consulted with parties regarding visitor access to historic resources in the WTC Memorial/Museum as well as impacts to the Vesey Street Stair Remnants. --Studio Daniel Libeskind worked extensively with LMDC, the Port Authority, the City of New York, and the World Trade Center leaseholders on further fine-tuning and revising the Commercial Design Guidelines for the World Trade Center site. These guidelines are intended to set out basic urban design principles for the overall site redevelopment and allow for the vision of the WTC plan to be fulfilled over time. The design guidelines include a chapter on sustainable design and green construction prepared by LMDC, the Port Authority, and Silverstein Properties. --In addition, LMDC continued working jointly with the Port Authority on planning for the Southern Site, including Liberty Park and St. Nicholas Church, in conjunction with the Vehicle Security Center and all other infrastructure elements on and under this site."],[136191,"As of December 30, 2006, the following parks are complete: Drumgoole, Tribeca Park, Battery Bosque, Bowling Green, East River Park, Al Smith Playground, Brooklyn Bridge Plaza, Old Slip, Columbus Park Landscape and Washington Market Park, Coenties Slip, and Sara D. Roosevelt. Wall Street park is substantially complete and a fountain will be installed in spring 2006. --The second phase of parks improvements is currently undergoing environmental review. These projects include: Washington Market Park Comfort Station, Collect Pond Park, Sara D. Roosevelt Phase 2, Allen and Pike Street Malls, James Madison Park and Canal Varick Laight Park."],[171002,"SMALL FIRM ATTRACTION AND RETENTION GRANT (SFARG) -- To date, we have made over 3,800 grants to 2,484 businesses for nearly $126 million."],[171228,"On November 18, 2005, HUD approved Partial Action Plan 6, which allocated funds for the planning and design, of a mixed income residential rental building at [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in Tribeca. The Subrecipient Agreement was executed on December 5 2006. The project broke ground on December of 2005, and the mixed-income rental component was 33.6% complete based on a site observation report dated December 7, 2006."],[172397,"SOUTHERN SITE--The World Trade Center Memorial and Redevelopment Plan (WTC Plan) includes several parcels of land just south of the World Trade Center Site, referred to collectively as the ¿Southern Site,¿ that will be used to construct infrastructure and other components of the WTC Plan. LMDC acquired the largest parcel at [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in August 2004, [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in February 2006, and the following parcels constitute the remainder: (1) privately-owned [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS]; and (2) several public New York City streets-Washington Street between Cedar and Liberty Streets and subsurface portions of Cedar and Liberty Streets. LMDC has been working with the The City of New York and The Port Authority of New York and New Jersey on the acquisition and configuration of the street parcels as well as the review and development of plans for the entire Southern Site, including the redesign and relocation of St. Nicholas Church."],[172949,"In December 2006, LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the New York City Department of Small Business Services."],[173173,"TRIBUTE VISITORS CENTER--The Tribute Center opened adjacent to the World Trade Center site in September 2006. It offers an interactive learning experience that focuses on providing visitor orientation and factual information about the terrorist attacks of February 26, 1993 and September 11, 2001. The Tribute Center focuses on telling the human stories of those affected through its exhibits, programs and docent-led tours of the site. Tribute is expected to be open for a minimum of 5 years, effectively bridging the gap between the present day and the completion of the future WTC Memorial, Reflecting Absence.-- The docent tour program officially began on November 19, 2005, with one tour per weekday and two tours per weekend day. The program increased to two weekday tours and four weekend tours in February 2006. To date, over 100 docents from the 9/11 community (victims&apos; families, survivors, rescue workers, residents, and others) have been trained to provide tours. The Center houses 4,000 square feet of gallery space on the first floor and 2,000 square feet of gallery and program space on the lower level, including a space for community gatherings. Galleries include: ¿World Trade Center Community Remembered,¿ celebrating the World Trade Center before September 11th; ¿Passage Through Time,¿ telling the personal stories of those who experienced September 11th first hand; ¿Aftermath and Recovery,¿ where a moving film shows the mammoth task and tremendous will of the rescue and recovery workers; ¿Tribute,¿ showing images and lovingly selected objects given by families to share their loved ones with the world; and ¿Voices of Promise,¿ where the vast international response to September 11th is documented."],[173329,"PERFORMING ARTS CENTER DESIGN---During quarter ending December 31, 2006 the LMDC met regularly with the Port Authority (PA) to determine how best to integrate the Performing Arts Center (PAC) in a proposed ¿Temporary North Egress¿ for the existing PATH station on the WTC Site. The temporary station would temporarily occupy the parcel of land allocated for the PAC building. The LMDC and the PA considered coordination efforts to the below ground space, and impacts of the temporary PATH station to the PAC¿s schedule and budget. During the fourth quarter of 2006 it was also decided that a second vehicular access ramp (the ¿helix¿) should be located on the site to be occupied by the PAC. This required further analysis of how the helix could be integrated in to the PAC building, and the associated impacts to program space, capital costs, and project schedule. LMDC approved a subrecipient agreement for $55,000,000 with New York City to continue design of the PAC. Design work will continue into the first quarter of 2007 with the City, LMDC and the PA."],[173383,"WTC MEMORIAL AND MEMORIAL MUSEUM -- LMDC, in partnership with the World Trade Center Memorial Foundation (WTCMF), will provide initial funding for the construction documentation and construction of the Memorial, and will plan for and oversee the design development and construction documentation for the Memorial Museum, and possibly other elements of the WTC Plan. In the fourth quarter of 2006, HUD approved a Final Action Plan that provides $45,000,000 for a reserve fund for the Memorial/Museum. Funds will be available to address unforeseen events or unavoidable costs related to the construction of the Memorial/Museum, including possible excess infrastructure costs. Progress this quarter includes: Construction Manager Bovis awarded work on footings and foundation and started actual work in the 3rd quarter of 2006. On March 30th this year, the Memorial Foundation will receive 100% Memorial Core and Shell Construction Documents, and this summer the Memorial Foundation will receive 100% Museum Fit-out Construction Documents. The WTC Memorial Foundation¿s museum team continued its work on the institutional development of the combined Visitor Orientation and Education Center and the Memorial Museum, including the space program and the development of exhibition design scope and curatorial resources. As of November 30, 2006, the Contract for the Plaza trees has been awarded to Environmental Tree and Design Inc. The tree selection and tagging is currently in progress and will continue into the next quarter. LMDC continues to have meetings with the Section 106 consulting parties and the Families Advisory Council. The design team continued to study various structural engineering options to support and expose the existing West Slurry Wall and Vesey Street Stair. Please see W-MUS..."],[173434,"WTC MEMORIAL AND MEMORIAL MUSEUM -- LMDC, in partnership with the World Trade Center Memorial Foundation (WTCMF), will provide initial funding for the construction documentation and construction of the Memorial, and will plan for and oversee the design development and construction documentation for the Memorial Museum, and possibly other elements of the WTC Plan. In the fourth quarter of 2006, HUD approved a Final Action Plan that provides $45,000,000 for a reserve fund for the Memorial/Museum. Funds will be available to address unforeseen events or unavoidable costs related to the construction of the Memorial/Museum, including possible excess infrastructure costs. Progress this quarter includes: Construction Manager Bovis awarded work on footings and foundation and started actual work in the 3rd quarter of 2006. On March 30th this year, the Memorial Foundation will receive 100% Memorial Core and Shell Construction Documents, and this summer the Memorial Foundation will receive 100% Museum Fit-out Construction Documents. The WTC Memorial Foundation¿s museum team continued its work on the institutional development of the combined Visitor Orientation and Education Center and the Memorial Museum, including the space program and the development of exhibition design scope and curatorial resources. As of November 30, 2006, the Contract for the Plaza trees has been awarded to Environmental Tree and Design Inc. The tree selection and tagging is currently in progress and will continue into the next quarter. LMDC continues to have meetings with the Section 106 consulting parties and the Families Advisory Council. The design team continued to study various structural engineering options to support and expose the existing West Slurry Wall and Vesey Street Stair. Please see W-MUS..."],[173518,"WEST STREET PEDESTRIAN CONNECTIONS¿All work has been completed. Construction of the Vesey Bridge was completed in stages, with the opening in time for the temporary PATH station in November 2003. This was followed by the completion of the Bridge Extension in June 2004, and final delivery of the elevators in November 2004"],[64615,"CATEGORY ONE--EMERGENCY AND TEMPORARY SERVICE RESPONSE--The Utility Restoration and Infrastructure Rebuilding Program¿s Partial Action Plan received the United States Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) approval on September 15, 2003. This Plan details the proposed expenditure of $750 million to restore, rebuild, and revitalize utility infrastructure in Lower Manhattan. Empire State Development Corporation (ESDC), in consultation with the Lower Manhattan Development Corporation and in cooperation with the New York City Economic Development Corporation, is administering the Plan. The plan details include funding the following activities: (1) Category One- Emergency and Temporary Service Response; (2) Category Two- Permanent Restoration and Infrastructure Improvements; (3) Category Three- Service Interference; (4) Category Four- Construction of Carrier Neutral Lateral Conduits; Category Five Construction of Redundant Fiber Connections; (6)Category Six- Mandated Infrastructure Improvements. In September 2006, LMDC issued proposed changes to Partial Action Plan S-2, and during the fourth quarter of 2006, LMDC continued to review comments. As of December 31, 2006, field audit work continued for AT&T Corporation. Field audit work has been concluded for Verizon Communications, Inc. and has been completed for Con Edison and AT&T Wireless. Requests for Category One totaled $403,674,125. Disbursements to date total $122,759,473, leaving a balance of $122,759,473 from the $250 million allocated for Category One--The following represents the number of Lower Manhattan businesses and residential customers served by the utilities receiving Category One reimbursements: (1) Number of Low/Mod income persons - 29,799 (2) Number of Low Income persons - 21,072 (3) Number of..."],[64645,"CATEGORY TWO--PERMANENT RESTORATION AND INFRASTRUCTURE IMPROVEMENTS--- The Utility Restoration and Infrastructure Rebuilding Program¿s Partial Action Plan received the United States Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) approval on September 15, 2003. This Plan details the proposed expenditure of $750 million to restore, rebuild, and revitalize utility infrastructure of Lower Manhattan. Empire State Development Corporation (ESDC) in consultation with the Lower Manhattan Development Corporation (LMDC) and with the cooperation of the New York City Economic Development Corporation (EDC) is administering the Plan. Plan details include funding the following activities: (1) Category One - Emergency and Temporary Service Response; (2) Category Two - Permanent Restoration and Infrastructure Improvements; (3) Category Three - Service Interference; (4) Category Four - Construction of Carrier Neutral Lateral Conduits; (5) Category Five - Construction of Redundant Fiber Connections; (6) Category Six- Mandated Infrastructure Improvements --- As outlined in Partial Action Plan S-2, Category Two pertains to permanent restoration and infrastructure improvements. As of April 27, 2006 Category Two has been divided into two separate sub-categories for funding, 1) Permanent Restoration and Infrastructure Improvements and 2) Southern Site. Category Two Southern Site qualifying expenditures are eligible for reimbursement according to the payment schedule as outlined in the Category Two Southern Site Guidelines. Category Two requests total $383,614,301 (Permanent Restoration $383,614,301 + Southern Site 37,428,264) Disbursements to date $38, 263,671, (Permanent Restoration $30,000,000 + 8,263,671) leaving a balance of $291,736,929 of $330 million allocation. The application sub..."],[136105,"PLANNING AND ADMINSTRATION--¿- UTILITIES RESTORATION AND INFRASTRUCTURE REBUILDING (URIR) PROGRAM.-- To date, program administration funds have been used for activities related to the implementation of Categories One and Two of the URIR Program. Such activities include general management, oversight, and coordination. These activities also include program publication, review of proposed infrastructure investments, environmental review clearance, financial evaluation and processing of funding reimbursement requests, and auditing of funds that have been reimbursed for Category One and Category Two.--Program administration funds have also been used to fund feasibility studies. -- AKRF Inc and The Louis Berger Group continue to provide environmental consulting services. AKRF is currently working on AT&T¿s Category Two application. The Louis Berger group has been assigned the Verizon Category Two application and work is ongoing. --KPMG is providing IT consulting services including testing and auditing the integrity and security of the electronic systems of the applicants. ¿ MOSAICS- LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the NYC Department of Transportation (DOT) in the third quarter of 2006. The DOT will conduct an initial phase of planning and design for certain upgrades to the MOSAICS system. This will include, but not be limited to, a review and analysis of current DOT and MOSAICS processes and a technical feasibility analysis and review of MOSAICS system architecture. In September 2006, LMDC issued proposed changes to Partial Action Plan S-2, and during the fourth quarter of 2006, LMDC continued to review comments."],[102373,"HUD approved the Bridge Loan Program May 9, 2006, as a part of the State&iquest;s Initial Action Plan. On February 10, 2006, a Cooperative Endeavor was entered into between the Louisiana Public Facilities Authority (LPFA), Louisiana Economic Development (LED), and the Division of Administration (DOA), which obligated $30,000,000 in State CDBG funds to be utilized in the Extended Bridge Loan program. That agreement was amended, on June 20, 2006, to include $10,000,000 to cover the cost of the Emergency Bridge Loan program that was originally funded by LED, as well as $50,000,000 to be utilized in the Enhanced Bridge Loan Program. Prior to the Enhanced Loan program being implemented, a change in geographical location of beneficiaries was requested through the submittal of a substantial amendment that was approved by HUD on September 15, 2006. This amendment revised the allocation language from a 50% allocation to the thirteen most impacted parishes, to a minimum of 50% will be allocated to the thirteen most impacted parishes, and up to 50% allocated to the other twenty-four impacted parishes. This change transpired due to the demand for loan guarantees amounting to $8,820,765 in the most impacted areas. Also, during the previous quarter, the Extended BL made a total of 325 loan guarantees totaling $28,696,080 in a 37 parish area. An estimated 3,872 jobs were reported as being created or retained as a result of the three Bridge Loan Programs. The Enhanced BL lender proposal deadline was October 30, 2006. Once an inter agency agreement is signed with Louisiana Office of Economic Development (LED), the selected lenders will begin actively implementing the program."],[102508,"The Tourism and Marketing Program was approved by HUD on September 15, 2006. A draft Cooperative Endeavor Agreement was developed by the Louisiana Department of Recreation and Tourism (CRT), following which the Department of Culture, Recreation and Tourism (DCR&T) and the OCD-DRU by entered into an interagency agreement to implement the Tourism and Marketing program. The program will award $28,500,000 to 17 tourism agencies throughout the affected area. Approximately 75% of all sub receipts agreements are in the continued process of contractual reviews with the projected completion of those reviews to occur in the near future."],[104296,"The Small Firm Technical Assistance Program was approved by HUD on September 15, 2006. Draft guidelines and Requests for Proposals (RFPs) were sent to Technical Assistance providers during the month of October. Discussions were held with the Louisiana Department of Economic Development (LED) and the Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) to determine performance measures and the review process for submittal of RFPs. In November a Review Board was initiated to review the proposals requested, the following month the intermediaries were determined and contacted and training detailing how to monitor sub recipients was provided by LED and other agencies."],[107404,"Prior to the implementation of the program the Office of Community Development (OCD), through the assistance of the Louisiana Recovery Authority and housing consultants began developing the program design of The Road Home program (TRH). A total of $1 million was allocated to this activity. In order to finalize major aspects of the program design, the State required more information. The OCD opened The Road Home Registry on March 8, 2006 to collect such data. Of particular interest at this point was the need to collect contact information on displaced homeowners for future outreach efforts when TRH Program was fully implemented. The Registry was designed to allow both telephone and online registration. The information requested included the homeowner&iquest;s name, current address and the location of the affected home, phone numbers, mortgage information, the status of any insurance settlements and any FEMA or U.S. Small Business Association (SBA) applications or assistance. A total of $2 million was allocated to support the registry. Through a rigorous selection process, ICF International was selected on June 2, 2006 to administer TRH Homeowner Assistance Program on behalf of the Office of Community Development. The bulk of the expenditures, approximately $10 million, related to The Road Home during this time went to fund the start up of the program. Part of this start up involved the establishment of a pilot program to test out the program design and procedures. On July 12, 2006, The Road Home program began a pilot to schedule consultation appointments with a small sample of homeowners who had pre-registered with The Road Home Housing Registry. During these appointments, housing professionals verified necessary documentation and assisted homeowners with any question..."],[107418,"Prior to the implementation of the program OCD, through the assistance of the Louisiana Recovery Authority and housing consultants, began developing the program design of The Road Home program. A total of $1 million was allocated to this activity. In order to finalize major aspects of the program design, the State required more information. The OCD opened The Road Home Registry on March 8, 2006 to collect such data. Of particular interest at this point was the need to collect contact information on displaced homeowners for future outreach efforts when The Road Home Program was fully implemented. The Registry was designed to allow both telephone and online registration. The information requested included the homeowner&iquest;s name, current address and the location of the affected home, phone numbers, mortgage information, the status of any insurance settlements and any FEMA or U.S. Small Business Association (SBA) applications or assistance. A total of $2 million was allocated to support the registry. Through a rigorous selection process, ICF International was selected on June 2, 2006 to administer The Road Home Homeowner Assistance Program on behalf of the Office of Community Development. The bulk of the expenditures, approximately $10 million, related to The Road Home during this time went to fund the start up of the program. Part of this start up involved the establishment of a pilot program to test the program design and procedures. On July 12, 2006, The Road Home program began a pilot to schedule consultation appointments with a small sample of homeowners who had pre-registered with The Road Home Housing Registry. During these appointments, housing professionals verified necessary documentation and assisted homeowners with any questions they may have had regardi..."],[112379,"Prior to the disastrous 2005 hurricane season, a large segment of low income working families resided in one- to four-unit rental buildings, including single-family homes, duplexes, three- and four-unit dwellings that were owned and operated by small-scale landlords. In the wake of the storms, it became clear that an unprecedented number (approximately 82,000) of multi-family rental properties had been destroyed or severely damaged. Replacement of the damaged or destroyed rental housing in the most heavily damaged hurricane areas is vital to the return of a strong workforce, and is a keystone of Louisiana&apos;s economic recovery. Small Rental Property Program eligibility will be limited to the 13 most affected parishes: Acadia, Calcasieu, Cameron, Iberia, Jefferson, Orleans, Plaquemines, St. Bernard, St. Tammany, Tangipahoa, Terrebonne, Vermilion and Washington. The Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) and the Office of Community Development (OCD) approved the program on December 18, 2006. The Small Rental Property Program will provide incentives for the repair of an estimated 18,000 rental housing units. The primary purposes of these incentives are to enable repairs to occur and to limit the amount of debt required on the properties, enabling rental property owners to charge affordable rents. In exchange for accepting financial incentives, property owners will be required to accept limitations on rents and incomes of tenants. Assisted units must be affordable to households earning less than 80 percent, 65 percent, or 50 percent of the Area Median Income and the amount of incentives available will correspond to the three tiers of AMI, with the highest amount per unit being available to property owners who agree to offer the lowest rents. The Small Rental Program will..."],[112719,"The State¿s CDBG funds will provide about $440M in gap financing and Project Based Rental Assistance in order to assist 33 projects that will create more than 5,700 new rental units in storm damaged areas. The LIHTC-CDBG Piggyback Program will combine the resources of Gulf Opportunity Zone Tax Credits, CDBG funding, Section 8 project based housing vouchers, and leveraged private investments to generate new or restored rental units. In addition to the 33 projects that received CDBG gap financing/and or Project Based Rental Assistance, the LHFA also awarded GO ZONE Tax Credits to another 10 projects accounting for approximately 1158 additional units. The program will support the development of mixed-income communities and ensure the restoration of rental housing in the most heavily impacted parishes. Eligible applicants are developers applying for Low Income Housing Tax Credits. The primary method of award will be to sponsors who apply for and receive GO Zone credits under the 2007/2008 LIHTC allocation rounds administered by the Louisiana Housing Finance Agency. The CBDG funds will be allocated among three pools identified as Mixed-Income, Additional Affordability, and Public Housing Authority Redevelopments. In most cases, these mixed income developments will contain at least 60% market rate units and at least 20% deeply targeted units ¿ affordable to households earning less than 40% of the Area Median Income. Funding will be available in the form of either project based rental assistance, gap financing, or a combination thereof. The program was available with the rollout of the 2007/2008 GO Zone LIHTC Qualified Allocation Plan on the schedule below. A Program Description has been approved by OCD and LRA. The plan was presented to developers at a series of LHFA Devel..."],[137259,"Through the work of its various committees and task forces, the Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) has developed a series of strategic priorities and recovery plans in the areas of housing, infrastructure and economic development and played the lead role in the development of recovery policy and initiatives in the areas of housing, infrastructure, and economic development. These also overlap into policy and planning in areas of health care, social services, education, and other subjects. The majority of LRA&apos;s work on the development, passage, submission and implementation of CDBG action plans has been tied to policies on housing, infrastructure and economic development -- the bedrocks of recovery. During this most recent quarter, the LRA has focused heavily on economic and workforce development program development and implementation; infrastructure recovery programs and implementation issues, including education infrastructure design considerations; homeowner and rental housing program policy and implementation oversight issues; and community and regional planning efforts related to recovery priorities. Regarding the long-term planning process, the LRA collaborated with the AIA and APA to develop rebuilding principles, upon which the LRA initiated a planning effort called Louisiana Speaks. This is a multifaceted planning initiative designed to address planning at every level. This extends from community planning such as the Unified New Orleans Plan (the LRA has supported UNOP through the recruitment of $3 million in private funding from the Rockefeller Foundation and the commitment of $2 million in CDBG funds for outreach, including communications and input with the New Orleans diaspora) to other parish-wide comprehensive, long-term recovery strategies in collab..."],[170195,"The Disaster Recovery Unit (DRU) of the Office of Community Development (OCD), Division of Administration, was created in the aftermath of the 2005 Hurricanes Katrina and Rita. The purpose of this unit is to administer the CDBG Disaster Recovery funds approved by Congress on December 23, 2005. Personnel were hired from around the United States with experience in all aspects of the CDBG program. Managers and staff were hired in the areas of homeownership housing, multifamily/rental/supportive housing, infrastructure, economic development, policy/reporting, legal, and financial and auditing. Employees of the Office of Community Development, CDBG Program, are available for training new employees. In addition, OCD provided CDBG boot camp training in partnership with the Council of State Community Development Agencies. The Division of Administration&iquest;s resources are available for expertise in budgeting, accounting, reporting, contract review and legal matters and we are in close contact with the Legislative Auditors in our State. At present, there are 37 people hired in the Disaster Recovery Unit, 10 of which were hired during the quarter. Approval was received in September 2006, for an additional 49 positions, currently there 23 vacancies. This does not include the 30 approved LRA positions. The administration budget will be used to fund salaries, related benefits, travel, operating supplies and services, professional services and inter agency transfers. Meetings are being held throughout the impacted parishes for citizen awareness and input of the needs that our funds can address. The state follows the State Procurement Code and all other sub recipients are required to follow Title 24 Part 84 and Part 85. Monitoring plans are being developed for the new disaster r..."],[170316,"Technical Asistance to State."],[2685,"The code inspectors have been hired and inspectors are proceeding with inspections."],[2695,"The city is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[2705,"The city is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[2713,"The city is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[2726,"The city is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[2738,"The county is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[2745,"The county is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[2753,"The code inspectors have been hired and inspectors are proceeding with inspections."],[2759,"The code inspectors have been hired and inspectors are proceeding with inspections."],[2767,"The city is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[2778,"The code inspectors have been hired and inspectors are proceeding with inspections."],[2786,"The code inspectors have been hired and inspectors are proceeding with inspections."],[2802,"The code inspectors have been hired and inspectors are proceeding with inspections."],[2811,"The county is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[2817,"The city is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[2825,"The city is in the process of hiring inspectors at this time."],[60481,"While the federal appropriation for WTC disaster relief allows an allocation of up to 10 percent of the $700 million award for administration and planning, Empire State Development Corporation, in cooperation with the NYC Economic Development Corporation, has allocated $14 million for administration. These activities provide for the coordination and administration of business assistance programs funded through the federal appropriation, centralization of application processing and disbursement of funds, access to assistance information, and records-keeping. Coordination of these activities greatly improves the efficiency of the delivery of disaster assistance, reduces the risk of abuse, and provides the opportunity to link resources to further maximize their benefits."],[20680,"[REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] BUILDING DECONSTRUCTION During the quarter ending March 31, 2007 the following occured: The removal of the cooling tower located on the lower roof was completed. While the Liberty Street sidewalk bridge was completed during the quarter ending December 31, 2006, relocation of office trailers to the top of that bridge was completed. Exterior facade removal of aluminum metal fascias, column covers and window glass continued, and was completed down to floor 35. Pre-abatement work, including setting up of decontamination units and achieving negative air pressure commenced on floors 25, 24, 23, 22, 21, and 20. By the end of this quarter, environmental work on the building was approximately 37% complete. Work commenced on structural removal of mechanical equipment and deconstruction of the building structure began on. By March 31, 2007, deconstruction work on the building was approximately 4% complete. An average of four (4) 100 yard containers of porous materials bagged and to be disposed of as ACM (Asbestos Containing Material) were removed from the site each weekday. Approximately 20% of the scheduled environmental work on the building has been completed. -- PUBLIC OUTREACH AND PARTICIPATION--LMDC continues its efforts to keep area stakeholders and the public at-large informed about the deconstruction of the [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] Building. In the quarter ending March 31, 2007 the following occured: An update was provided to the WTC Redevelopment Committee Community Board 1 Meeting on February 12, 2007. On March 14, 2007, the LMDC sent out an e-update informing 906 subscribers of the commencement of structural deconstruction on the building. On March 28, 2007 a meeting of the [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-..."],[60563,"LMDC GENERAL ADMINISTRATION --- LMDC maintains a staff and office space to carry out its planning and community development activities, including general management, oversight, monitoring, and coordination. In addition, LMDC provides local officials, businesses, and citizens with information about programs. LMDC conducts additional outreach initiatives on a continuous basis to city, state, and federal elected officials, civic and community organizations, and victims families, participates in Community Board meetings, and meets regularly with community groups, civic organizations, and public officials to ensure that the opinions of those who were affected are taken into account. The LMDC website also solicits public feedback on all of its initiatives, including WTC site planning, transportation, Lower Manhattan neighborhoods, the WTC Site Memorial, and Partial Action Plans"],[61921,"LOWER MANHATTAN TRANSPORTATION PLANNING: COMMUTER AND AIRPORT ACCESS ALTERNATIVE ANALYSES-- LMDC&apos;s portion of this activity has been completed."],[64233,"BUSINESS RECOVERY GRANT PROGRAM--The WTC Business Recovery Grant Program, by far the largest of the disaster recovery grant programs, ended December 31, 2002. In October 2002, LMDC and ESDC worked with HUD to process an allocation of $150 million to BRG as part of a total $350 million allocation to three existing programs administered by ESDC, as described in Partial Action Plan No. 2. In August 2003, pursuant to the Partial Action Plan No.4, an additional $74.5 million was allocated to the BRG program. The total LMDC allocation for the BRG program is $224,500,000.--This program ended December 31, 2002 and final payments were made in 2003. The balance of the obligated funds were disbursed to reimburse the New York City Economic Development Corporation for the Lower Manhattan Business Recovery Grant Program, as approved in Partial Action Plan 4. In July 2006, $5.07 million was disbursed to NYC EDC . In addition, NYC EDC received $5 million from FEMA, which is expected to reduce the amount of funds needed from LMDC. There was not activity on this project in the first quarter of 2007."],[64754,"COMMUNITY/CULTURAL ENHANCEMENTS (FAP)-- LMDC issued an RFP for its Community Enhancement Program. Proposal&apos;s were received in November 2006. LMDC is assembling an advisory panel to evaluate proposals previously submitted for the Community Enhacement Program."],[65349,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) CAPITAL-- Funds provided for capital activities will support new construction of buildings, renovations, expansions into adjoining spaces, equipment purchases, and site acquisitions. LMDC continues to work with selected grant recipients to develop project scopes and to ensure compliance with all necessary and related federal laws and regulations, including compliance with HUD&apos;s environmental review procedures and required public notices. At the end of the first quarter, there were 11 executed contracts for capital projects. For those signed during quarter 4, 2006, Art in General completed its public art gallery renovation project; Lower Manhattan Cultural Council and Home for Contemporary Theater dba HERE Art Center successfully purchased their public facilities; and New Dance Alliance completed its dance floor installation. Battery Dance Company completed its public dance facility renovation and continues work on its arts in eduction program with Millenium High School. The New Museum of Contemporary Art continues to make progress on the architectural development and construction of its new museum in Lower Manhattan. Newly executed contracts include Downtown Community Television Center, National Museum of the American Indian, George Gustav Heye Center, Eldridge Street Project, and New York City Police Museum."],[65390,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PROGRAM AND EVENTS -- Funds provided will support educational programs, exhibitions, and developing promotional materials and initiatives. LMDC continues to work with selected grant recipients to further develop project scopes for the programs and events. In November 2006, HUD approved an amended PAP and reduced the Cultural Enhancement Fund by $7,000,000 to $28,000,000. These funds were reallocated to the Final Action Plan for cultural uses. As of March 31, 2007, eleven contracts supporting programmatic and event related activities were executed and the LMDC funded activities successfully launched. Alliance for Downtown NY (hosting the River to River festival), Bang on a Can, Battery Park City Parks Conservancy (hosting the Harmony on the Hudson family festival), and Tribeca Fiml Festival are all in the planning phase for upcoming summer events. New York Scandia Symphony hosted the second of three LMDC-funded concerts at Trinity Church, with an audience of over 200 people. Scandia is already preparing for the last LMDC funded concert in June. CultureNOW successfully redesigned its website which had a soft launch and will formally launch this summer. 3-Legged Dog continues to produce contemporary theatrical productions. Asian American Art Center commenced procurement of consultants related to its digitizing project. New York City Opera will complete its opera education course at the High School of Finance and Economics by the end of the academic year. Work continues on the remaining five contracts to be executed."],[65419,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PLANNING -- Funds for planning projects will support cultural organizations seeking new homes in Lower Manhattan, as well as several organizations that are in the early stages of a major expansion or early renovation. The funds will be used to assist these organizations with site search, project management, business plan development, and pre-design activities. LMDC continues to work with grant recipients to develop project planning scopes. As of March 31, 2007, the five institutions with fully executed contracts continued to make progress toward planning efforts. Children¿s Museum of the Arts, Harvestworks, Roulette, and Moving Image are engaged with consultants and are moving forward with business plans, project management, and consultant work. National Dance Institute has nearly completed its planning process and will soon submit its final report."],[66786,"CHINATOWN LOCAL DEVELOPMENT CORPORATION (LDC)--Funding for this program will be used to spearhead community improvements in Chinatown and engage in a combination of short-term projects and long-term planning. CHINATOWN CLEAN STREETS PROGRAM--The Chinatown Partnership LDC, in conjunction with the New York City Department of Small Business Services (SBS), will implement and oversee a comprehensive, multi-year Clean Streets program. A maintenance subcontractor was selected; Clean Streets workers have been deployed; and daily power washing, sweeping and tidy work have been performed. In previous quarters events such as the Lunar Stages and the 5th Annual Taste of Chinatown were held in Chinatown. Clean Street workers helped maintain the area during these events, and clean up after the events. Feedback on the Clean Streets program from the community indicates that Chinatown has been much cleaner since the inception of the Clean Streets program. It is estimated that 500-600 bags of garbage are collected daily because of the Clean Streets Program. CHINATOWN SHORT-TERM PROJECTS AND LONG-TERM PLANNING--In addition to the Clean Streets program, the Chinatown Partnership LDC will spearhead many community enhancement projects and initiatives. LMDC and the LDC are working on the scope of the project and expect to finalize a subrecipient agreement in the coming quarter."],[66844,"CHINATOWN ACQUISITION GRANT PROGRAM-- The planning phase of the project has been completed. HPD has announced the creation of the new Chinatown/Lower East Side Acquisition Program and issued an RFQ soliciting non-profit organizations to participate in the program. Three entities have been pre-qualified for participation in the Chinatown Lower East Side Acquisition Program: Asian Americans for Equality (AAFE), Cooper Square Mutual Housing Association in partnership with the Cooper Square Committee, and Settlement Housing Fund Inc. in partnership with Two Bridges Neighborhood Council. To date, HPD has approved one funding request: AAFE has been approved to receive $3.6 million in program funding for the acquisition of 36 units in two buildings on Henry Street. The funds were closed on February 7, 2007 for this project. HPD began reviewing three new building proposals from AFFE in the first quarter of 2007 and is working towards commitments on two of the three projects that were identified."],[66895,"EXPLORE CHINATOWN CAMPAIGN-- The Explore Chinatown Campaign has been transitioned to the Chinatown Partnership Local Development Corporation (LDC). A new outline for the ExploreChinatown.com site includes the ability to add an outline for adding multimedia content. Advertisements were updated for the Lunar New Year and the Read Lantern Market Month production began a series of short films to eventually be added to the site. Added to the site were restaurant reviews, Taste of Chinatown information, links to CCBA and NY Guides, coupons, reader reviews and various listings in the Visit and Locate sections of the site. A huge highlight for the CPLDC, this quarter was an article published in New York Magazine entitled ¿Essential Guide to Chinatown.¿ All activities related to this program are complete. LMDC is awaiting the final invoice from the subrecipient."],[66349,"COLUMBUS PARK PAVILION -- In the first quarter of 2007 work on the gas lines, gas meter and boiler neared completion with the exception of the awaiting inspections. The general contractor anticipates placing the final finish on the floors this quarter, but that has been hampered by low weather temperatures. The plumber and electrician have minor punch list work that needs to be performed. The site should be ready for public use by the end of May 2007."],[77225,"THE DRAWING CENTER-- In November 2006 HUD approved an amendment to the PAP reducing the Drawing Center¿s allocation to $2,000,000, and reallocating $8,000,000 to the Final Action Plan for cultural and community uses, with the Drawing Center preserved as an eligible recipient. A contract for $2,000,000 is in progress between the LMDC and The Drawing Center. In the first quarter of 2007 LMDC continued to work with the Drawing Center to complete and execute the subrecipient agreement."],[102447,"LOWER MANHATTAN ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT PROGRAM -- In the quarter ending March 31, 2007, LMDC and the City of New York executed a sub-recipient agreement that defined the three phases of the project: project planning and administration, project development, and implementation."],[102526,"The LMDC signed a Subrecipient Agreement with the New York City Department of Education in December 2006. LMDC has continued to work with the School Construction Authority to identify possible projects."],[103485,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT ACCESS--In the first quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with a project team consisting of the New York City Departments of Parks and Recreation, City Planning and Transportation, New York City Mayor&apos;s Office, environmental consultant AKRF, and environmental legal counsel Carter Ledyard & Milburne, LLP. During the first quarter of 2007, Phase I Environmental Site Assessments were prepared for the five project area subject sites. The scope of the environmental review was also expanded to encompass additional Phase 1A Archaeological Assessment studies."],[103825,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT ESPLANADE AND PIERS--During the first quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with a project team consisting of the New York City Departments of City Planning and Transportation, the New York City Economic Development Corporation (EDC), the New York City Mayor&apos;s Office, environmental consultant AKRF, and environmental legal counsel Carter Ledyard & Milburne, LLP. Public review of the Draft Environmental Impact Statement (DEIS) adopted on October 12, 2006 began on January 18, 2007 with publication and distribution of the DEIS. Comments to the DEIS were received at a public hearing held by LMDC on March 5, 2007. LMDC and its project team began preparing responses to comments to the DEIS. The public comment period closed on March 19, 2007."],[104121,"EAST SIDE K-8 SCHOOL¿The environmental review for this project is complete and HUD has approved the necessary funding. During the 1st Quarter of 2007, the LMDC continued to work with the School Construction Authority to finalize the Subrecipient Agreement."],[104283,"REALLOCATE EMPLOYMENT TRAINING ASSISTANCE PROGRAM-- There was no activity on this project in the first quarter of 2007."],[104329,"During the first quarter of 2007, LMDC met with the New York City Economic Development Corporation¿s consultants to develop the program description and draft design guidelines."],[104371,"FULTON CORRIDOR OPEN SPACES-- During the first quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with the Department of Parks in the environmental review process to include the completion of Phase 1A documentary studies for Pearl Street Playground and Titanic Park."],[104413,"FULTON CORRIDOR PROJECT PLANNING-- During the first quarter of 2007, LMDC met with its team of project consultants to start the environmental review process, including the environmental assessment examining the storefront and facade incentives program, open space improvements and the acquisition of a parcel of private property."],[104461,"During the first quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with EDC¿s project team in the environmental review process to include the completion of Phase 1A documentary studies for streetscape and street improvements."],[104685,"FITERMAN HALL RECONSTRUCTION-- LMDC is working with the City University of New York and the Dormitory Authority of the State of New York to finalize necessary agreements for this project."],[110037,"HISTORY AND HERITAGE IN DOWNTOWN NYC/Museums of Lower Manhattan (MOLM) -- In the first quarter of 2007 LMDC continued its ambitious marketing of the Museums of Lower Manhattan. Fifty-three thousand Museums of Lower Manhattan maps/brochures were distributed. Bandujo, Donker and Brothers continued advertisement placements in various tourist publications, local newspapers, magazines and on internet websites. A total of 27 Museums of Lower Manhattan print ads were placed during the quarter. The Kids Passport Program continued during the first quarter with an additional printing of 3,000 passports. Kids who visit any three of ten participating museums can get their passport stamped and receive a Museums of Lower Manhattan t-shirt. The campaign also ran a series of ads offering a \"Kids get in Free\" program during public school winter recess. The campaign will continue its print and internet advertisement featuring 2 for 1 offers and internet coupons to try and offset the transportation issues in Lower Manhattan."],[111899,"REHABILITATION/RECONSTRUCTION OF A PUBLIC IMPROVEMENT ---HUDSON RIVER PARK SEGMENT 3-- HUD approved Partial Action Plan 10, which allocated up to $70 million for the completion of Hudson River Park Segment 3 in Tribeca. Within this area would be two public recreational piers (Piers 25 and 26), an ecological pier, and an adjacent upland park. HRPT¿s contractor completed demolition of Piers 25 and 26 last quarter. There has been no on-site construction activity in the first quarter of 2007, however HRPT did award several contracts to gear up for construction activity in the upcoming quarter."],[112275,"AFFORDABLE HOUSING (FAP)-- LMDC has signed a subrecipient agreement with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development. HPD has begun the process of identifying an affordable housing project that is consistent with the General Project Criteria (as defined above)."],[120401,"INTERIM MEMORIAL-- This activity is complete."],[123915,"JOB CREATION AND RETENTION PROGRAM-- As of March 31, 2007, ESD has offered incentive grants to 125 companies. Seventy-seven companies have accepted grant offers worth $293.38 million to date. Of those accepted offers, 72 active projects have received ESD¿s Board approval, representing 72,956 created and retained jobs in Lower Manhattan and an additional 15,649 jobs retained elsewhere in New York City. The value of the ESD Board approved grants is $288,311,697. ESD has disbursed all of its original $170 million allocation for the program and has been drawing on LMDC¿s $150 million sub-allocation since August 2003."],[124127,"KNICKERBOCKER VILLAGE-- LMDC continues to work with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development to plan and develop the scope of the rehabilitation for the development. It is anticipated that this scope planning phase will be completed in the second quarter 2007 and a subrecipient agreement finalized in the near term. Once the subrecipient agreement is finalized progress will ensue."],[125468,"LOWER MANHATTAN COMMUNICATIONS OUTREACH CAMPAIGN ---The campaigns three components include: the creation of informational kiosks at strategic locations downtown that will double as visitor centers, the development of branded marketing materials and the distribution of LMDC printed materials and electronic updates. More than half of the campaign¿s funds have been allocated for the creation, design, construction, and maintenance of the two informational kiosks: the Downtown Rebuilding Information Kiosk, which is located at the new World Trade Center Path Station, and the Chinatown Kiosk, which is located between Canal, Baxter, and Walker Streets. In the first quarter of 2007 the WTC Kiosk had 132,575 visitors and the Chinatown Kiosk served 43,082 visitors. Both kiosks are stocked with current information about Lower Manhattan and the rebuilding efforts. The campaign provided for the printing of marketing materials, including five Renew Downtown newsletters of which hundreds-of-thousands were printed and distributed to various locations all over Lower Manhattan. Palm Cards were developed, printed, and circulated at special events, the WTC kiosk, and various other locations and functions. The Palm Cards included: Greenmarket 2003, River to River 2003, the opening of Vesey Street Bridge, the opening of the PATH Station, the History & Heritage Campaign, Downtown for the Holidays, Tribeca Film Festival 2004, Chinatown (3 printings), Parks (two printings), the Greenmarket 2004, the WTC Memorial 2006 (3 printings), and Downtown Culture (3 printings)."],[125483,"LIVING MEMORIAL-- There was no activity to report on this project in the first quarter of 2007."],[126790,"LOWER MANHATTAN FERRY SERVICE PROGRAM-- In the quarter ending March 31, 2007, LMDC, The Port Authority of New York and New Jersey, New York State Department of Transportation, City of Yonkers, and New York Water Taxi planned for the initiation of a new ferry service. The service is expected to start in May 2007. A fare of $400 for 40 trips has been set, a marketing plan developed, and work on the Yonkers Pier is proceeding. Rockland County is negotiating with New York Water Taxi to operate ferry service between Haverstraw and Yonkers."],[126814,"LOWER MANHATTAN PUBLIC INFORMATION PROGRAM --- The Lower Manhattan Public Information Plan Program (Program) is a public awareness campaign which aims to facilitate the area¿s recovery by providing a trusted source of information. It is intended to enhance the coordinated efforts of the City of New York, New York State, and federal agencies throughout the long-term process of rebuilding and redevelopment. The Program consists of a website, a newsletter, and informational construction signage that focuses on Lower Manhattan and delivers the most accurate and timely information about the area¿s rebuilding progress to the area stakeholders including residents, businesses, and visitors. This is accomplished through marketing, advertising, stakeholder outreach, and strategic planning. The centerpiece of the Program is a comprehensive website, www.LowerManhattan.info, which offers regularly updated news and information for area residents, workers, and visitors. -- October 2005 was the final month for LMDC¿s hosting of the LowerManhattan.info program. As of November 2005 the Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center is in charge of the site and program. The Program continues to move forward with oversight and funding from other sources. The Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center is now in charge of monitoring the site."],[126836,"LOWER MANHATTAN STREET MANAGEMENT PROGRAM¿ In the quarter ending March 31, 2007, work progressed on the following four tasks: - Merging of two traffic models into one traffic model and the incorporation of the Battery Park City area into the model. (At the end of the first phase of this project, NYC DOT had two separate traffic models that covered the eastern and western portions of Lower Manhattan.) - Coordination efforts with the Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center (LMCCC) - Bus Study to determine the existing supply and demand of Lower Manhattan bus parking, as well as the volume and staging locations of buses entering the study area. - Analysis of ¿placard parking¿ in the various districts of Lower Manhattan in order to assess the impact of these patterns on the general user. - Traffic analysis that determined the segment of Pearl Street next to a playground can be closed and made into a pedestrian only space."],[127931,"There is nothing to report on this project."],[125160,"TRIBUTE IN LIGHT-- The Tribute in Light of September 11, 2006 was successfully and momentously displayed from the Battery Park Tunnel Garage, where it will remain housed through 2008. During the first quarter of 2007, there was no activity. Municipal Art Society intends to begin preparation for the 2007 display during the second quarter."],[129387,"MASARYK TOWER REHABILITATION-- LMDC continues to work with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development to plan and develop the scope of the rehabilitation for the development. It is anticipated that this planning phase will be completed in the following quarter. Once a subrecipient agreement is finalized, progress will ensue."],[134416,"NYSE -- During the first quarter of 2007, the City of New York (with the input of stakeholders) evaluated and planned various improvements including: anchoring NoGos (polygonal sidewalk barriers) to the sidewalk, a turntable for vehicle security, new street furnishings, light poles, repaneling of security booths, pedestrian interdiction near New York Stock Exchange, Eurocobble sidewalk treatment, and replacement of the fence in front of the Stock Exchange. Various issues are being addressed including access to buildings, maintenance, security, and potential for expanded space to accommodate special events."],[135869,"LOWER MANHATTAN PLANNING STUDIES AND IMPROVEMENTS-- The East River Waterfront Planning Study has been completed and led to a second phase of work for the East River Esplanade/Piers and East River Access (see reports ERA-0240 and EREP-0240)."],[135814,"LMDC PLANNING - ECONOMIC ANALYSIS ¿ In the quarter ending March 31, 2007, LMDC continued efforts to measure the economic benefits of its Cultural Enhancement Fund (CEF) Program. Economists at the Louis Berger Group are assisting LMDC in the development of performance measures as well as a database for calculating and tracking these measures."],[135920,"LMDC PLANNING--ENVIRONMENTAL --- LMDC sought the advice and counsel of law firms and environmental consulting firms on retainer on environmental and land use requirements and matters relating to LMDC¿s planning and development initiatives in Lower Manhattan. Historically, such projects included early phases of (1) the World Trade Center Memorial and Redevelopment Plan (WTC Plan), (2) the East River Waterfront Esplanade and Piers Project, (3) cultural enhancement and community improvement projects, and (4) public information, outreach, and participation efforts relating to the foregoing activities. With the assistance of the firms, LMDC reviewed technical issues relating to the April 2004 Programmatic Agreement relating to the WTC Plan pursuant to Section 106 of the National Historic Preservation Act; refinements to the WTC Plan; and the deconstruction of [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS]. During the first quarter of 2007, LMDC considered potential environmental review requirements for future projects and programs as well as possible adjustments to approved projects."],[135959,"LMDC PLANNING - OFF-SITE INITIATIVES-- LMDC made drawdowns related to Chinatown traffic studies and Greenwich street planning studies were made during the first quarter of 2007."],[135994,"WEST STREET PEDESTRIAN CONNECTIONS-- All planning for West Street has been completed. The New York State Department of Transportation has opened the West Street Promenade South, a tree-lined pedestrian walkway extending along both sides of Route 9A (West Street) from West Thames Street to Battery Place in Lower Manhattan. Roadway and sidewalk improvements provide increased pedestrian space by adding recreational areas and lush greenery to the heavily trafficked roadway and complete a substantial portion of the Hudson River Park. Promenade South is the first phase of a two-phase State Department of Transportation (NYSDOT) project designed to repair damage sustained during the terrorist attacks on the World Trade Center on September 11, 2001 and provide an appropriately dignified and aesthetically graceful setting for the World Trade Center Memorial planned nearby. When both phases are complete, the full promenade project will improve pedestrian and vehicular circulation at Route 9A between Chambers Street and Battery Place in Lower Manhattan. The project will strengthen the connection among the future World Trade Center Memorial on the east side of Route 9A, the World Financial Center and Battery Park City on the west side of the roadway, and historic Battery Park, embarkation point for the Statue of Liberty and Ellis Island at the southern tip of Manhattan."],[136038,"WORLD TRADE CENTER PLANNING -- During the quarter ending March 31, 2007, LMDC¿s Board of Directors took several actions relating to its ongoing work at the World Trade Center. The Board affirmed an amended General Project Plan for the World Trade Center Memorial and Cultural Program, approved an addendum to the Record of Decision and Findings Statement for the World Trade Center Memorial and Redevelopment Plan, and approved the findings of a Technical Memorandum analyzing the amendments and addendum. The original General Project Plan and Record of Decision and Findings Statement had been approved by the Board in June 2004. LMDC also worked with Studio Daniel Libeskind, Port Authority, City of New York, and the World Trade Center leaseholders on further fine-tuning and revising the Commercial Design Guidelines for the World Trade Center site. These guidelines are intended to set out basic urban design principles for the overall site redevelopment and allow for the vision of the WTC plan to be fulfilled over time. The design guidelines include a chapter on sustainable design and green construction."],[136192,"NEIGHBORHOOD PARKS AND OPEN SPACES-- As of March 31, 2007 all thirteen park sites have been substantially completed. Progress on the remaining site, the Wall Street Park fountain, continues while the fabrication of the fountain glass nears completion. It is anticipated that the fountain will be installed in Spring 2007. The second phase of parks improvements continued the environmental review process in the first quarter of 2007. These projects included: Washington Market Park Comfort Station, Collect Pond Park, Sara D. Roosevelt Phase 2, Allen and Pike Street Malls, James Madison Park and Canal Varick Laight Park. The subrecipient agreement for this portion of work was executed in March 2007. It is expected that work will proceed according to the scope of the agreement in the future. LMDC is working with the NYC Department and Design and Construction regarding improvements to Louise Nevelson Plaza, which is expected to be a component of the street reconstruction of Liberty Street, Maiden Lane, and Pearl Street."],[144503,"RIVER TO RIVER FESTIVAL 2002-- This project is completed."],[144512,"RIVER TO RIVER FESTIVAL 2004-- There was no activity on this project in the first quarter of 2007."],[169137,"RESIDENTIAL GRANT PROGRAM (RGP)-- LMDC RESIDENTIAL GRANT PROGRAM (RGP) -- As of March 31, 2007, 39,802 applications have been approved. Of the 39,802 approved applications representing approximately $227,248,354.97 million in grants, LMDC distributed 30,923 September 11th Residents Grants, 26,713 2-Year Grants, and 8,122 Family Grants to individuals. LMDC also distributed 1,465 2-Year Grants to 79 businesses. One additional grant will be disbursed during the second quarter of 2007. LMDC continues to implement all necessary program close-out procedures, including review of all appeals received, as well as perform any necessary recoupment procedures."],[171003,"SMALL FIRM ATTRACTION AND RETENTION GRANT (SFARG) -- LMDC and ESDC have made over 3,800 grants to 2,484 businesses. Twenty million dollars of LMDC funds have been disbursed for this program. Sixty-three of the grants that were made were distributed in this quarter."],[171229,"SITE 5B ([REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS])-- Construction of the mixed income rental component of [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] is 55% complete. Work is proceeding in general accordance with the plan and specifications."],[172398,"SOUTHERN SITE--The World Trade Center Memorial and Redevelopment Plan (WTC Plan) includes several parcels of land just south of the World Trade Center Site, referred to collectively as the ¿Southern Site,¿ that will be used to construct infrastructure and other components of the WTC Plan. LMDC acquired the largest parcel at [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in August 2004, [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in February 2006, and the following parcels constitute the remainder: (1) privately-owned [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS]; and (2) several public New York City streets-Washington Street between Cedar and Liberty Streets and subsurface portions of Cedar and Liberty Streets. LMDC has been working with the The City of New York and The Port Authority of New York and New Jersey on the acquisition and configuration of the street parcels as well as the review and development of plans for the entire Southern Site, including the redesign and relocation of St. Nicholas Church. In the first quarter of 2007, the Southern Site was expanded slightly to include additional street parcels and added to the project site for the WTC Plan along with certain parcels to the north of the WTC Site, as described in the General Project Plan for the World Trade Center Memorial and Cultural Program, as amended February 17, 2007."],[172302,"SPLENDOR OF FLORENCE FESTIVAL-- There was no activity to report on this project in the first quarter of 2007."],[172792,"STORY CORPS-- This project is completed."],[172808,"DOWNTOWN ALLIANCE STREETSCAPE PROGRAM-- This project is completed."],[172915,"TRIBECA FILM FESTIVAL (Film Festival)-- All activities and payments related to the 2004 and 2005 Film Festivals are complete. Going forward all activities related to the Film Festival will be funded through the Cultural Enhancement Grant allocations."],[172950,"LOWER MANHATTAN TRANSPORTATION IMPROVEMENT PROGRAM -- In 2007, LMDC and the City of New York executed a subrecipient agreement that defined the three phases of the project: project planning and administration, project development, and implementation."],[173174,"TRIBUTE VISITORS CENTER-- All activities have been completed for this project. The final payment to the subrecipient is pending and should happen in the second quarter of 2007."],[173330,"PERFORMING ARTS CENTER DESIGN-- During the first quarter of 2007, LMDC continued its study of Below Grade Infrastructure for the World Trade Center Performing Arts Center. The study evaluates options for the Performing Arts Center and identifies special considerations and open issues for moving forward with the next stage of the cultural building¿s development."],[173384,"WTC MEMORIAL AND MEMORIAL MUSEUM -- LMDC, in partnership with the World Trade Center Memorial Foundation (WTCMF), will provide initial funding for the construction documentation and construction of the Memorial, and will plan for and oversee the design development and construction documentation for the Memorial Museum, and possibly other elements of the WTC Plan. In the quarter ending March 31, 2007, the WTC Memorial Foundation performed the following: ¿ Excavated and poured harden wall foundations ¿ Evaluated exhibition design service firms ¿ Institutional development of the combined Museum Pavillion and the Memorial Museum, including the space program and the development of exhibition design scope and curatorial resources. ¿ Approved selection and retention of Owen Steel Company, Inc. to provide the Jumbo Steel portion of the Steel package for the construction of the Memorial and Memorial Museum. ¿ Redesigned the North/South Shear Wall between the HUB and Memorial projects accommodating the Port Authority¿s request to change the boundaries of the project as it relates to the HUB project. ¿ Digging of the trees began in Maryland. ¿ Studied various structural engineering options to support and expose the existing West Slurry Wall. Please see W-MUS-0171 for information on the related Museum Pavillion."],[173435,"MUSEUM PAVILLION-¿ In the first quarter of 2007, the World Trade Center Memorial Foundation announced that the Visitor Orientation and Education Center (VOEC) will be referred to as the ¿Museum Pavilion¿ going forward. Several schematic design options were presented by Snohetta to the WTC Memorial Foundation, City Planning, and City Hall. The WTC Memorial Foundation received approval to proceed with a Museum Pavilion that would have the following program: auditorium with 199 seats and raked floor, flexible space on second level, family room, and security and screening at the plaza level. Schematic design for the Museum Pavilion began in March. The Museum Pavillion design team continues to coordinate with the Port Authority World Trade Center Transportation Hub design team and the Memorial design team on below grade spaces, structural and mechanical issues."],[173519,"WEST STREET PEDESTRIAN CONNECTIONS¿All work has been completed. Construction of the Vesey Bridge was completed in stages, with the opening in time for the temporary PATH station in November 2003. This was followed by the completion of the Bridge Extension in June 2004, and final delivery of the elevators in November 2004"],[64616,"CATEGORY ONE--EMERGENCY AND TEMPORARY SERVICE RESPONSE-- In September 2006, LMDC issued proposed changes to Partial Action Plan S-2, and during the fourth quarter of 2006, LMDC reviewed public comments to such changes. As of March 31, 2007, field audit work has been concluded for AT&T Corporation, Verizon Communications, Inc., Consolidated Edison, Inc. and AT&T Wireless Services Incorporated. Verizon Communications, Inc. has appealed some of the findings included in the final audit report. Requests for Category One totaled $403,674,125."],[64646,"CATEGORY TWO--PERMANENT RESTORATION AND INFRASTRUCTURE IMPROVEMENTS--- As outlined in Partial Action Plan S-2, Category Two pertains to permanent restoration and infrastructure improvements. As of April 27, 2006 Category Two has been divided into two separate sub-categories for funding, 1) Permanent Restoration and Infrastructure Improvements and 2) Southern Site. Category Two Southern Site qualifying expenditures are eligible for reimbursement according to the payment schedule as outlined in the Category Two Southern Site Guidelines. Category Two requests total $421,042,565 (Permanent Restoration $383,614,301 + Southern Site 37,428,264). Disbursements to date total $31,289,698, (Permanent Restoration $30,000,000 + Southern Site 1,289,698), leaving a balance of $291,736,329 of a $330 million allocation. The application submission deadline for Category Two Permanent Restoration and Infrastructure Improvements was December 31, 2004, and amended applications were accepted through June 30, 2005. As of March 31, 2007, Category Two ¿ Permanent Restoration reimbursement requests were received from Consolidated Edison, Inc., Verizon Communications, Inc. and the AT&T Corporation. The application submission deadline for Category Two ¿Southern Site is March 31, 2008. As of March 31, 2007, Category Two ¿ Southern Site reimbursement requests were received from Consolidated Edison, Inc. and Verizon Communications, Inc. During the first quarter of 2007, an advance of $1,289,698 was made to Consolidated Edison, Inc. under the Category Two Southern Site Guidelines. In September 2006, LMDC issued proposed changes to Partial Action Plan S-2, and during the fourth quarter of 2006, LMDC reviewed public comments to such changes."],[136106,"PLANNING AND ADMINSTRATION--¿- UTILITIES RESTORATION AND INFRASTRUCTURE REBUILDING (URIR) PROGRAM.-- To date, program administration funds have been used for activities related to the implementation of Categories One and Two of the URIR Program. Such activities include general management, oversight, and coordination. These activities also include program publication, review of proposed infrastructure investments, environmental review clearance, financial evaluation and processing of funding reimbursement requests, and auditing of funds that have been reimbursed for Category One and Category Two.--Program administration funds have also been used to fund feasibility studies. -- AKRF Inc and The Louis Berger Group continue to provide environmental consulting services. AKRF is currently working on AT&T¿s Category Two application. The Louis Berger group has been assigned the Verizon Category Two application and work is ongoing. --KPMG is providing IT consulting services including testing and auditing the integrity and security of the electronic systems of the applicants. ¿ MOSAICS- LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the NYC Department of Transportation (DOT) in the third quarter of 2006. The DOT will conduct an initial phase of planning and design for certain upgrades to the MOSAICS system. This will include, but not be limited to, a review and analysis of current DOT and MOSAICS processes and a technical feasibility analysis and review of MOSAICS system architecture. In September 2006, LMDC issued proposed changes to Partial Action Plan S-2, and during the fourth quarter of 2006, LMDC continued to review comments."],[102374,"HUD approved the Bridge Loan Program May 9, 2006, as a part of the State&iquest;s Initial Action Plan. On February 10, 2006, a Cooperative Endeavor was entered into between the Louisiana Public Facilities Authority (LPFA), Louisiana Economic Development (LED), and the Division of Administration (DOA), which obligated $30,000,000 in State CDBG funds to be utilized in the Extended Bridge Loan program. That agreement was amended, on June 20, 2006, to include $10,000,000 to cover the cost of the Emergency Bridge Loan program that was originally funded by LED, as well as $55,000,000 to be utilized in the Enhanced Bridge Loan Program. Prior to the Enhanced Loan program being implemented, a change in geographical location of beneficiaries was requested through the submittal of a substantial amendment that was approved by HUD on September 15, 2006. This amendment revised the allocation language from a 50% allocation to the thirteen most impacted parishes, to a minimum of 50% will be allocated to the thirteen most impacted parishes, and up to 50% allocated to the other twenty-four impacted parishes. This change transpired due to the demand for loan guarantees amounting to $8,820,765 in the most impacted areas. Also, during the previous quarter, the Extended BL made a total of 325 loan guarantees totaling $28,696,080 in a 37 parish area. An estimated 3,872 jobs were reported as being created or retained as a result of the first two Bridge Loan Programs. The Enhanced BL program deadline was December 29, 2006. There were a total of 57 loans guaranteed, for a principal sum of $ 5,409,427 for this BL program. The estimated number of jobs created and retained will be 304. The total amount paid this quarter for Bridge Loan principal is $1,985,300."],[102509,"The Tourism Marketing Program was approved by HUD on September 15, 2006. The program has awarded $28,500,000 to 17 tourism agencies throughout the area. The interagency agreement with the Office of Tourism is complete and all the sub-recipient agreements have been signed. A funds distribution process was established between the Department of Culture, Recreation and Tourism (DCR&T) and OCD-DRU by establishing internal controls to insure a reasonable turn around in DCR&T&apos;s request for payments. Tourism has spent funds totaling $704,217 on Disaster Marketing and of the 17 sub-recipients&apos; projects underway, only 10 have spent funds by the end of this quarter. Performance Measures for 2006 will be available summer 2007."],[102551,"HUD approved Action Plan One Amendment 5 on January 3, 2007;OCD is currently awaiting a HUD waiver that would allow CDBG funding to be used for research activities in higher education. Eligible applicants would be limited to institutions of higher education in southeastern Louisiana. Action Plan One Amendment 5 declares eligible applicants are limited to institutions of higher education in the southeastern Louisiana: Baptist Theological Seminary, Delgado Community College, Dillard University, LSU Agricultural Center (hurricane-impacted facilities), LSU Health Sciences Center-New Orleans, Louisiana Universities Marine Consortium, Loyola University New Orleans, McNeese State University, Nunez Community College, Our Lady of Holy Cross College, Southern University-New Orleans, Sowela Technical Community College, Tulane Health Sciences Center, Tulane University, University of New Orleans, and Xavier University of Louisiana."],[104024,"HUD approved the Recovery Workforce Program on September 15, 2006. A Request for Proposals was issued on October 4, 2006; proposals were due to Workforce Commission (WC) on or before November 28, 2006. The WC created an evaluation team to review the proposals. There were 48 proposals submitted for review amounting to $165 million. The final evaluation of the proposals was due to the WC by December 7th for final approval by December 14th. Of the 48 proposals submitted 19 were chosen to be funded. In March, agreement between WC and OCD-DRU was signed and submitted to OCR. Meeting between WC Acting Director and OCD-DRU staff resulted in the completion of template for sub-recipient agreements."],[104146,"The Small Firm Recovery Loan and Grant (SFLG) program was approved in Action Plan One, Amendment 2 on September 15, 2006. The Solicitation for Grant Application process was initiated in October 2006 and due December 1. Just prior to the SGA due date, the State decided to change the parameters of the program and augment it with additional funds beyond $38 million allocated in Amendment 2. An additional $105 million was transferred to the program in Amendment 8, approved on February 9, 2007. In the first round of grant reviews, 5,553 applications for this funding were received and 3,469 (62%) grants were awarded to small firms. The total amount of funding was $62.7 million with the average amount being $18,100. The funds were distributed across the state as follows: 8% - Central LA, 12% - Southwest LA, and 80% Southeast LA. Minority and/or woman owned businesses accounted for 53% of the funds. Approximately one third of the businesses assisted were single person firms which explains why 88% of the firms assisted created less than 10 jobs each; leaving 9% creating 11-25 and 3% creating 26-50. A second round for this program will be undertaken later in the year, with the potential for additional funding of $38 million in grants and loans."],[104165,"The Small Firm Recovery Loan and Grant (SFLG) program was approved in Action Plan One, Amendment 2 on September 15, 2006. The Solicitation for Grant Application process was initiated in October 2006 and due December 1. Just prior to the SGA due date, the State decided to change the parameters of the program and augment it with additional funds beyond $38 million allocated in Amendment 2. An additional $105 million was transferred to the program in Amendment 8, approved on February 9, 2007. In the first round of grant reviews, 5,553 applications for this funding were received and 3,469 (62%) grants were awarded to small firms. The total amount of funding was $62.7 million with the average amount being $18,100. The funds were distributed across the state as follows: 8% - Central LA, 12% - Southwest LA, and 80% Southeast LA. Minority and/or woman owned businesses accounted for 53% of the funds. Approximately one third of the businesses assisted were single person firms which explains why 88% of the firms assisted created less than 10 jobs each; leaving 9% creating 11-25 and 3% creating 26-50. A second round for this program will be undertaken later in the year, with the potential for additional funding of $38 million in grants and loans."],[104204,"See narrative for Small Loan - LMI (ESLG)"],[104297,"The Small Firm Technical Assistance Program as outlined in Action Plan One Amendment 2 was approved by HUD on September 15, 2006. The Solicitation for Grant Applications process commenced in October 2006. In January, a Review Board was initiated. In February, 19 sub-recipients were selected. The Louisiana Department of Economic Development met with the 19 in March to go over program content and process."],[107405,"By the end of the second quarter, the Road Home Homeowner Assistance program had received and recorded 120,789 applications (68,900 online; 46,234 hardcopy; 5,655 phone in system) from Louisiana homeowners whose homes had been adversely affected by hurricanes Katrina and Rita. Out of this number, a total of 93,037 appointments were held by ICF International with these Louisianans for the purpose of discussing and deciding upon the homeowner&apos;s best option with the program. As of March 31, 5,708 closing were held, resulting in the release of $428,401,080.36 to Louisiana homeowners."],[107419,"See Activity Narrative for Road Home-LMI (HAOO). As of March 31, 5,708 closing (2,139 being LMI) were held, resulting in the release of $428,401,080.36 to Louisiana homeowners."],[108331,"As of March 2007, the Louisiana Institute for Building Technology and Safety (LA IBTS) had deployed 78 personnel to assist with code inspections, plan evaluations, code permitting, and training in the 11 parishes most impacted by Hurricanes Katrina and Rita: Orleans; Jefferson; St. Bernard; St. Tammany; Calcasieu; Plaquemines; Vermillion; Terrebonne; Cameron; Washington; and Iberia. Based on Memorandums of Understanding entered into by the Department of Public Safety (DPS), these personnel are currently deployed in ten of the eleven parishes and in 12 separate cities. Although Iberia Parish has yet to request assistance, one of its municipalities has. Quarterly Slum & Blight and Urgent Need activities include, respectively: Plan Reviews - 179, 968; Building Inspections - 3319, 5395; Electrical Inspections - 4630, 6022; Mechanical Inspections - 0, 1188; and Plumbing Inspections - 0, 2412. Number of individuals trained in the Slum and Blight area (Orleans Parish) this quarter is 0, while the total number trained is 54. This is only a six month contract, but already the parishes and cities are requesting assistance for at least another year."],[108354,"See Activity Narrative for Bldg Code - SB (HBCE). Quarterly Slum & Blight and Urgent Need activities include, respectively: Plan Reviews - 179, 968; Building Inspections - 3319, 5395; Electrical Inspections - 4630, 6022; Mechanical Inspections - 0, 1188; and Plumbing Inspections - 0, 2412. Number of individuals trained in the Slum and Blight area (Orleans Parish) this quarter is 0, while the total number trained is 54."],[108409,"# of Households Assisted (Face to Face Counseling) - 73 moderate income applicants and 36 in the Urgent Need category. # of Households Assisted (Call Center) - 66 moderate income applicants and 44 in the Urgent Need category."],[108435,"See Activity Narrative for Capacity Grant - LMI (HCAP). # of Households Assisted (Face to Face Counseling) - 73 moderate income applicants and 36 in the Urgent Need category. # of Households Assisted (Call Center) - 66 moderate income applicants and 44 in the Urgent Need category."],[109097,"The Housing Development Loan Fund program is combined contractually with the Land Assembly Operations program. A meeting is scheduled with Local Initiatives Support Corporation (LISC) and Enterprise Community Partners Inc. for April 13 to finalize programmatic details and DRU requirements. Launch of this program is planned for late April."],[109143,"Contract with environmental consulting firm, Environ, is still undergoing negotiations. Once the contract is signed, Environ will be responsible for the environmental review for approximately 2000 state buildings, as well as site-specific review of approximately 2,950 small rental units (from 1-4 units) to determine if historic preservation issues, such as historic structures and archaeological artifacts, are cleared before any new construction or reconstruction begins. These will be handled through the Environmental Section of OCD-DRU through the use of work orders and regular updates that are sent to the Agency by Environ."],[110044,"The Land Assembly Operations program is combined contractually with the Housing Development Loan Fund program. A meeting is scheduled with Local Initiatives Support Corporation (LISC) and Enterprise Community Partners Inc. for April 13 to finalize programmatic details and DRU requirements. Launch of this program is planned for late April."],[110192,"The state entered into an interagency agreement with the Department of Social Services (DSS) effective March 27, 2007. The first priority for use of the $25.9 million in CDBG funds is to restore storm-damaged homeless shelters in the most heavily impacted parishes. A Request for Proposals was advertised in January, 2007. Proposals submitted by March 2, 2007 were evaluated for awards. DSS announced that $2.1 million would be awarded to three local entities for repairs at 25 homeless facilities in the parishes of Orleans, St. Tammany, and Calcasieu. The repairs will restore 547, or approximately 32%, of an estimated 1,700 beds lost during the storms. The awards are to Unity of Greater New Orleans ($1,400.490 for 433 beds at 11 facilities), South West Louisiana Homeless Coalition ($399,966 for 60 beds at 13 facilities) and St. Tammany Parish ($254,818 for 54 beds at one facility). The second priority is to initiate the Homeless Prevention and Rapid Rehousing portion of the program. Total funding for this program is $6.2 million. Of that, $4 million is being allocated to impacted parishes and the balance to the remainder of the state. Additionally, $2.5 million is being made available in PSH Interim Rental Assistance for the most vulnerable population including the homeless, disabled homeless, children aging out of Foster Card and other groups targeted by DSS. These funds are being allocated to Local Continuums of Care organizations or local governments to address homeless prevention and move-in expenses, Housing First supportive services and Housing First rental assistance. The RFP for the Homeless Prevention and Rapid Rehousing program is scheduled to be released March 30, 2007."],[112242,"The State¿s CDBG funds totaling $72,730,000 will be administered by the Department of Health and Hospitals (DHH) through an interagency agreement that is currently being finalized. DHH will, in turn, contract with designated Local Lead Agencies (LLA) who will directly administer the funds for supportive services in conjunction with the property providers. The majority of the PSH units will be provided by LIHTC developments that have committed to at least a 5% set aside of units for PSH. It is anticipated that the Department of Social Services, through a separate interagency agreement, will provide some additional PSH units that can be served through DHH. Additionally, in the Small Rental Property program, some non-profits receiving incentive awards will be providing PSH units that will be available for support through this grant. Awards are limited to $25,000 per PSH unit and have a term of 10 years. As PSH units are built and available for occupancy, the LLAs will contract directly with the property providers to ensure compliance with the requirements of the funding and provide the PSH tenant to the owner for occupancy. LIHTC units are not expected to be available until 2008 and 2009."],[112380,"Prior to the disastrous 2005 hurricane season, a large segment of low-income working families resided in one- to four-unit rental buildings, including single-family homes, duplexes, three- and four-unit dwellings that were owned and operated by small-scale landlords. In the wake of the storms, it became clear that an unprecedented number (approximately 82,000) of multi-family rental properties had been destroyed or severely damaged. Replacement of the damaged or destroyed rental housing in the most heavily damaged hurricane areas is vital to the return of a strong workforce and is a keystone of Louisiana&apos;s economic recovery. Small Rental Property Program eligibility will be limited to the 13 most affected parishes: Acadia, Calcasieu, Cameron, Iberia, Jefferson, Orleans, Plaquemines, St. Bernard, St. Tammany, Tangipahoa, Terrebonne, Vermilion and Washington. The Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) and the Office of Community Development (OCD) approved the program on December 28, 2006; the final scoring design was approved January 5, 2007. The small Rental application was approved by the LRA late in January 2007. ICF International began accepting applications for the first of multiple competitive rounds on January 29, 2007. Round 1 closed on March 15th for the general pool and on March 22nd for the non-profit pool. Over 6500 applications were received. Of those, 2,693 small-scale rental property owners were awarded over $202 million in federal funds. Through the funding provided in the first round, more than 4.276 affordable rental units will be restored and offered to low- to moderate-income level working families. The Small Rental Property Program expects Round 2 to begin accepting applications in May and remain open for 30 days."],[112720,"The State&apos;s CDBG funds will provide about $440M in gap financing and Project Based Rental Assistance in order to assist 33 projects that will create more than 5,700 new or restored rental units in storm damaged areas. The LIHTC-CDBG Piggyback Program will combine the resources of Gulf Opportunity Zone Tax Credits, CDBG funding, Section 8 project based housing vouchers, and leveraged private investiments to generate the rental units. The program will support the development of mixed-income communities and ensure the restoration of rental housing in the most heavily impacted parishes. Eligible applicants were developers who applied for Low Income Housing Tax Credits. The primary method of award was to sponsors who applied for the received GO Zone credits under the 2007/2008 LIHTC allocation rounds administered by the Louisiana Housing Finance Agency. The CDBG funds were allocated among three pools identified as Mixed-Income, Additional Affordability, and Public Housing Authority Redevelopments. In most cases, these mixed income developments contained at least 60% market rate units and at least 20% deeply targeted units - affordable to households earning less than 40% of the Area Median Income. Funding was available in the form of either project based rental assistance, gap financing, or a combination thereof. All 33 developments assisted with Tax Credits and CDBG will provide at least 5 percent of their units for Permanent Supportive Housing (PSH). Many will provide more PSH units than the required set aside. This effort represents the first major development of PSH in the State of Louisiana and the very first PSH units to be provided in mixed income settings. The program was available with the rollout of the 2007/2008 GO Zone LIHTC Qualified Allocation Plan. The..."],[118753,"This activity originally held all of the funds for Long Term Community Recovery, and the obligation above had been created for that purpose. It has since been broken out into the various activities."],[119183,"Action Plan One, Amendment 10, which includes $100 million for match of Category A&B FEMA Public Assistance (PA) grants, was submitted to HUD on March 23, 2007. The application forms for PA match and FEMA-ineligible repairs at schools have been tested and will be available to all potential applicants next quarter. A Request For Proposal to procure services to aid in outreach and assistance to local governments and in processing applications has been published."],[137260,"Through the work of its various committees and task forces, the Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) has developed a series of strategic priorities and recovery plans in the areas of housing, infrastructure and economic development. The LRA has led the development of recovery policy and initiatives in the areas of housing, infrastructure, and economic development. These initiatives overlap with policy and planning in areas of health care, social services, education, and other subjects. The majority of LRA&apos;s work on the development, passage, submission and implementation of CDBG action plans has been tied to policies on housing, infrastructure and economic development -- the bedrocks of recovery. During this most recent quarter, the LRA has focused heavily on economic and workforce development program development and implementation; infrastructure recovery programs and implementation issues, including education infrastructure design considerations; homeowner and rental housing program policy and implementation oversight issues; and community and regional planning efforts related to recovery priorities. Regarding the long-term planning process, the LRA collaborated with the AIA and APA to develop rebuilding principles, upon which the LRA initiated a planning effort called Louisiana Speaks. This is a multifaceted planning initiative designed to address planning at every level. This extends from community planning such as the Unified New Orleans Plan (the LRA has supported UNOP through the recruitment of $3 million in private funding from the Rockefeller Foundation and the commitment of $2 million in CDBG funds for outreach, including communications and input with the New Orleans diaspora) to other parish-wide comprehensive, long-term recovery strategies in collaborat..."],[170196,"The Disaster Recovery Unit (DRU) of the Office of Community Development (OCD), Division of Administration (DOA), was created in the aftermath of the 2005 Hurricanes Katrina and Rita. The DRU administers the CDBG Disaster Recovery funds approved by Congress on December 23, 2005. Personnel were hired from within Louisiana and around the United States with experience in all aspects of CDBG programs. Managers and staff were retained in the areas of homeownership housing, multifamily/rental/supportive housing, infrastructure, economic development, policy and reporting, legal, and financial and auditing. Experienced employees of the Office of Community Development, CDBG Program, provide training as needed to new hires. In addition, the OCD supplied CDBG &iquest;boot camp&iquest; training in partnership with the Council of State Community Development Agencies. The DOA&iquest;s resources are available to augment the DRU with expertise in budgeting, accounting, reporting, contract review and legal matters. The DRU is in close contact with the Louisiana Legislative Auditors. Currently, 8 auditors are assigned to the DRU. We are planning on adding 4 more auditors in the near future. At present, there are 49 people working in the DRU. The DRU received approval in September 2006 to raise our total number of staff to 86. This does not include the 30 approved LRA positions. Currently, there 37 vacant positions. The administrative budget is used to fund salaries and related benefits, travel expenses, operating supplies and services, professional services, and inter-agency transfers. Community meetings have been held throughout the impacted parishes and in cities with large numbers of evacuees. These meetings have raised citizen awareness of the recovery programs. Importantly, the me..."],[170317,"Technical Assistance to State."],[60482,"While the federal appropriation for WTC disaster relief allows an allocation of up to 10 percent of the $700 million award for administration and planning, Empire State Development Corporation, in cooperation with the NYC Economic Development Corporation, has allocated $14 million for administration. These activities provide for the coordination and administration of business assistance programs funded through the federal appropriation, centralization of application processing and disbursement of funds, access to assistance information, and records-keeping. Coordination of these activities greatly improves the efficiency of the delivery of disaster assistance, reduces the risk of abuse, and provides the opportunity to link resources to further maximize their benefits."],[20681,"[[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]130 LIBERTY[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] BUILDING¿ [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]130 LIBERTY[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] BUILDING DECONSTRUCTION During the quarter ending June 30, 2007 the following occurred: Abatement work on floors 30-31 and construction of deconstruction units on floors 22 and 23 was completed during the week ending April 6. During the same week the PHR search of the 5th floor was completed. During the week ending April 14, vestibule abatement on floor 32 was completed. During the week ending April 21, demolition of the roof was completed. Abatement of vestibules on floors 30-31 and façade removal of floors 32-34 was completed the week ending May 5. Abatement work on floors 28-29 and the PHR search of the 4th floor was completed the week ending April 28. During the week ending May 12, façade removal of floors 30-31 and the demolition of the 38th and 37th floor slabs was completed. During the week ending May 26 façade removal was completed for floors 28-31 and the PHR search of the 1st and 2nd floors was completed. Abatement on floors 26-27 was completed during the week ending June 2. Demolition of the 36th floor slab, the creation of a plywood enclosure of floors 28-35 and the abatement on floors 26 and 27 was completed during the week of June 9. Demolition of the 35th floor slab and abatement of deconstruction units on floors 26 and 27 was completed during the week ending June 16. During the week ending June 23 demolition of the 34th floor was completed. During the week ending June 30, demolition of the 33rd floor, façade removal on the 26th and 27th floors and abatement on the 24th and 25th floors was completed. -- PUBLIC OUTREACH AND PARTICIPATION-- PUBLIC OUTREACH AND PARTICIPATION--LMDC continues its efforts to keep area stakeholders and the public at-lar..."],[60564,"LMDC GENERAL ADMINISTRATION --- LMDC maintains a staff and office space to carry out its planning and community development activities, including general management, oversight, monitoring, and coordination. In addition, LMDC provides local officials, businesses, and citizens with information about programs. LMDC conducts additional outreach initiatives on a continuous basis to city, state, and federal elected officials, civic and community organizations, and victims families, participates in Community Board meetings, and meets regularly with community groups, civic organizations, and public officials to ensure that the opinions of those who were affected are taken into account. The LMDC website also solicits public feedback on all of its initiatives, including WTC site planning, transportation, Lower Manhattan neighborhoods, the WTC Site Memorial, and Partial Action Plans."],[61922,"AIRPORT ACCESS ALTERNATIVES ANALYSES: The alternatives and environmental impact analysis is jointly conducted by LMDC, the Port Authority of New York and New Jersey, the Metropolitan Transportation Authority (MTA) and the City of New York. The Port Authority has programmed $60 million for planning and engineering services relating to this effort. The MTA is managing the contract with the consultants who are conducting the ongoing alternatives and environmental impact analysis. More information about this project is available at the Metropolitan Transportation Authority¿s web site at www.mta.info/mta/planning/lmlink. LMDC has amended Partial Action Plan 4 to reduce the amount allocated for this project to the amount already expended to date. In its Final Action Plan, LMDC allocated up to $31,000,000 to address a range of transportation initiatives that will benefit commuters, businesses, residents and pedestrians in Lower Manhattan. Projects expected to be funded include those that improve mobility and improve connectivity between Lower Manhattan, the rest of New York City and the region."],[64234,"Funds returned to HUD in a prior period are reported in this quarter. The return of these funds to HUD was not reported previously because of system limitations."],[65350,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PROGRAM-- Funds provided for capital activities will support new construction of buildings, renovations, expansions into adjoining spaces, equipment purchases, and site acquisitions. LMDC continues to work with selected grant recipients to develop project scopes and to ensure compliance with all necessary and related federal laws and regulations, including compliance with HUD&apos;s environmental review procedures and required public notices. At the end of the second quarter of 2007, there were nineteen executed contracts for capital projects. Newly executed contracts include: National Museum of the American Indian, Church Street School for Music and Art, Open Channels, Dance New Amsterdam, The Judd Foundation, Museum of American Finance, and New York City Police Museum. Dance New Amsterdam has already completed their work to renovate and purchase equipment. The Judd Foundation has already begun work including the hiring of consultants. LMDC has also undertaken a survey to assist in documenting the Cultural Enhancement Fund¿s impacts and benefits as well as to enable LMDC to evaluate progress in meeting the program¿s overall goals. This survey was undertaken in the second quarter of 2007 and a pilot survey was distributed in May. New York Foundation for the Arts has completed its work and we are awaiting their CEF survey before final payment is made. LMDC anticipates sending out surveys to all CEF sub-recipients in subsequent quarters as their projects proceed."],[65391,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PROGRAM-- Funds provided will support educational programs, exhibitions, and developing promotional materials and initiatives. LMDC continues to work with selected grant recipients to further develop project scopes for the programs and events. In November 2006, HUD approved an amended PAP and reduced the Cultural Enhancement Fund by $7,000,000 to $28,000,000. These funds were reallocated to the Final Action Plan for cultural uses. In the second quarter of 2007, several subrecipient provided programs and events benefiting Lower Manhattan. For example, Bang on a Can, a 24-hour continuous music festival, was held in June. The New York Scandia Symphony hosted the last of its three LMDC-funded concerts at Trinity Church in June. The Tribeca Film Festival held its three day drive-in in April. In addition, other grant recipients continue with their planning efforts. The Battery Park City Parks Conservancy (hosting the Harmony on the Hudson family festival) continues its planning for a September event that will include a free festival with food, music and games. Wall Street Rising also continues to plan for their September event, a three day music festival. Project Rebirth continues to record time-lapse footage of the rebuilding of the World Trade Center site as part of a documentary film venture. CultureNOW has developed new brochures and an interactive website and its contract is scheduled to conclude in August. In addition, New York City Opera and the Skyscraper Museum have both completed their work and will now complete their CEF surveys. Work continues on executing contracts with the remaining sub-recipients. LMDC has also undertaken a survey to assist in document the Cultural Enhancement Fund¿s impacts and benefits as well..."],[65420,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PROGRAM-- Funds for planning projects will support cultural organizations seeking new homes in Lower Manhattan, as well as several organizations that are in the early stages of a major expansion or early renovation. The funds are used to assist these organizations with site search, project management, business plan development, and pre-design activities. LMDC continues to work with grant recipients to develop project planning scopes. In the second quarter of 2007, several subrecipients continued work on their CEF grants. Museum of Chinese in the Americas, Architectural League of New York, Artists Space, and City Lore all signed subrecipient agreements during this quarter. Soho Repertory Theatre, through the contract signing process, requested to change their planning grant to a capital grant. This change was approved by the LMDC Board on May 18 and Soho Rep will now move forward with their plan to renovate their lobby, box office and restrooms. Moving Image is moving forward with new offices and its space is under construction. We have also begun payment of invoices associated with Harvestworks in connection with a study on the creation of an electronic and digital art center. Finally, the National Dance Institute has completed its planning process. We continue to work with two additional institutions to execute final contracts. One group that had received a grant, Exhibitions International, is no longer in existence and will not receive a grant from LMDC. LMDC has also undertaken a survey to assist in document the Cultural Enhancement Fund¿s impacts and benefits as well as to enable LMDC to evaluate progress in meeting the program¿s overall goals. This survey was undertaken in the second quarter of 2007 and a pilot surv..."],[66787,"CHINATOWN LOCAL DEVELOPMENT CORPORATION (LDC)--Funding for this program will be used to spearhead community improvements in Chinatown and engage in a combination of short-term projects and long-term planning. CHINATOWN CLEEN STREETS PROGRAM--The Chinatown Partnership LDC, in conjunction with the New York City Department of Small Business Services (SBS), will implement and oversee a comprehensive, multi-year Clean Streets program. A maintenance subcontractor was selected and Clean Streets workers have been deployed. Power washing and daily sweeping and tidy work has been executed everyday. In June 2007, Chinatown cleaning crew used 20,449 trash bags to collect an estimated 511,225 pounds of litter for removal from Chinatown. They performed power-washing on 159 sidewalk segments and removed graffiti from two storefronts. In addition, they painted 1 fire hydrant, 1 fire alarm box, 20 mailboxes, 16 traffic boxes, and 23 lamp posts. In the second quarter 2007 Lunar Stages, a special event to celebrate the performing arts of Asia, was presented. Hundreds of residents and visitors enjoyed the event. The Chinatown cleaning crew worked the event to keep Columbus Park and the surrounding area free of litter. A community forum was held in April 2007 in which the CPLDC was able to obtain important feedback on the Clean Streets Program from the community. The event was held to educate the community on the work conducted by CPLDC. The CPLDC outreach team has also met with several major property owners in Chinatown to solicit their support for the Clean Streets Program. Their responses have been positive. Chinatown Outreach educates merchants, property owners and residents about the benefits of the Clean Streets Program and solicits their support for a permanent maintenance program..."],[66845,"The planning phase of the project was completed in the quarter ending September 30, 2006. After the subrecipient agreement was finalized on September 12th; HPD announced the creation of the new Chinatown/Lower East Side Acquisition Program and issued an RFQ soliciting non-profit organizations to participate in the program. Three entities have been pre-qualified for participation in the Chinatown Lower East Side Acquisition Program: Asian Americans for Equality (AAFE), Cooper Square Mutual Housing Association in partnership with the Cooper Square Committee, and Settlement Housing Fund Inc. in partnership with Two Bridges Neighborhood Council. To date, HPD has approved one funding request: AAFE has been approved to receive $3.6 million in program funding for the acquisition of 36 units in two buildings on Henry Street. The funds were closed on February 7, 2007 for this project. During the second quarter of 2007 HPD began reviewing three new building proposals from AFFE and is working towards commitments on two of the three projects that were identified. HPD received the State Historic Preservation Office¿s approval and Environmental approval from LMDC for [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]112 Eldridge Street[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] and [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]58-62 Hester Street[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] this quarter. The [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]58-62 Hester Street[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] project was closed on May 18, 2007. HPD planned to close on [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]112 Eldridge Street[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] in June 2007 but the transaction will occur on July 27, 2007. LMDC Funds will be directly used for acquisition at the closing with the private lender, Cathay Bank. Asian Americans for Equality have indicated that they are working on submitting a proposal for another project at [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]28 Forsythe Street[...[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]"],[66896,"EXPLORE CHINATOWN CAMPAIGN--As of the quarter ending June 30, 2006, the Explore Chinatown Campaign was transitioned to the Chinatown Partnership Local Development Corporation (LDC). A new outline for the ExploreChinatown.com site redesign was developed for the future of the site which includes an outline for adding multi media content to the site. CPLDC is awaiting final invoice payment from LMDC."],[66350,"COLUMBUS PARK PAVILION -- In the second quarter of 2007, gas lines, gas meter and boiler work have all been completed with Use Inspections held in June. Final Inspections and completion of the Plumbing and Electrical minor punch list work will occur after gas service is turned on by Con Edison. The general contractor completed placing the final finish on the floors this quarter and a Final Inspection was held in June. The Pavilion is now open for public use."],[77226,"In November 2006 HUD approved an amendment to the PAP reducing the Drawing Center¿s allocation to $2,000,000, and reallocating $8,000,000 to the Final Action Plan for cultural and community uses, with the Drawing Center preserved as an eligible recipient. A contract for $2,000,000 is in progress between the LMDC and The Drawing Center. In in the second quarter of 2007 LMDC continued to work with the Drawing Center to complete and execute the subrecipient agreement."],[102448,"LOWER MANHATTAN ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT PROGRAM -- LMDC and the CIty of New York continue to work on project planning and administration, project development, and implementation through the executed subrecipient agreement."],[102527,"The LMDC signed a Subrecipient Agreement with the New York City Department of Education in December 2006. LMDC has continued to consult with the School Construction Authority to identify possible projects, although no selections have been made."],[103486,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT ACCESS-- In the second quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with a project team consisting of the NYC Departments of Parks and Recreation, City Planning and Transportation, New York City Hall, environmental consultant AKRF, and environmental legal counsel Carter Ledyard & Milburne, LLP. During this quarter, the City finalized the project scopes for each project area. The lead agency letters were distributed and AKRF continued work on the archaeological reports for each project section as well as the Environmental Assessment for Peck Slip. The team also consulted with the State Historic Preservation Office (SHPO) and the City pursuant to Section 106 of the National Historic Preservation Act regarding designs, archaeology, and the effect determination."],[103826,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT ESPLANADE AND PIERS--During the second quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with a project team consisting of the NYC Departments of City Planning and Transportation, the NYC Economic Development Corporation (EDC), New York City Hall, environmental consultant AKRF, and environmental legal counsel Carter Ledyard & Milburne, LLP. The first draft of the Final Environmental Impact Statement (FEIS) was distributed to the team for review and comment in early April 2007. Several rounds of revisions were completed and the FEIS was distributed to the LMDC Board on May 11, 2007. The FEIS, proposed Programmatic Agreement (between LMDC, SHPO, and ACHP), and the Draft Air Conformity Determination were adopted by the LMDC on May 18, 2007. These materials were noticed in the local newspaper and circulated to the FEIS distribution list. The public comment period for the FEIS began on June 1, 2007."],[104122,"EAST SIDE K-8 SCHOOL¿ The environmental review for this project is complete and HUD has approved the necessary funding. Core and shell construction has started for the mixed-use building. During the second quarter of 2007, LMDC continued to work with the School Construction Authority to finalize the Subrecipient Agreement, which requires a revised budget and construction timeline."],[104330,"During the second quarter of 2007 LMDC continued to meet with the project team to develop the project description and draft the design guidelines and incentive program."],[104372,"During the second quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with the Department of Parks and Recreation and the project team in the environmental review process to include the completion of Phase 1A documentary studies for Pearl Street Playground, Titanic Park, DeLury Square, and Street Improvements."],[104414,"During the second quarter of 2007, LMDC continued to meet with its team of project consultants to start the environmental review process, including the environmental assessment examining the storefront and façade incentives program, open space improvements and the acquisition of a parcel of private property."],[104451,"During the 4th quarter of 2006, HUD approved an amendment to PAP 6 to eliminate the allocation of $8,000,000 for the Fulton Corridor Residential Rehabilitation and Conversion Program."],[104462,"During the second quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with EDC¿s project team in the environmental review process to include the completion of Phase 1A documentary studies for streetscape and street improvements."],[104686,"FITERMAN HALL RECONSTRUCTION-- LMDC is working with the City University of New York and the Dormitory Authority of the State of New York to finalize necessary agreements in order to provide funds for this project."],[110038,"HISTORY AND HERITAGE IN DOWNTOWN NYC/Museums of Lower Manhattan (MOLM)-- On June 14, 2003, HUD approved the allocation of $4.664 million for History and Heritage in Downtown NYC. History and Heritage in Downtown NYC (HHD), now called the Museums of Lower Manhattan campaign, is a joint initiative of various Lower Manhattan museums whose mission is history and heritage. HHD is a marketing initiative that will promote downtown as a cultural destination. HHD has provided for the creation of various marketing and advertising materials, a website www.MuseumsOfLowerManhattan.org, and other activities that promote Lower Manhattan Culture. Participating museums include: Castle Clinton National Monument; the Eldridge Street Project, Inc.; Federal Hall National Memorial; Fraunces Tavern Museum; Lower East Side Tenement Museum; Museum of Chinese in the Americas; Museum of American Financial History; Museum of Jewish Heritage- A Living Memorial to the Holocaust; Smithsonian National Museum of the American Indian; New York City Fire Museum; New York City Police Museum; South Street Seaport Museum; the Skyscraper Museum; the Statue of Liberty National Monument and Ellis Island Immigration Museum; and the Unwavering Spirit Exhibit, St. Paul¿s Chapel. In the second quarter of 2007 LMDC continued its ambitious marketing of the Museums of Lower Manhattan. CTM distributed 48,300 Museum of Lower Manhattan maps/brochures. Bandujo, Donker and Brothers continued advertisement placements in various tourist publications, local newspapers, magazines and on internet websites. A total of 27 Museums of Lower Manhattan print ads were placed during the quarter."],[111900,"REHABILITATION/RECONSTRUCTION OF A PUBLIC IMPROVEMENT --- --HUDSON RIVER PARK SEGMENT 3--On February 28, 2006, HUD approved Partial Action Plan 10, which allocated up to $70 million for the completion of Hudson River Park Segment 3 in Tribeca. Within this area would be two public recreational piers (Piers 25 and 26), an ecological pier, and an adjacent upland park. Structural work such as pile removal and preparation for pile driving has begun on Pier 25. Work in the North Upland portion of the project including site engineering , site logistics planning, and excavation for the underground drainage structures occurred this quarter. On Pier 26 the removal of existing piles and preparations for pile driving occurred. Engineering work, equipment mobilization, and pile removals was performed for work pertaining to the Platform between Piers 25 & 26."],[112276,"The New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development (HPD) is continuing the process of identifying an affordable housing project that is consistent with the General Project Criteria (as defined above). HPD is currently evaluating potential projects for which to allocate and administer funds for an affordable housing program. As HPD is responsible for implementing the NYC¿s New Housing Marketplace Plan, HPD is evaluating how the allocation can best be used towards the goal of building and preserving 165,000 units of affordable housing."],[120402,"This activity is complete."],[123916,"The Job Creation and Retention Program (JCRP) provides discretionary incentives to companies making commitments to attract, create and/or retain at least 200 jobs in Lower Manhattan. The program will run though 2007. JCRP is managed by ESD in cooperation with NYC EDC. JCRP assistance is in the form of job-based grants for companies making a minimum 7 year commitment to Lower Manhattan. The amount of assistance is based on several criteria including the number of jobs, economic impact, the creation of new employment in Lower Manhattan and the degree of risk of employment leaving Lower Manhattan. As of June 30, 2007, ESD has offered incentive grants to 124 companies (excluding those companies whose projects were terminated after an offer was issued). Seventy-six companies have accepted grant offers worth $293.11 million to date. Of those accepted offers, 73 active projects have received ESD¿s Board approval, representing a commitment of 73,943 created and retained jobs in Lower Manhattan and an additional 15,649 jobs retained elsewhere in New York City. The value of the ESD Board approved grants is $290.9 million. ESD has disbursed all of its original $170 million allocation for the program and has been drawing on LMDC¿s $150 million sub-allocation since August 2003. However, there is a balance of $114,810 in the $170 million allocation due to ESD¿s recapture of grant funds from BPC/Embassy Suites (due to BPC¿s sale of the business). Of the original $150,000,000 LMDC sub-allocation, ESD has disbursed $98,243,447 to date."],[124128,"LMDC continues to work with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development to plan and develop the scope of the rehabilitation for the development. It is anticipated that this scope planning phase will be completed in the following quarter and a subrecipient agreement finalized in the near term. Once the subrecipient agreement is finalized progress will ensue."],[125469,"LOWER MANHATTAN COMMUNICATIONS OUTREACH CAMPAIGN ---The campaign¿s three components include: the creation of informational kiosks at strategic locations downtown that will double as visitor centers; the development of branded marketing materials; and the distribution of LMDC printed materials and electronic updates. More than half of the campaign¿s funds have been allocated for the creation, design, construction, and maintenance of the two informational kiosks; the Downtown Rebuilding Information Kiosk which is located at the new World Trade Center Path Station and the Chinatown Kiosk which is located between Canal, Baxter, and Walker Streets. The campaign also allocated funds for staffing the WTC Path Station kiosk for two years. In the second quarter of 2007 the WTC Kiosk had 234,240 visitors and the Chinatown Kiosk served 84,276visitors. Both kiosks are stocked with current information about Lower Manhattan and the rebuilding efforts."],[126791,"LOWER MANHATTAN FERRY SERVICE PROGRAM-- On May 1, 2007, ferry services began between the City of Yonkers, the World Financial Center (on the Hudson River) and Pier 11 (on the East River). In the first month 1,743 riders used the ferry. In the second quarter, LMDC, Port Authority, New York State Department of Transportation, Rockland County, City of Yonkers, and New York Water Taxi also planned for the initiation of new ferry service between Haverstraw and Lower Manhattan. This service is expected to start in September 2007."],[126815,"LOWER MANHATTAN PUBLIC INFORMATION PROGRAM -- The Lower Manhattan Public Information Plan Program (Program) is a public awareness campaign which aims to facilitate the area¿s recovery by providing a trusted source of information. It is intended to enhance the coordinated efforts of the City of New York, New York State, and federal agencies throughout the long-term process of rebuilding and redevelopment. The Program consists of a website, a newsletter, and informational construction signage that focuses on Lower Manhattan and delivers the most accurate and timely information about the area¿s rebuilding progress to the area stakeholders including residents, businesses, and visitors. This is accomplished through marketing, advertising, stakeholder outreach, and strategic planning. The centerpiece of the Program is a comprehensive website, www.LowerManhattan.info, which offers regularly updated news and information for area residents, workers, and visitors. -- October 2005 was the final month for LMDC¿s hosting of the LowerManhattan.info program. As of November 2005 the Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center is in charge of operating and the site. The Program continues to move forward with oversight and funding from other sources. The Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center is now in charge of monitoring the site."],[126837,"LOWER MANHATTAN STREET MANAGEMENT PROGRAM¿ In the quarter ending June 30, 2007, work progressed on the following tasks: - Completion of a schedule-facilitated traffic & network simulation tool that will help the Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center (LMCCC) understand the traffic impacts of construction and assist with their decision-making process. - Merging of two traffic models into one traffic model and the incorporation of the Battery Park City area into the model. (At the end of the first phase of this project, NYC DOT had two separate traffic models that covered the east and western portions of Lower Manhattan.) - Coordination efforts with the LMCCC. - Bus Study to determine the existing supply and demand of Lower Manhattan bus parking, as well as the volumes and staging locations of buses entering the study area. - Analysis of ¿placard parking¿ in the various districts of Lower Manhattan, in order to assess the impact of these patterns on the general user."],[125161,"TRIBUTE IN LIGHT-- The Tribute in Light of September 11, 2006 was successfully and momentously displayed from the Battery Park Tunnel Garage, where it will remain housed through 2008. The 2007 Tribute in Light display is currently in the planning phase."],[129388,"MASARYK TOWER REHABILITATION-- LMDC continues to work with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development to plan and develop the scope of the rehabilitation for the development. It is anticipated that this scope planning phase will be completed in the following quarter. Once a subrecipient agreement is finalized, progress will ensue."],[134417,"During the second quarter of 2007, the City of New York (with the input of stakeholders) evaluated and planned various improvements including: anchoring NoGos (polygonal sidewalk barriers) to the sidewalk, a turntable for vehicle security, new street furnishings, light poles, repaneling of security booths, pedestrian interdiction near New York Stock Exchange, Eurocobble sidewalk treatment, and replacement of the fence in front of the Stock Exchange. Various issues are being addressed including access to buildings, maintenance, security, and the potential for expanded space to accommodate special events."],[135870,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT PLANNING--During the second quarter of 2007, additional work continued regarding the wrap-up of payment to consultants and individual reporting of work. Consultants remained available for inquiry regarding specific details of the Foundation Plan document. The East River Waterfront Planning Study has been completed and led to a second phase of work for the East River Esplanade/Piers and East River Access (see reports ERA-0240 and EREP-0240)."],[135815,"In the quarter ending June 30, 2007, LMDC developed surveys that will assist in documenting the Cultural Enhancement Fund (CEF) Program¿s economic impacts as well as enable LMDC to evaluate progress in meeting the program¿s overall goals. Survey questions relate to leveraging funding, generating economic benefits, promoting access to the arts, creating a vibrant community. LMDC sent the survey to a select group of CEF recipients for pilot testing and to begin collecting data. See Activity Progress Narrative for the CEF program."],[135921,"LMDC PLANNING--ENVIRONMENTAL --- LMDC sought the advice and counsel of law firms and environmental consulting firms on retainer on environmental and land use requirements and matters relating to LMDC¿s planning and development initiatives in Lower Manhattan. Historically, such projects included early phases of (1) the World Trade Center Memorial and Redevelopment Plan (WTC Plan), (2) the East River Waterfront Esplanade and Piers Project, (3) cultural enhancement and community improvement projects, and (4) public information, outreach, and participation efforts relating to the foregoing activities. During the quarter LMDC considered potential environmental review requirements for future projects and programs as well as possible adjustments to approved projects."],[135960,"LMDC continues to work with the NYC Department of Design and Construction regarding improvements to Louise Nevelson Plaza. This project will be implemented through LMDC¿s NEIGHBORHOOD PARKS AND OPEN SPACES program and a subrecipient agreement with the New York City Department of Design and Construction. LMDC continues to evaluate further options for implementation of projects related to its Lower Manhattan planning efforts including those related to transportation, traffic, housing, open space, retail development, and economic development."],[135995,"WEST STREET PEDESTRIAN CONNECTIONS-- All planning for West Street has been completed. In 2006, the New York State Department of Transportation opened the West Street Promenade South, a tree-lined pedestrian walkway extending along both sides of Route 9A (West Street) from West Thames Street to Battery Place in Lower Manhattan. Roadway and sidewalk improvements provide increased pedestrian space by adding recreational areas and lush greenery to the heavily trafficked roadway and complete a substantial portion of the Hudson River Park. Promenade South is the first phase of a two-phase State Department of Transportation (NYSDOT) project designed to repair damage sustained during the terrorist attacks on the World Trade Center on September 11, 2001 and provide an appropriately dignified and aesthetically graceful setting for the World Trade Center Memorial planned nearby. When both phases are complete, the full promenade project will improve pedestrian and vehicular circulation at Route 9A between Chambers Street and Battery Place in Lower Manhattan. The project will strengthen the connection among the future World Trade Center Memorial on the east side of Route 9A, the World Financial Center and Battery Park City on the west side of the roadway, and historic Battery Park, embarkation point for the Statue of Liberty and Ellis Island at the southern tip of Manhattan."],[136039,"WORLD TRADE CENTER PLANNING: World Trade Center planning activities are now performed largely as part of the further study, development, funding, and implementation of the World Trade Center Memorial and Memorial Museum as well as the World Trade Center Performing Arts Center which are components of LMDC¿s General Project Plan for the World Trade Center Memorial and Cultural Program, as amended February 14, 2007."],[136193,"Parks Phase 1 - As of June 30, 2007 all thirteen park sites are substantially completed. The remaining site to be completed, the Wall Street Park fountain, has continued to have glass fabrication problems with three fountain pieces yet to be successfully fabricated. It was anticipated that the fountain will be completed in Spring 2007. Parks Phase 2 - The second phase of parks improvements continued to the environmental review process and all projects involved have started design this quarter, except Allen and Pike Street Malls. The projects which started design include: Washington Market Park Comfort Station, Collect Pond Park, Sara D. Roosevelt Phase 2 and James Madison Park. Canal Varick Laight Park has completed design but has not yet been transmitted for bidding and construction. LMDC entered into a subrecipient agreement with the NYC Department of Design and Construction to improve Louise Nevelson Plaza. The construction work will be a component of the street reconstruction of Liberty Street, Maiden Lane, and Pearl Street. Enhancements to Louise Nevelson Plaza include upgrades to lighting, accessibility, and aesthetics while highlighting the Louise Nevelson sculptures."],[169138,"RESIDENTIAL GRANT PROGRAM (RGP) -- As of June 30, 2007, 39,805 applications have been approved. Of the 39,805 approved applications representing approximately $227,164,110.95 million in grants, LMDC distributed 30,923 September 11th Residents Grants, 26,713 2-Year Grants, and 8,123 Family Grants to individuals. LMDC also distributed 1,465 2-Year Grants to 79 businesses. The RGP program officially ended on August, 31, 2005. LMDC continues to implement all necessary program close-out procedures, including review of all appeals received, as well as perform any necessary recoupment procedures. During the third quarter 2006, LMDC amended Partial Action Plan 1 to reduce the $280,500,000 allocation for the RGP by $43,000,000 to reflect the final amount disbursed under the Residential Grant Program and a reserve."],[171004,"SMALL FIRM ATTRACTION AND RETENTION GRANT (SFARG) -- To date, ESD and LMDC have made 3,877 grants to 2,634 businesses for over $126 million. Fourteen of the grants that were made were distributed in the second quarter of 2007."],[171230,"On November 18, 2005, HUD approved Partial Action Plan 6, which allocated funds for the planning and design, of a mixed income residential rental building at [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in Tribeca. The Subrecipient Agreement was executed on December 5, 2006. The project broke ground in December of 2005, and the mixed-income rental component was 68.5% complete based on a site observation report dated May 18, 2007. Work is proceeding in general accordance with the plan and specifications. Professional services including architectural, engineering, legal, environmental, and other professional services necessary for the planning and design of the Mixed Income Rental Building continue to be rendered to secure project completion, including oversight of compliance with plans, specifications, government approvals, and financing requirements as the project continues to progress as described above. Financing transactions for the Mixed Income Rental Building have been secured, and interest payments for the construction loan are ongoing on a monthly basis. All necessary insurance for the Mixed Income Rental Building have been secured, and payments are ongoing on a monthly basis. The Developer continues to coordinate professional services, financing, insurance, and other activities in accordance with the Program Guidelines."],[172399,"SOUTHERN SITE--The World Trade Center Memorial and Redevelopment Plan (WTC Plan) includes several parcels of land just south of the World Trade Center Site, referred to collectively as the ¿Southern Site,¿ that will be used to construct infrastructure and other components of the WTC Plan. LMDC acquired the largest parcel at [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in August 2004, [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in February 2006, and the following parcels constitute the remainder: (1) privately-owned [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS]; and (2) several public New York City streets-Washington Street between Cedar and Liberty Streets and subsurface portions of Cedar and Liberty Streets. LMDC has been working with the The City of New York and The Port Authority of New York and New Jersey on the acquisition and configuration of the street parcels as well as the review and development of plans for the entire Southern Site, including the redesign and relocation of St. Nicholas Church. This quarter, the Southern Site was expanded slightly to include additional street parcels and added to the project site for the WTC Plan along with certain parcels to the north of the WTC Site, as described in the General Project Plan for the World Trade Center Memorial and Cultural Program, as amended February 17, 2007. LMDC held a public hearing on May 17, 2007 regarding LMDC¿s acquisition of certain parcels necessary to construct infrastructure on the Southern Site."],[172916,"TRIBECA FILM FESTIVAL (Film Festival)-- The Film Festival was the first major event to be held in Lower Manhattan after the September 11th attacks. The Film Festival has been responsible for attracting hundred-of-thousands of visitors and generating millions of dollars in revenue for Lower Manhattan. As of March 31, 2006, funds drawn during the quarter are related to activities for the Film Festival 2005. All activities and payments related to the 2004 and 2005 Film Festival are complete. Going forward all activities related to the Film Festival will be funded through the Cultural Enhancement Grant allocations."],[172951,"LOWER MANHATTAN TRANSPORTATION IMPROVEMENT PROGRAM -- LMDC and the City of New York have executed a subrecipient agreement that defined the three phases of the project: project planning and administration, project development, and implementation."],[173175,"TRIBUTE VISITORS CENTER-- The Tribute Center opened adjacent to the World Trade Center site in September 2006. It offers an interactive learning experience that focuses on providing visitor orientation and factual information about the terrorist attacks of February 26, 1993 and September 11, 2001. The Tribute Center focuses on telling the human stories of those affected through its exhibits, programs and docent-led tours of the site. Tribute is expected to be open for a minimum of 5 years, effectively bridging the gap between the present day and the completion of the future WTC Memorial, Reflecting Absence.-- The docent tour program officially began on November 19, 2005, with one tour per weekday and two tours per weekend day. The program increased to two weekday tours and four weekend tours in February 2006. To date, over 100 docents from the 9/11 community (victims&apos; families, survivors, rescue workers, residents, and others) have been trained to provide tours. The Center houses 4,000 square feet of gallery space on the first floor and 2,000 square feet of gallery and program space on the lower level, including a space for community gatherings. Galleries include: ¿World Trade Center Community Remembered,¿ celebrating the World Trade Center before September 11th; ¿Passage Through Time,¿ telling the personal stories of those who experienced September 11th first hand; ¿Aftermath and Recovery,¿ where a moving film shows the mammoth task and tremendous will of the rescue and recovery workers; ¿Tribute,¿ showing images and lovingly selected objects given by families to share their loved ones with the world; and ¿Voices of Promise,¿ where the vast international response to September 11th is documented. All activities have been completed for this project. In the second..."],[173331,"During the second quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with the City of New York to prepare preliminary designs and coordinate the planning of below grade infrastructure. Regarding planning in the quarter, LMDC continued to participate in the further development and review of the General Project Plan for the World Trade Center Memorial and Cultural Program, as amended February 14, 2007."],[173385,"WTC MEMORIAL AND MEMORIAL MUSEUM -- LMDC, in partnership with the World Trade Center Memorial Foundation (WTCMF), will provide initial funding for the design and construction of the Memorial, and will plan for and oversee the design development and construction documentation for the Memorial Museum, and possibly other elements of the WTC Plan. Highlights of the quarter ending June 2007, include - Various structural engineering options to support and expose the existing West Slurry Wall were studies - Memorial Construction Documents were delivered. - Names parapet mock-up was built at the Brooklyn Navy Yard. - Trees for the Memorial area have been purchased and transported (from NY, PA, DC) to a holding site in New Jersey. - Thinc Design selected as lead exhibition designers for the Memorial Museum. - Contracts awarded for structural steel to Owen Steel Company - Elevator, escalator, and ejector pits were poured (remaining pits will be poured over the next few months. - Footings were poured (82 footings out of 150 have been poured thus far) - Owner Controlled Insurance Program went into effect. - Construction Manager/ General Contractor participated in the Minority/Women Owned Business Enterprises (MWBE) conferences and hosted a Business Opportunity Forum Regarding planning in the quarter, LMDC continued to participate in the further development and review of the General Project Plan for the World Trade Center Memorial and Cultural Program, as amended February 14, 2007. See Activity Progress Narrative for the related W-MUS-0171 (Museum Pavilion)."],[173436,"The WTC Memorial Foundation continued its work on the programmatic development of the combined Museum Pavilion and the Memorial Museum, including development of a schematic outline delineating the programmatic uses of the Museum¿s public spaces and the continued research and acquisition of curatorial resources. The Museum Pavilion design team continues to coordinate with the Port Authority World Trade Center Transportation Hub design team and the Memorial design team on below grade spaces, structural and mechanical issues. In the second quarter of 2007, Snohetta completed 50% Design Development Drawings. Snohetta continues to work on the Design Development Documents which are scheduled to be completed in the third quarter. Regarding planning in the quarter, LMDC continued to participate in the further development and review of the General Project Plan for the World Trade Center Memorial and Cultural Program, as amended February 14, 2007. See Activity Progress Narrative for the related W-MEM-0171."],[173520,"WEST STREET PEDESTRIAN CONNECTIONS¿ All work has been completed. Construction of the Vesey Bridge was completed in stages, with the opening in time for the temporary PATH station in November 2003. This was followed by the completion of the Bridge Extension in June 2004, and final delivery of the elevators in November 2004."],[64617,"CATEGORY ONE--EMERGENCY AND TEMPORARY SERVICE RESPONSE--The Utility Restoration and Infrastructure Rebuilding Program¿s Partial Action Plan received the United States Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) approval on September 15, 2003. This Plan details the proposed expenditure of $750 million to restore, rebuild, and revitalize utility infrastructure in Lower Manhattan. Empire State Development Corporation (ESDC), in consultation with the Lower Manhattan Development Corporation and in cooperation with the New York City Economic Development Corporation, is administering the Plan. The plan details include funding the following activities: (1) Category One- Emergency and Temporary Service Response; (2) Category Two- Permanent Restoration and Infrastructure Improvements; (3) Category Three- Service Interference; (4) Category Four- Construction of Carrier Neutral Lateral Conduits; Category Five Construction of Redundant Fiber Connections; (6)Category Six- Mandated Infrastructure Improvements. In September 2006, LMDC issued proposed changes to Partial Action Plan S-2, and during the fourth quarter of 2006, LMDC reviewed and responded to public comments to such changes. As of March 31, 2007, field audit work has been concluded for AT&T Corporation, Verizon Communications, Inc., Consolidated Edison, Inc. and AT&T Wireless Services Incorporated. Verizon Communications, Inc. has appealed some of the findings included in the final audit report. Requests for Category One totaled $403,674,125. Disbursements to date total $160,069,892, leaving a balance of $89,930,108 from the $250 million allocated for Category One. The following represents the number of Lower Manhattan businesses and residential customers that will be served by the utilities receiving Category One..."],[64647,"CATEGORY TWO--PERMANENT RESTORATION AND INFRASTRUCTURE IMPROVEMENTS--- The Utility Restoration and Infrastructure Rebuilding Program¿s Partial Action Plan received the United States Department of Housing and Urban Development (HUD) approval on September 15, 2003. This Plan details the proposed expenditure of $750 million to restore, rebuild, and revitalize utility infrastructure of Lower Manhattan. Empire State Development Corporation (ESDC) in consultation with the Lower Manhattan Development Corporation (LMDC) and with the cooperation of the New York City Economic Development Corporation (EDC) is administering the Plan. Plan details include funding the following activities: (1) Category One - Emergency and Temporary Service Response; (2) Category Two - Permanent Restoration and Infrastructure Improvements; (3) Category Three - Service Interference; (4) Category Four - Construction of Carrier Neutral Lateral Conduits; (5) Category Five - Construction of Redundant Fiber Connections; (6) Category Six- Mandated Infrastructure Improvements. As outlined in Partial Action Plan S-2, Category Two pertains to permanent restoration and infrastructure improvements. As of April 27, 2006 Category Two has been divided into two separate sub-categories for funding, 1) Permanent Restoration and Infrastructure Improvements and 2) Southern Site. Category Two Southern Site qualifying expenditures are eligible for reimbursement according to the payment schedule as outlined in the Category Two Southern Site Guidelines. Category Two requests to date total $421,042,565 (Permanent Restoration $383,614,301 + Southern Site 37,428,264). Disbursements to date total $109,357,751, (Permanent Restoration $108,068,053 + Southern Site $1,289,698), leaving a balance of $220,642,249 of a $330 million..."],[136107,"PLANNING AND ADMINSTRATION--¿- UTILITIES RESTORATION AND INFRASTRUCTURE REBUILDING (URIR) PROGRAM.-- To date, program administration funds have been used for activities related to the implementation of Categories One and Two of the URIR Program. Such activities include general management, oversight, and coordination. These activities also include program publication, review of proposed infrastructure investments, environmental review clearance, financial evaluation and processing of funding reimbursement requests, and auditing of funds that have been reimbursed for Category One and Category Two.--Program administration funds have also been used to fund feasibility studies. -- AKRF Inc and The Louis Berger Group continue to provide environmental consulting services. AKRF is currently working on AT&T¿s Category Two application. The Louis Berger group has been assigned the Verizon Category Two application and work is ongoing. --KPMG is providing IT consulting services including testing and auditing the integrity and security of the electronic systems of the applicants. ¿ MOSAICS- LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the NYC Department of Transportation (DOT) in the third quarter of 2006. The DOT will conduct an initial phase of planning and design for certain upgrades to the MOSAICS system. This will include, but not be limited to, a review and analysis of current DOT and MOSAICS processes and a technical feasibility analysis and review of MOSAICS system architecture. In September 2006, LMDC issued proposed changes to Partial Action Plan S-2, and during the fourth quarter of 2006, LMDC continued to review comments."],[102375,"HUD approved the Bridge Loan Program May 9, 2006, as a part of the State&apos;s Initial Action Plan. On February 10, 2006, a Cooperative Endeavor was entered into between the Louisiana Public Facilities Authority (LPFA), Louisiana Economic Development (LED), and the Division of Administration (DOA), which obligated $30,000,000 in State CDBG funds to be utilized in the Extended Bridge Loan program. That agreement was amended, on June 20, 2006, to include $10,000,000 to cover the cost of the Emergency Bridge Loan program that was originally funded by LED, as well as $55,000,000 to be utilized in the Enhanced Bridge Loan Program. Prior to the Enhanced Loan program being implemented, a change in geographical location of beneficiaries was requested through the submittal of a substantial amendment that was approved by HUD on September 15, 2006. This amendment revised the allocation language from a 50% allocation to the thirteen most impacted parishes, to a minimum of 50% will be allocated to the thirteen most impacted parishes, and up to 50% allocated to the other twenty-four impacted parishes. This change transpired due to the demand for loan guarantees amounting to $8,820,765 in the most impacted areas. Also, during the previous quarter, the Extended BL made a total of 325 loan guarantees totaling $28,696,080 in a 37 parish area. An estimated 3,872 jobs were reported as being created or retained as a result of the first two Bridge Loan Programs. The Enhanced BL program deadline was December 29, 2006. There were a total of 57 loans guaranteed, for a principal sum of $5,409,427 for this BL program. The estimated number of jobs created and retained will be 304. The total amount paid this quarter for Bridge Loan principal is $472,525.78 and $9,084.84 in interest. The total a..."],[102510,"HUD approved the release of funds for the Louisiana Tourism Marketing Program; outlined in Action Plan One, Amendment 2; on September 15, 2006, to provide resources to conduct a national campaign and other initiatives designed to bring out-of-state travelers back to the New Orleans region, Southeast and Southwest Louisiana. The program has awarded $28,500,000 to 17 tourism agencies throughout the disaster affected parishes. The interagency agreement with the Department of Culture, Recreation, & Tourism (DCR&T) is complete and all the sub-recipient agreements have been signed. A funds distribution process was established between DCR&T and OCD-DRU for creating internal controls to insure a reasonable turn-around in DCR&T&apos;s request for payments. Tourism has spent funds on disaster marketing and of the 17 sub-recipients only 3 have not began submitting request for reimbursement. OCD-DRU staff has made pre-monitoring visits to the New Orleans area and one pre-monitoring visit to the Lafayette area. In June of 2007, the OCD-DRU staff continued the pre-monitoring visits. There is now visible evidence of DCR&T&apos;s marketing and public relations efforts in advertising campaigns with Patricia Clarkson, John Goodman and Tim McGraw. There have been many public relations stories related to the changes in the New Orleans area, positive media stories and advertising coverage in magazines such as Costal Living, National Geographic Traveler and Time Magazine."],[102552,"HUD provided the waiver for eligible activities that would allow CDBG funding to be used for research activities in higher education in March. The Louisiana Board of Regents (LBR) received applications from the eligible applicants, institutions of higher education in southeastern Louisiana, on April 26. On June 23 a selection panel of experts from around the nation completed a review of the proposals, including in person interviews, and developed a list of recommendations. On June 28, the LBR approved the recommendation of 11 awards for a total of $27,605,424. Contract negotiations between the Board of Regents and the OCD-DRU are in their final stages with a signed contract expected by the end of July."],[104025,"HUD approved the release of funds for the Recovery Workforce Training Program; outlined in Action Plan One, Amendment 2; on September 15, 2006, to provide grants to workforce providers to implement sector based work force training programs to train and place workers into jobs within businesses involved in the six identified recovery sectors. Of the $38 million awarded 54% will serve the Katrina affected areas, 16% benefit the Rita area and 30% covers both regions. The RFP issued on October 4, 2006 required proposals to be turned in to the Workforce Commission (WC) on or before November 28, 2006. The WC created an evaluation team to review the submitted 48 proposals amounting to $165 million. The final evaluation of the proposals was due to the WC by December 7 for final approval by December 14, 2006. Of the 48 proposals submitted 19 were chosen to be funded. In March of 2007, an agreement between WC and OCD-DRU was signed and submitted to the Office of Contractual Review (OCR). Meeting between WC Acting Director and OCD-DRU staff resulted in the completion of a template for sub-recipient agreements. As of June, 2007, all completed agreements submitted by the WC in the previous month have passed contractual review and all agreements are now with the OCD-DRU whose staff is in the process of creating a system of tracking and utilizing the information contained in the agreements to help with future monitoring efforts. One of the programs under development by the WC is HeaRTS, which stands for Healthcare Recovery Training System to train and find positions for nearly 1000 people in the healthcare sector. It will be starting sometime in July of 2007."],[104147,"The Small Firm Recovery Loan and Grant (SFLG) program was approved in Action Plan One, Amendment 2 on September 15, 2006. The Solicitation for Grant Application process was initiated in October 2006 and due December 1. Just prior to the SGA due date, the State decided to change the parameters of the program and augment it with additional funds beyond $38 million allocated in Amendment 2. An additional $105 million was transferred to the program in Amendment 8, approved on February 9, 2007. In the first round of grant reviews, 5,553 applications for this funding were received and 3,469 (62%) grants were awarded to small firms. The total amount of funding was $62.7 million with the average amount being $18,100. The funds were distributed across the state as follows: 8% - Central LA, 12% - Southwest LA, and 80% Southeast LA. Minority and/or woman owned businesses accounted for 53% of the funds. Approximately one third of the businesses assisted were single person firms which explains why 88% of the firms assisted created less than 10 jobs each; leaving 9% creating 11-25 and 3% creating 26-50. The total amount of funding approved was $30,856,618 in amounts of up to $20,000. A second round for this program will be undertaken later in the year, with the potential for additional funding of $38 million in grants and loans. Collaboration with the OCD-DRU, SBA and LED continued in May to ensure non-duplication of benefits to business grantees. This monitoring continues in weekly telephone conferences to ensure that financial responsibility and proper reimbursement processes are in place. Pre-monitoring reviews were conducted for all sub-recipients/lenders by the OCD-DRU staff. The reviews are to identify any problems with grantee files and documentation and to provide LMI trai..."],[104166,"Administrative costs incurred by Louisiana Economic Development related to the Small Firm Loan and Grant Program."],[104205,"See narratvie for Small Loan - LMI (ESLG)"],[104298,"The Small Firm Technical Assistance Program as outlined in Action Plan One Amendment 2 was approved by HUD on September 15, 2006. The Solicitation for Grant Application (SGA) process commenced in October, 2006. In January of 2007, a review board was initiated. In February, 19 sub-recipients were selected. LED met with the 19 in March to go over program content and process. In April, LED established a contract template through collaboration with OCD-DRU. In May, a kick-off meeting was held for the program sub-recipients. The agenda consisted of training on LED&iquest;s Sage system and program training by the OCD-DRU staff. Contracts with sub-recipients are still being negotiated with LED at this time. Some recipients attended basic CDBG training provided by the OCD-DRU in June."],[107406,"On June 25, the LRA board passed a resolution increasing the amount of this program by moving $3 million from the Infrastructure programs. During the second quarter of 2007, ICF International held 36,306 appointments with Louisiana homeowners whose homes had been adversely affected by hurricanes Katrina and Rita, for the purpose of discussing and deciding upon the homeowner&apos;s best option with the program. ICF received 30,870 applications for assistance this quarter (20,450 online; 5,242 hardcopy; 5,178 phone in system). HGI and First American Title companies held 28,746 closing, resulting in the release of $2,096,246,267.79 to Louisiana homeowners. According to ICF, 6,717 closings ($555,620,271.28), were held with clients falling in the elderly category."],[107420,"See Road Home-LMI (HAOO) Activity Progress Narrative. HGI and First American Title companies held 28,746 closing, resulting in the release of $2,096,246,267.79 to Louisiana homeowners. According to ICF, 6,717 closings ($555,620,271.28), were held with clients falling in the elderly category."],[108332,"During the second quarter of 2007, the Louisiana Institute for Building Technology and Safety (LA IBTS) continued to deploy personnel to assist with code inspections, plan evaluations, code permitting, and training in the 11 parishes most impacted by Hurricanes Katrina and Rita: Orleans; Jefferson; St. Bernard; St. Tammany; Calcasieu; Plaquemines; Vermillion; Terrebonne; Cameron; Washington; and Iberia. Based on Memorandums of Understanding entered into by the Department of Public Safety (DPS), these personnel are currently deployed in ten of the eleven parishes and in 12 separate cities. Although Iberia Parish has yet to request assistance, one of its municipalities has. This was only a six month contract, but because the parishes and cities are requesting assistance for at least another year, this contract has been extended."],[108355,"See Bldg Code-SB (HBCE) Activity Progress Narrative."],[108410,"HUD approved the release of funds for the Support to Community Based Programs; outlined in Action Plan One, Amendment 1; on May 30, 2006 for work to be done in the following two areas: Housing Outreach Services--To provide outreach and technical assistance to low to moderate income individuals in the Homeowner Housing Assistance Program. This public service program will provide outreach, housing counseling, and transportation services to individuals in communities with low volumes of Road Home applicants. Housing Legal Services--To provide legal assistance to low to moderate income individuals through The Road Home application process. The ACORN Housing Corporation (AHC) contract was approved by the State Office of Contractual Review (OCR) on May 11, 2007. ACORN Housing Corporation has begun to provide services to support and encourage homeowners in need of technical assistance to utilize the Road Home program. The Southeast Louisiana Legal Services (SLLS) contract was approved in June. 2nd Quarter 2007: # of Households Assisted (Face to Face Counseling) LMI - 142; # of Households Assisted (Call Center) LMI - 100;"],[108436,"See Capacity Grant - LMI (HCAP) Activity Progress Narrative. # of Households Assisted (Face to Face Counseling) - 27; # of Households Assisted (Call Center) - 35; Total # of Households Served - 60;"],[109098,"The Housing Development Loan Fund program is combined contractually with the Land Assembly Operations program. In April, OCD-DRU met with representatives of Local Initiatives Support Corporation (LISC) and Enterprise Community Partners Inc. to discuss program implementation details including environmental reviews, acceptance and review of applications and billing/reimbursement/draw-down procedures. In June, three training sessions were held (New Orleans, Baton Rouge, Lake Charles) to provide information to developers on the availability of the fund and the process to apply for funds. Applications can now be accepted. 2nd Quarter 2007: # of loans closed - 0."],[109144,"The Environs contract was signed on May 8, 2007, but had to be revisited when it was determined that Environs could not purchase the needed IT equipment."],[110045,"See the Housing Development Loan Fund program as these two programs are contractually combined."],[110193,"HUD approved the release of funds for the Homelessness Supports and Housing Program; outlined in OCD¿s Action Plan One, Amendment 1; on May 30, 2006, to immediately restore and expand housing capacity in hurricane impacted areas by providing capital funds for repairing damaged homeless housing facilities, providing capital grants and shallow rental subsidies to encourage increasing the supply of permanent supportive housing (PSH) for homeless and at-risk households, providing PSH interim rental assistance to immediately house PSH eligible households and provide short term rental assistance in the form of deposits and other assistance to prevent persons at risk from becoming homeless or to assist households who have already fallen into homelessness, including low wage workers, at risk youth, victims of domestic violence, and low income seniors and people with disabilities to obtain and retain housing. Additionally, the Program will provide funding for the development of a transitional Safe Haven Project and partial funding for a statewide Homeless Management Information System (HMIS). The Department of Social Services (DSS) shall serve as the lead agency. The state entered into an interagency agreement with the Department of Social Services (DSS) effective March 27, 2007. The first priority for use of the $25.9 million in CDBG funds is to restore storm-damaged homeless shelters in the most heavily impacted parishes. A Request for Proposals was advertised in January, 2007. Proposals submitted by March 2, 2007 were evaluated for awards. DSS announced that $2.1 million would be awarded to three local entities for repairs at 25 homeless facilities in the parishes of Orleans, St. Tammany, and Calcasieu. The repairs will restore 547, or approximately 32%, of an estimated 1,..."],[112243,"The State¿s CDBG funds totaling $72,730,000 will be administered by the Department of Health and Hospitals (DHH) through an interagency agreement should be complete in July, 2007. DHH will, in turn, contract with designated Local Lead Agencies (LLA) who will directly administer the funds for supportive services in conjunction with the property providers. The majority of the PSH units will be provided by LIHTC developments that have committed to at least a 5% set aside of units for PSH. It is anticipated that the Department of Social Services, through a separate interagency agreement, will provide some additional PSH units that can be served through DHH. Additionally, in the Small Rental Property program, some non-profits receiving incentive awards will be providing PSH units that will be available for support through this grant. Awards are limited to $25,000 per PSH unit and have a term of 10 years. As PSH units are built and available for occupancy, the LLA¿s will contract directly with the property providers to ensure compliance with the requirements of the funding and provide the PSH tenant to the owner for occupancy. LIHTC units are not expected to be available until 2008 and 2009."],[112381,"The Small Rental Property Program design was approved by LRA and OCD-DRU on December 28, 2006; the final scoring design was approved on January 5, 2007. The Small Rental application was approved by the LRA late in January, 2007. ICF International began accepting applications for the first of multiple competitive rounds on January 29, 2007, and continued receiving them through March 15, 2007, for the general pool, and through March 22, 2007 for the non-profit pool. There were $202,029,100 in federal funds awarded to 2,693 applications in Round 1, with an average award of $75,020 for a project. The total number of applications received was 6,734. Of all the applications received, 5,338 met basic eligibility requirements and were admitted to the Round 1 competition. The other 1,396 either were deferred or denied; they have the opportunity to re-apply and compete again in the next round. Of the 2,693 awarded applications, 237 were owner-occupants of three- and four-unit properties. Non-profit organizations submitted 80 of the awarded applications. There were 2,376 awards made to applicants from a \"general pool\" of small owners of rental properties. The remaining 2,645 applicants may apply in Round 2. Round 2 opened on June 5, and will accept applications until July 31. Since the opening of Round 2, there have been 2,559 applications received (812 paper; 1747 online). By the end of June, out of 2,693, the total of conditional award packages returned for processing was 2,046. There have been 1,998 packages reviewed. There were 98 complete packages, meaning no information was missing. There have been 368 withdrawn packages."],[112721,"The program was available with the rollout of the 2007/2008 GO Zone LIHTC Qualified Allocation Plan. The Program Description was approved by OCD and the LRA. The plan was presented to developers at a series of LHFA Developers Meetings in August and September of 2006. The application deadline for the 2007 Tax Credit Applications was October 20, 2006 with reservation of credits made on December 13, 2006. Award Acceptance Agreements were mailed to the 33 projects receiving the Piggyback awards on December 21, 2006 and all were returned to OCD by the January 5, 2007 deadline. The 33 projects will result in a total of 5,737 new units when completed. In April, OCD/LHFA addressed the skyrocketing insurance costs for LIHTC developments by launching the Operating Deficit Loan Program (ODLP). Applications for ODLP were accepted through April 4, 2007. ODLP Award Acceptance letters continue to be returned with 69 of the 84 accepting the conditional award. No ODLP closings took place in June. The first is expected in early August. The first CDBG Piggyback development to close was 200 Carondelet; it is a 190 unit mixed-income (60% Market rate, 40% affordable) acquisition/rehab in the CBD of New Orleans. This CDBG loan of $26,500,000 is part of $70,000,000 in total development costs and closed May 16, 2007. OCD-DRU also closed the second and third Piggyback deals (the Crescent Club and the Preserve) on May 24th. These two mixed income developments account for $19,600,000 and $15,950,000, respectively, in CDBG funding. Both are new construction and located in New Orleans on Tulane Avenue. The Crescent Club will develop 228 units while the Preserve will account for 183 total units. Closing in June, Tiffany Apartments, located in Abbeville in Vermilion Parish, is the first South-West..."],[118537,"This activity originally held some of the funds for Long Term Community Recovery, and the obligation above had been created for that purpose. It has since been broken out into the various activities."],[119184,"HUD approved the release of funds for the Local Government Infrastructure Program; outlined in OCD&apos;s Action Plan One; on May 9, 2006, to provide the match funding for FEMA Public Assistance and Hazard Mitigation projects to restore critical local infrastructure damaged by the storms, as well as other eligible local response/recovery activities. Action Plan One, Amendment 10, which includes $100 million for match of Category A&B FEMA PA grants, was submitted to HUD on March 23, 2007. The application forms for PA match and FEMA-ineligible repairs to schools, along with instructions for completing them, were posted on the OCD-DRU website, allowing local governments to begin applying for CDBG funds to cover the 10% non-federal match on FEMA Public Assistance grants. E-mails were sent to each PA applicant in the state to let them know the application was available, and training sessions/input meetings were held with several potential applicants. On June 25, the LRA board passed a resolution reducing the amount of this program by $20,273,333 in order to provide additional funding to the Road Home program."],[123365,"In March 2007, the contract was finalized between OCD-DRU and Entergy and $171.7M was transferred to Entergy New Orleans to mitigate rate increases in New Orleans. In May, Entergy submitted an additional request for payment for $5,092,614 for eligible activities performed during the month of March. In June, Entergy submitted an additional request for payment for $4,019,979 also for eligible activities performed during the month of March. Personnel from the Infrastructure and Financial teams plan to perform monitoring on this project during the month of July. # of businesses LMI - 7,128; # of households benefiting LMI - 61,398."],[137261,"Through the work of its various committees and task forces, the Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) has developed a series of strategic priorities and recovery plans in the areas of housing, infrastructure and economic development. The LRA has led the development of recovery policy and initiatives in the areas of housing, infrastructure, and economic development. These initiatives overlap with policy and planning in areas of health care, social services, education, and other subjects. The majority of LRA&apos;s work on the development, passage, submission and implementation of CDBG action plans has been tied to policies on housing, infrastructure and economic development -- the bedrocks of recovery. During 2006 and the first 2 quarters of 2007, the LRA focused heavily on economic and workforce development program development and implementation; infrastructure recovery programs and implementation issues, including education infrastructure design considerations; homeowner and rental housing program policy and implementation oversight issues; and community and regional planning efforts related to recovery priorities. Regarding the long-term planning process, the LRA collaborated with the American Institute of Architecture (AIA) and American Planning Association (APA) to develop rebuilding principles, upon which the LRA initiated a planning effort called Louisiana Speaks. This was a multifaceted planning initiative designed to address planning at every level. This extends from community planning such as the Unified New Orleans Plan (the LRA has supported UNOP through the recruitment of $3 million in private funding from the Rockefeller Foundation and the commitment of $2 million in CDBG funds for outreach, including communications and input with the New Orleans Diaspora)..."],[170197,"The Disaster Recovery Unit (DRU) of the Office of Community Development (OCD), Division of Administration (DOA), was created in the aftermath of the 2005 Hurricanes Katrina and Rita. The DRU administers the CDBG Disaster Recovery funds approved by Congress on December 23, 2005 and on June 30, 2007. Personnel were hired from within Louisiana and around the United States with experience in all aspects of CDBG programs. Managers and staff were retained in the areas of homeownership housing, multifamily/rental/supportive housing, infrastructure, economic development, policy and reporting, legal, and financial and auditing. Experienced employees of the Office of Community Development, CDBG Program, provide training as needed to new hires. In addition, the OCD continues to supply CDBG training in partnership with the Council of State Community Development Agencies and ICF International. The DOA&iquest;s resources are available to augment the DRU with expertise in budgeting, accounting, reporting, contract review and legal matters. The DRU is in close contact with the Louisiana Legislative Auditors. Currently, 8 auditors are assigned to the DRU. We are planning on adding 4 more auditors in the near future. At the close of this quarter, we had 51 people working in the DRU. The DRU received approval in September, 2006, to raise our total number of staff to 74. This does not include the 30 approved LRA positions. The administrative budget is used to fund salaries and related benefits, travel expenses, operating supplies and services, professional services, and inter-agency transfers. As mentioned last quarter, community meetings have been held throughout the impacted parishes and in cities with large numbers of evacuees. These meetings have raised citizen awareness of the reco..."],[170318,"Technical Assistance to the State."],[60483,"ESDC continues to review and pay out the second round of Small Firm Attraction and Retention Grants (SFARG). Additionally, ESDC is doing post grant requirement follow up or compliance work on SFARG and other programs."],[20682,"[[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]130 LIBERTY[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] BUILDING DECONSTRUCTION During the quarter ending September 30, 2007 structural deconstruction of floors 32 through 26 were completed. Deconstruction of the 25th floor began on August 9, 2007. Abatement of floors 25 through 20 was completed and approved by the Regulators with an anticipated inspection for the 19th and 18th floors to be scheduled for the week of August 20, 2007. All abatement and deconstruction work stopped following a fire at the building on Saturday, August 18, 2007, during which two firefighters perished. At the end of August 2007, one of the subcontractors was terminated. Certain safety, fire clean up, and other interim work has been performed to address post-fire conditions. Air monitoring has continued throughout the post-fire period and the results of that testing continues to be reported on LMDC¿s web site www.renewnyc.com. PUBLIC OUTREACH AND PARTICIPATION-- PUBLIC OUTREACH AND PARTICIPATION-- Following the August 18, 2007 fire, several electronic updates (E-Updates) were sent to subscribers to alert regarding conditions and work in and around the building and a meeting of the [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]130 Liberty Street[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] Advisory Committee was held on October 12, 2007."],[61923,"There was no activity to report in the quarter."],[64755,"Last year LMDC received 206 applications to the Community Enhancement Fund program. An advisory panel was formed and convened to review applications. Applications are under review. Additional site visits are being performed and follow-up information collected by LMDC staff. During the the quarter LMDC entered into a contract with a consultant to advise LMDC during the grant making process and oversee process."],[65351,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PROGRAM -- Funds provided for capital activities will support new construction of buildings, renovations, expansions into adjoining spaces, equipment purchases, and site acquisitions. LMDC continues to work with selected grant recipients to develop project scopes and to ensure compliance with all necessary and related federal laws and regulations, including compliance with HUD&apos;s environmental review procedures and required public notices. At the end of the third quarter of 2007, there were twenty executed contracts for capital projects. Newly executed contracts include: Pace University: Center for Cultural Affairs. The New Museum continued work towards opening a new 50,000 square foot public facility by next quarter. Work on Eldridge Street project renovations continue on the historic synagogue with an opening still planned for next quarter. LMDC has also undertaken a survey to assist in documenting the Cultural Enhancement Fund¿s impacts and benefits as well as to enable LMDC to evaluate progress in meeting the program¿s overall goals. During the third quarter, seven surveys were completed by sub-recipients at or near the end of their contracts. A total of eight surveys have been received to date. The survey was undertaken at the end of the quarter in 2007 and LMDC anticipates sending out surveys to all sub-recipients in subsequent quarters as their projects proceed."],[65392,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PROGRAM -- Funds provided will support educational programs, exhibitions, and developing promotional materials and initiatives. LMDC continues to work with selected grant recipients to further develop project scopes for the programs and events. In November 2006, HUD approved an amended PAP and reduced the Cultural Enhancement Fund by $7,000,000 to $28,000,000. These funds were reallocated to the Final Action Plan for cultural uses. In the third quarter of 2007, there were fourteen active contracts supporting programmatic and event related activities. Several subrecipients provided programs or events benefiting Lower Manhattan for example, Wall Street Rising held a three day music festival in September that was well attended and drew audiences to Lower Manhattan. The Battery Park Conservancy also hosted its Evening Stars festival in September. Project Rebirth continues to record time lapse footage of the rebuilding of the World Trade Center site and to screen the documentary film venture to audiences. During the third quarter four CEF surveys were completed by subrecipients at or near the end of their contracts. A total of six surveys have been received to date. This survey was undertaken in the second quarter of 2007 and a pilot survey distributed in May. LMDC anticipates sending out surveys to all subrecipients in subsequent quarters as their projects proceed."],[65421,"LOWER MANHATTAN CULTURAL ENHANCMENT FUND (CEF) PROGRAM -- Funds for planning projects will support cultural organizations seeking new homes in Lower Manhattan, as well as several organizations that are in the early stages of a major expansion or early renovation. The funds will be used to assist these organizations with site search, project management, business plan development, and pre-design activities. LMDC continues to work with grant recipients to develop project planning scopes. In the third quarter of 2007, ten subrecipients continued work begun during the previous quarter. International Studio and Curatorial Program signed a subrecipient agreement in this quarter. Museum of the Chinese in the Americas has begun invoicing for their creation of a new 12,500 square foot museum designed by famed artist, Maya Linn. We continue to work towards getting subrecipient agreements signed with Soho Repertory Theatre and Asian Americans for Equality/Chinatown Partnership LDC (CREATE). The Children&apos;s Museum of the Arts is nearing completion of its planning grant that has been instrumental in the development of a business plan in connection with its proposed relocation to another facility in Lower Manhattan. Roulette Intermedium has distributed surveys and held group interviews with artists and audiences to determine service needs for artists and audiences in conjunction with the relocation of its public facility to Lower Manhattan. During the third quarter, Roulette Intermedium completed a CEF survey, the first received from a planning subrecipient. The CEF survey project was undertaken in the second quarter of 2007 and LMDC anticipates sending out surveys to all sub-recipients in subsequent quarters as their projects proceed."],[66788,"CHINATOWN LOCAL DEVELOPMENT CORPORATION (LDC) -- Funding for this program will be used to spearhead community improvements in Chinatown and engage in a combination of short-term projects and long-term planning. CHINATOWN CLEEN STREETS PROGRAM--The Chinatown Partnership LDC, in conjunction with the New York City Department of Small Business Services (SBS), will implement and oversee a comprehensive, multi-year Clean Streets program. A maintenance subcontractor was selected and Clean Street workers have been deployed, power washing and daily sweeping and tidy work has been executed everyday. This quarter Lunar Stages, a special event to celebrate the performing arts of Asia, was presented. Hundreds of residents and visitors enjoyed the events. The Chinatown cleaning crew worked the event to keep Columbus Park and the surrounding area free of litter. The Chinatown Outreach educates merchants, property owners and residents about the benefits of the Clean Streets, and solicit their support for a permanent maintenance program. CHINATOWN SHORT-TERM PROJECTS AND LONG-TERM PLANNING -- In addition to the Clean Streets program, the Chinatown Partnership LDC will spearhead many community enhancement projects and initiatives. LMDC and the LDC are working on finalizing the subrecipient agreement."],[66846,"The planning phase of the project was completed in the quarter ending September 30, 2006. After the subrecipient agreement was finalized on September 12th HPD announced the creation of the new Chinatown/Lower East Side Acquisition Program and issued an RFQ soliciting non-profit organizations to participate in the program. Three entities have been pre-qualified for participation in the Chinatown Lower East Side Acquisition Program: Asian Americans for Equality (AAFE), Cooper Square Mutual Housing Association in partnership with the Cooper Square Committee, and Settlement Housing Fund Inc. in partnership with Two Bridges Neighborhood Council. To date, HPD has approved one funding request: AAFE has been approved to receive $3.6 million in program funding for the acquisition of 36 units in two buildings on Henry Street. The funds were closed on February 7, 2007 for this project. HPD closed on [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]112 Eldridge Street[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] in in August 2007 using a short term bridge loan from Cathay Bank secured by CD¿s held by AAFE, the sponsor. The loan will be used to pay the HSBC debt, bridging the period that it takes for the HPD check to clear, once it is deposited at closing. HPD sent out a letter in August to the other 2 pre-qualified organizations, Settlement Housing Fund/Two Bridges Neighborhood Council and Cooper Square Mutual Housing Ass/Cooper Square Committee to encourage future proposals form the organizations. AAFE submitted another proposal for a 20 unit property at [[PUBLIC_ADDRESS]]211 Madison St.[[/PUBLIC_ADDRESS]] The commitment letter has been signed by HPD for this property and the estimated date for closing is December 15, 2007."],[66897,"EXPLORE CHINATOWN CAMPAIGN--As of the quarter ending June 30, 2006, the Explore Chinatown Campaign was transitioned to the Chinatown Partnership Local Development Corporation (LDC). All activities related to this program are complete. CPLDC is awaiting the final invoice from LMDC."],[66351,"COLUMBUS PARK PAVILION --.In the third quarter of 2007 final finishes and painting occurred as well as the placement of the bird netting on the underside of the Pavilion Roof. All lighting for the exterior has been installed and is operational. The final approval for the hydraulic elevator in the Pavilion was obtained. The one remaining item for the Pavilion is the installation of the gas meter and test firing of the boiler. The filing with the Department of Buildings (DOB) required numerous filings and re-filings and now at this date the gas meter filing has been approved. The gas meter is expected to be installed and the boiler tested in late October."],[77227,"In November 2006 HUD approved an amendment to the PAP reducing the Drawing Center¿s allocation to $2,000,000, and reallocating $8,000,000 to the Final Action Plan for cultural and community uses, with the Drawing Center preserved as an eligible recipient. A contract for $2,000,000 is in progress between LMDC and the Drawing Center. LMDC continued to work with the Drawing Center during the third quarter 2007 to complete and execute a subrecipient agreement."],[102449,"LOWER MANHATTAN ECONOMIC DEVELOPMENT PROGRAM -- A description of the Small Firm Assistance Program was released to the public in June and comments were solicited. This program will provide grants to eligible small firms in Lower Manhattan adversely affected by street closures related to publically-funded construction. LMDC and the City continue to work on developing the Small Firm Assistance Program."],[102528,"LMDC signed a subrecipient agreement with the New York City Department of Education in December 2006. In the third quarter 2007 LMDC continued to consult with the School Construction Authority to identify possible projects, although no selections have been made."],[103487,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT ACCESS-- In the third quarter 2007, LMDC worked with a project team consisting of the NYC Departments of Parks and Recreation, City Planning and Transportation, New York City Hall, environmental consultant AKRF, and environmental legal counsel Carter, Ledyard & Milburne, LLP. The team met several times over this quarter to discuss design modifications to address comments received from the State Historic Preservation Office (SHPO). A follow up meeting with SHPO is expected next quarter to continue the consultation process, pursuant to Section 106 of the National Historic Preservation Act regarding designs, archaeology, and the effect determination. LMDC has also been involved in meetings with the Federal, State, and local transportation authorities to coordinate environmental reviews associated with roadbed reconstruction in and around Peck Slip. Additionally, LMDC submitted Coastal Zone Consistency forms to the New York State Department of State (DOS) this quarter for Peck, Catherine, Montgomery, and Rutgers Slips and the East River Park Connector. In August, LMDC received a general concurrence letter from DOS for these activities."],[103827,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT ESPLANADE AND PIERS--During the third quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with a project team consisting of the NYC Departments of City Planning and Transportation, the NYC Economic Development Corporation (EDC), New York City Hall, environmental consultant AKRF, and environmental legal counsel Carter Ledyard & Milburne, LLP. The public comment period for the Final Environmental Impact Statement (FEIS) ended on July 2, 2007. LMDC prepared responses to the comments received and will attach them to the Record of Decision (ROD), which has been drafted and is expected to be approved next quarter. HUD published the notice of the Draft Conformity Determination in the Federal Register and LMDC received approval of the methodologies used from the New York State Department of Environmental Conservation. The City also concluded their ULURP process this quarter. A Programmatic Agreement was executed on August 3, 2007 between LMDC, the State Historic Preservation Office (SHPO), and the Advisory Council on Historic Preservation (ACHP). LMDC, SHPO, and the City will continue to consult on preservation issues throughout this project, but this agreement concludes the Section 106 process, in accordance with the National Historic Preservation Act."],[104123,"There was no activity to report on this program in the quarter."],[104331,"During the third quarter of 2007 LMDC continued to meet with the project team to develop the Fulton-Nassau Corridor Design Guidelines and Incentive Program."],[104373,"During the third quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with the Department of Parks and Recreation and the project team to continue the environmental review process for Pearl Street Playground, Titanic Park, DeLury Square, and Burling Slip. The State Historic Preservation Organization and Landmarks Preservation Commission were consulted regarding the designs for Titanic Park and Burling Slip."],[104415,"During the third quarter of 2007, LMDC continued to meet with its team of project consultants to continue the environmental review process, including the environmental assessment examining the storefront and façade incentives program, open space improvements and the acquisition of a parcel of private property. The State Historic Preservation Organization and Landmarks Preservation Commission were consulted regarding parts of the project of interest to them."],[104452,"During the 4th quarter of 2006, HUD approved an amendment to PAP 6 to eliminate the allocation of $8,000,000 for the Fulton Corridor Residential Rehabilitation and Conversion Program."],[104463,"During the third quarter of 2007, LMDC worked with the project team to continue the environmental assessment. The State Historic Preservation Organization and Landmarks Preservation Commission were consulted on the parts of the project of interest to them."],[104687,"FITERMAN HALL RECONSTRUCTION -- LMDC is working with the City University of New York and the Dormitory Authority of the State of New York to finalize necessary agreements in order to provide funds for this project."],[110039,"Activity for this project has been completed. Disbursement of funds in this quarter represent payment of invoices for work done in previous quarters."],[111901,"REHABILITATION/RECONSTRUCTION OF A PUBLIC IMPROVEMENT -- HUDSON RIVER PARK SEGMENT 3--On February 28, 2006, HUD approved Partial Action Plan 10, which allocated up to $70 million for the completion of Hudson River Park Segment 3 in Tribeca. Within this area would be two public recreational piers (Piers 25 and 26), an ecological pier, and an adjacent upland park. Structural work such as pile driving, structural component fabrication and placement of precast deck components has begun. Work in the North Upland portion of the project including site engineering and site logistics planning continued this quarter. Installation of underground drainage structures and utilities has begun. The foundation wall and slab layout is in progress and bulkhead rehabilitation continues. Engineering work on the Platform between piers 25 & 26 as well as pile driving and structural component fabrication is underway."],[112277,"The New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development (HPD) is continuing the process of identifying an affordable housing project that is consistent with the General Project Criteria (as defined above). HPD is currently evaluating potential projects for which to allocate and administer funds for an affordable housing program. As HPD is responsible for implementing the NYC¿c New Housing Marketplace Plan, HPD is evaluating how the allocation can best be used towards achieving the goal of building and preserving 165,000 units of affordable housing. By December 2007 HPD hopes to conduct the following activities in order to have a final list of proposed project(s) and preliminary scope(s). 1) Evaluate the use of funds in conjunction with current Federal new construction financing programs, specifically the 202 senior housing program 2) Evaluate allocating funds towards an expansion of the Chinatown/Lower East Side Acquisition Program 3)Meet with elected officials and Community Boards to gather feedback."],[123917,"The Job Creation and Retention Program (JCRP) provides discretionary incentives to companies making commitments to attract, create and/or retain at least 200 jobs in Lower Manhattan. The program will run though 2007. JCRP is managed by ESD in cooperation with NYC EDC. JCRP assistance is in the form of job-based grants for companies making a minimum 7 year commitment to Lower Manhattan. The amount of assistance is based on several criteria including the number of jobs, economic impact, the creation of new employment in Lower Manhattan and the degree of risk of employment leaving Lower Manhattan. As of September 30, 2007, ESD has offered incentive grants to 124 companies (excluding those companies whose projects were terminated after an offer was issued). Seventy-five companies have accepted grant offers worth $290.6 million to date (not counting projects that were terminated after offer acceptance). Of those accepted offers, 73 active projects have received ESD¿s Board approval, representing a commitment of 74,052 created and retained jobs in Lower Manhattan and an additional 14,946 jobs retained elsewhere in New York City. The value of the ESD Board approved grants is $289.2 million. ESD has disbursed all of its original $170 million allocation for the program and has been drawing on LMDC¿s $150 million sub-allocation since August 2003. However, there is a balance of $176,343 in the $170 million allocation due to ESD¿s recapture of grant funds from Countrywide Insurance and Embassy Suites (due to job reductions). Of the original $150,000,000 LMDC sub-allocation, ESD has disbursed $98,243,447 to date."],[124129,"LMDC continues to work with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development to plan and develop the scope of the rehabilitation for the development. It is anticipated that this scope planning phase will be completed in the following quarter and a subrecipient agreement finalized in the near term. The subrecipient agreement is scheduled to be finalized next quarter and progress with the project will ensue."],[125470,"There was no activity to report on this program in the quarter."],[126792,"LOWER MANHATTAN FERRY SERVICE PROGRAM-- On May 1, 2007, ferry services began between the City of Yonkers, the World Financial Center (on the Hudson River) and Pier 11 (on the East River). New York Water Taxi inaugurated service between Haverstraw and Lower Manhattan in September 2007. In September there were a total of 2,295 riders on the ferry services."],[126816,"LOWER MANHATTAN PUBLIC INFORMATION PROGRAM --- The Lower Manhattan Public Information Plan Program (Program) is a public awareness campaign which aims to facilitate the area¿s recovery by providing a trusted source of information. It is intended to enhance the coordinated efforts of the City of New York, New York State, and federal agencies throughout the long-term process of rebuilding and redevelopment. The Program consists of a website, a newsletter, and informational construction signage that focuses on Lower Manhattan and delivers the most accurate and timely information about the area¿s rebuilding progress to the area stakeholders including residents, businesses, and visitors. This is accomplished through marketing, advertising, stakeholder outreach, and strategic planning. The centerpiece of the Program is a comprehensive website, www.LowerManhattan.info, which offers regularly updated news and information for area residents, workers, and visitors. October 2005 was the final month for LMDC¿s hosting of the LowerManhattan.info program. As of November 2005 the Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center is in charge of operating the program. The Program continues to move forward with oversight and funding from other sources. The Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center is now in charge of monitoring the site."],[126838,"LOWER MANHATTAN STREET MANAGEMENT PROGRAM¿ Work progressed on several tasks in the quarter ending September 30, 2007. Two traffic models were merged into one traffic model and the Battery Park City area was incorporated into the model. (At the end of the first phase of this project, NYC DOT had two separate traffic models that covered the east and western portions of Lower Manhattan.) Coordination efforts continued with the Lower Manhattan Construction Command Center (LMCCC). Evaluation of the feasibility of converting Battery Parking Garage into a facility that could accommodate commuter and tour buses occurred. A Bus Study to determine the existing supply and demand of Lower Manhattan bus parking, as well as the volumes and staging locations of buses entering the study area continued. Data for analysis of ¿placard parking¿ in the various districts of Lower Manhattan was collected. Schedule Simulation tool on Lower Manhattan Construction Command¿s computers was completed and installed. This will help LMCCC understand the traffic impacts of construction and assist with their decision-making process. The study of Pearl Street closure was completed."],[125162,"TRIBUTE IN LIGHT -- On March 11, 2002, marking the six-month anniversary of the World Trade Center tragedy, two beams of light rose into the night sky from a site just north of the World Trade Center site to honor those lost on September 11th, and to celebrate the spirit of all New Yorkers who have worked to rebuild and renew New York City. LMDC funding allowed for the purchase of the lights (which had previously been rented), technical support, and on-going maintenance for the display for five years. The Tribute in Light of September 11, 2007 was successfully and momentously displayed from the Battery Park Tunnel Garage, where it will remain through 2008. The 2008 Tribute in Light display is currently in the early stages of the planning phase."],[129389,"MASARYK TOWER REHABILITATION -- LMDC continues to work with the New York City Department of Housing Preservation and Development to plan and develop the scope of the rehabilitation for the development. It is anticipated that the agreement will be finalized next quarter."],[134418,"During the third quarter of 2007, LMDC and the City of New York met with stakeholders to evaluate and plan various improvements including: anchoring NoGos (polygonal sidewalk barriers) to the sidewalk, a turntable for vehicle security, new street furnishings, light poles, repaneling of security booths, pedestrian interdiction near New York Stock Exchange, Eurocobble sidewalk treatment, and replacement of the fence in front of the Stock Exchange. Various issues are being addressed and investigated including access to buildings, maintenance, and security."],[135871,"EAST RIVER WATERFRONT PLANNING-- The East River Waterfront Planning Study has been completed. The study led to a second phase of work for the East River Esplanade/Piers and East River Access (see reports ERA-0240 and EREP-0240). The remaining reimbursement requests have not yet been received from the City."],[135816,"LMDC PLANNING - ECONOMIC ANALYSIS ¿ LMDC has distributed surveys to 17 Cultural Enhancement Fund (CEF) recipients. These surveys will help LMDC evaluate the economic impacts of the CEF program as well as enable LMDC to evaluate progress in meeting the program¿s overall goals. Survey questions relate to leveraging funding, generating economic benefits, promoting access to the arts, and creating a vibrant community. See the activity progress narratives for the CEF Program."],[135922,"LMDC PLANNING--ENVIRONMENTAL --- LMDC sought the advice and counsel of law firms and environmental consulting firms on retainer on environmental and land use requirements and matters relating to LMDC¿s planning and development initiatives in Lower Manhattan. Historically, such projects included early phases of (1) the World Trade Center Memorial and Redevelopment Plan (WTC Plan), (2) the East River Waterfront Esplanade and Piers Project, (3) cultural enhancement and community improvement projects, and (4) public information, outreach, and participation efforts relating to the foregoing activities. During the quarter LMDC considered environmental review requirements for potential future projects and programs."],[135961,"There was no activity to report on this program during the quarter."],[135996,"There was no activity to report on this project in the quarter."],[136040,"There was no activity to report in the quarter."],[136194,"Parks Phase 1 - As of the end of the second quarter 2007 all thirteen park sites were substantially completed. The remaining site to be completed, the Wall Street Park fountain, has continued to have glass fabrication and re-fabrication problems with glass breakage occurring. The City of New York Department of Parks and Recreation expects to receive the glass in the fourth quarter of 2007. Parks Phase 2 - The second phase of parks improvements continued with the environmental review process and all have started design this quarter, except Allen and Pike Street Malls. The projects which have started design include Washington Market Park Comfort Station, Collect Pond Park, Sara D. Roosevelt Phase 2 and James Madison Park. Canal Varick Laight Park has completed design and a bid date is pending. Construction on this park is expected to begin in December 2007. LMDC entered into a subrecipient agreement with the NYC Department of Design and Construction to improve Louise Nevelson Plaza. The construction work will be a component of the street reconstruction of Liberty Street, Maiden Lane, and Pearl Street. Enhancements to Louise Nevelson Plaza include upgrades to lighting, accessibility, and aesthetics while highlighting the Louise Nevelson sculptures."],[169139,"RESIDENTIAL GRANT PROGRAM (RGP) -- As of September 30, 2007 LMDC provided over $227 million in grants, approving over 39,000 total applications and distributing over 30,000 September 11th Residents Grants, 26,000 2-Year Grants, and 8,000 Family Grants to individuals. The program officially ended on August 31, 2005. LMDC continues to implement all necessary program close-out procedures, answer inquiries by grant applicants and perform any necessary recoupment procedures."],[171005,"SMALL FIRM ATTRACTION AND RETENTION GRANT (SFARG) -- To date, ESDC and LMDC have made 3,911 grants to 2,634 businesses for approximately $127 million. Thirty-four of the grants that were made were distributed in the third quarter of 2007."],[171231,"On November 18, 2005, HUD approved Partial Action Plan 6, which allocated funds for the planning and design, of a mixed income residential rental building at [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in Tribeca. The subrecipient agreement was executed on December 5, 2006. The project broke ground in December of 2005, and the mixed-income rental component was 82% complete based on a site observation report dated September 21, 2007. Work is proceeding in general accordance with the plans, specifications and all pertinent documents reviewed by inspectors."],[172400,"SOUTHERN SITE--The World Trade Center Memorial and Redevelopment Plan (WTC Plan) includes several parcels of land just south of the World Trade Center Site, referred to collectively as the ¿Southern Site,¿ that will be used to construct infrastructure and other components of the WTC Plan. LMDC acquired the largest parcel at [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in August 2004, [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS] in February 2006, and the following parcels constitute the remainder: (1) privately-owned [REDACTED — POTENTIAL SINGLE-FAMILY ADDRESS]; and (2) several public New York City streets-Washington Street between Cedar and Liberty Streets and subsurface portions of Cedar and Liberty Streets. LMDC has been working with the The City of New York and The Port Authority of New York and New Jersey on the acquisition and configuration of the street parcels as well as the review and development of plans for the entire Southern Site, including the redesign and relocation of St. Nicholas Church. The Southern Site was expanded slightly to include additional street parcels and added to the project site for the WTC Plan along with certain parcels to the north of the WTC Site, as described in the General Project Plan for the World Trade Center Memorial and Cultural Program, as amended February 17, 2007. On behalf of LMDC, Empire State Development Corporation submitted information regarding the acquisition and disposition of certain street related parcels at the World Trade Center and adjacent areas to the New York State Public Authorities Control Board (PACB) and the matter was considered and approved by the PACB at its meeting on September 26, 2007."],[172917,"TRIBECA FILM FESTIVAL (Film Festival)-- The Film Festival was the first major event to be held in Lower Manhattan after the September 11th attacks. The Film Festival has been responsible for attracting hundred-of-thousands of visitors and generating millions of dollars in revenue for Lower Manhattan. All activities and payments related to the 2004 and 2005 Film Festival are complete. Going forward all activities related to the Film Festival will be funded through Cultural Enhancement Grant allocations."],[172952,"There was no activity to report in the quarter."],[173176,"TRIBUTE VISITORS CENTER -- The Tribute Center opened adjacent to the World Trade Center site in September 2006. It offers an interactive learning experience that focuses on providing visitor orientation and factual information about the terrorist attacks of February 26, 1993 and September 11, 2001. The Tribute Center focuses on telling the human stories of those affected through its exhibits, programs and docent-led tours of the site. Tribute is expected to be open for a minimum of 5 years, effectively bridging the gap between the present day and the completion of the future WTC Memorial, Reflecting Absence. The docent tour program officially began on November 19, 2005, with one tour per weekday and two tours per weekend day. The program increased to two weekday tours and four weekend tours in February 2006. To date, over 100 docents from the 9/11 community (victims&apos; families, survivors, rescue workers, residents, and others) have been trained to provide tours. The Center houses 4,000 square feet of gallery space on the first floor and 2,000 square feet of gallery and program space on the lower level, including a space for community gatherings. Galleries include: ¿World Trade Center Community Remembered,¿ celebrating the World Trade Center before September 11th; ¿Passage Through Time,¿ telling the personal stories of those who experienced September 11th first hand; ¿Aftermath and Recovery,¿ where a moving film shows the mammoth task and tremendous will of the rescue and recovery workers; ¿Tribute,¿ showing images and lovingly selected objects given by families to share their loved ones with the world; and ¿Voices of Promise,¿ where the vast international response to September 11th is documented. All activities have been completed for construction associated with..."],[173332,"PERFORMING ARTS CENTER DESIGN---During the third quarter of 2007, a subrecipient agreement was signed between LMDC and the City of New York Department of Small Business Services to define and analyze Performing Arts Center programming, operation, design, costs, and constructability to determine subgrade requirements."],[173386,"W-MEM-0171 WTC MEMORIAL AND MEMORIAL MUSEUM -- LMDC, in partnership with the National September 11th Memorial & Museum, will provide initial funding for the design and construction of the Memorial, and will plan for and oversee the design development and construction documentation for the Memorial Museum, and possibly other elements of the WTC Plan. There were several highlights for the quarter ending September 2007. Hardened wall foundations were approximately 85 percent completed. Footings and foundations contract work was approximately 95 percent completed. Backfill operations continued. The north viewing gallery wall and slab were poured; the south viewing gallery construction began and is expected to be completed by the end of this year. Elevator, escalator, and ejector pits are also nearly complete. Underground piping work continued. Work continued on the programmatic development of the combined Museum Pavilion and the Memorial Museum, including development of a schematic outline delineating the programmatic uses of the Museum¿s public spaces and the continued research and acquisition of curatorial resources. Review and refinement of schematic layouts (spatial allocations and placement of large artifacts) continued for the exhibition areas. The Memorial trees were maintained at the holding site in New Jersey. A mock-up was prepared of two options for the design of the Memorial names parapet. The Foundation has realized $119 million in contributions for construction and other expenses. See Activity Progress Narrative for the related W-MUS-0171 (Museum Pavilion)."],[173437,"Work continued on the programmatic development of the combined Museum Pavilion and the Memorial Museum, including development of a schematic outline delineating the programmatic uses of the Museum¿s public spaces and the continued research and acquisition of curatorial resources. Regular content development meetings were held between Museum staff and the exhibition design consultants to review and develop the overall content outline, concentrating mainly on the Primary Exhibition. Regular design development meetings were also held between Museum staff and the exhibition design consultants to review and refine schematic layouts (spatial allocations and placement of large artifacts) for the exhibition areas. The exhibition designers continued with their architectural coordination work. Value engineering exercises were conducted on the Museum Pavilion based on 50% Design Development Documents. See Activity Progress Narrative for the related W-MEM-0171."],[173521,"There was no activity to report in the quarter. Disbursements in this quarter represent payment of invoices for work done in previous quarters."],[64618,"CATEGORY ONE -- EMERGENCY AND TEMPORARY SERVICE RESPONSE -- Requests for Category One totaled $403,674,125. No disbursements were made during the third quarter of 2007 under Category One. Disbursements to date total $160,069,892, leaving a balance of $89,930,108 from the $250 million allocated for Category One."],[64648,"CATEGORY TWO--PERMANENT RESTORATION AND INFRASTRUCTURE IMPROVEMENTS --- Category Two requests to date total $421,042,565 (Permanent Restoration $383,614,301 + Southern Site 37,428,264). No disbursements were made during the third quarter of 2007 under Category Two. Disbursements to date total $109,357,751, (Permanent Restoration $108,068,053 + Southern Site $1,289,698), leaving a balance of $220,642,249 of a $330 million allocation."],[136108,"PLANNING AND ADMINSTRATION --¿- UTILITIES RESTORATION AND INFRASTRUCTURE REBUILDING (URIR) PROGRAM.-- In the third quarter of 2007, ESD continued its review of the appeal by Verizon Communications, Inc. of some of the findings included in the final audit report for Category One. In addition, during such quarter, ESD continued to incur costs in connection with the general management, oversight and coordination of the URIR Program."],[102376,"The application for the Enhanced Bridge Loan Program was revised and posted on the website in August of 2006. The application deadline was December 29, 2006. The total obligation for the three programs is approximately $45,000,000. As of May 31, 2007, the total amount returned to the state on defaulted loans was $371,206.00. Twelve recipients have signed workout agreements amounting to $466,712.00. An additional eight defaults were paid in June for a total of $198,942. In July, the program paid out $419,163.28 in principal and interest and no monies were returned to the state. An additional three defaults were paid in August for a total of $75,000. As of August 31, 2007, the total amount returned to the state on defaulted loans was $419,951.65 including payments remitted by the DOJ; or 14.3% of what has been paid out in defaults through this period. Thirteen recipients have signed workout agreements amounting to $560,300.00. As of September 30, 2007, the total amount returned to the state on defaulted loans was $450,544.00; or 15% of what has been paid out in defaults. Fifteen recipients have signed workout agreements amount to $602,000.00."],[102511,"The program has awarded $28,500,000 to 17 tourism agencies throughout the disaster-affected parishes. The interagency agreement with the Department of Culture, Recreation, & Tourism (DCR&T) is complete, and all the sub-recipient agreements have been signed. A funds distribution process was established between DCR&T and OCD-DRU for creating internal controls to insure a reasonable turn-around in DCR&T&apos;s requests for payments. OCD-DRU staff has made pre-monitoring visits to the New Orleans area and one pre-monitoring visit to the Lafayette area. In June of 2007, the OCD-DRU staff continued the pre-monitoring visits. In the third quarter of 2007, there were 245 ads created (50% of total - 489), 4,535 print ads run by the sub-recipients (96% of total &ndash; 4,746), 583 television ads run (58% of total - 1,000), 302 radio spots (96% of total - 316), 47 billboards ads placed (50% of total - 94), 43 internet ads (43% of total - 99), 2 FAM tours (25% of total - 8) and 2 sales missions (5% of total &iquest; 44). Since the end of the second quarter, the DCR&T has conducted seven on-site monitoring visits. In the third quarter, Washington Parish Tourism Commission tracked visitor inquiries by their 800 number and reported 91 inquires versus 86 in the previous quarter for a total of 177. Third quarter activity accounted for 51% of the total activity for this year. The Vermillion Parish Tourism Commission received 497 more inquires (call ins, walk ins, mail, e-mails and website information) over the same period last year. St. Bernard Parish indicated 6,177 people attended St. Bernard State Parks in the third quarter of 2007 and 14,635 people attended Chalmette National Park. New Orleans Tourism Marketing Corp. indicated a website usage increase of 76% and an increase of 463..."],[102553,"HUD provided the waiver for eligible activities that would allow CDBG funding to be used for research activities in higher education in March of 2007. The Louisiana Board of Regents (LBR) received applications from the eligible applicants, institutions of higher education in southeastern Louisiana, on April 26. On June 23, a selection panel of experts from around the nation completed a review of the proposals, including in person interviews, and developed a list of recommendations. On June 28, the LBR approved the recommendation of 11 awards for a total of $27,605,424. Contract negotiations between the LBR and the OCD-DRU resulted in a signed contract in July of 2007. In August 2007, the program reached the first stages of implementation. The LBR RC/EEP Program Manager was identified and began employment on August 27. Negotiations were finalized between the LBR and OCD-DRU related to the development of a standard sub-recipient contract. On August 17, the LBR conducted a training session for award recipients, a group which included the Principal Investigators as well as fiscal, grants management and other institutional representatives. Upon receipt and approval of all final work plans and budgets, the documents of the eleven sub-recipients were forwarded to the OCD-DRU environmental reviewer. The environmental review process continued through the end of September. The Memorandum of Understanding (MOU) between OCD-DRU and the LBR was approved by the Office of Contractual Review on September 10. The LBR staff reviewed the sub-recipient final revised work plans and budgets beginning September 13. Feedback was given to projects on September 20 and included a request for a composite budget detail. All completed documents were returned by September 28. Also during September..."],[104026,"The RFP issued on October 4, 2006 required proposals to be turned in to the Workforce Commission (WC) on or before November 28, 2006. The WC created an evaluation team to review the submitted 48 proposals amounting to $165,000,000. The final evaluation of the proposals was due to the WC by December 7 for final approval by December 14, 2006. Of the 48 proposals, submitted 19 were chosen to be funded. In March of 2007, an agreement between WC and OCD-DRU was signed and submitted to the Office of Contractual Review (OCR). The meeting between WC Acting Director and OCD-DRU staff resulted in the completion of a template for sub-recipient agreements. As of June, all completed agreements submitted by the WC in the previous month have passed contractual review, and all agreements are now with the OCD-DRU whose staff is in the process of creating a system of tracking and utilizing the information contained in the agreements to help with future monitoring efforts. One of the sub-recipients named Pelican Chapter Associated Builder & Contractors completed a construction industry training program in August. It has placed 75% of the trainees participating in their program for the first round. They are actively pursuing placement of the remainder of trainees, and they are preparing to establish their next round of training to ensure accomplishment of their projected goals. # of participants entering training: Mod - 529 # of participants completing training: Mod - 168 # of participants completing training and placed in employment: Mod - 99"],[104148,"The Solicitation for Grant Application (SGA) process was initiated in October of 2006 and closed December 1, 2006. Just prior to the SGA due date, the State decided to change the parameters of the program and augment it with additional funds beyond the $38,000,000 allocated in Amendment 2. Additional money was transferred to the program in Amendment 8, approved on February 9, 2007. Over 5,500 applications for this funding were received in March 2007, and 3,423 grants were awarded to small firms. The total amount of funding approved was $30,856,618 in amounts of up to $20,000. Collaboration between the OCD-DRU, Small Business Administration (SBA), and Louisiana Economic Development (LED) continued in May to ensure non-duplication of benefits to business grantees. This monitoring continues in weekly telephone conferences to ensure that financial responsibility and proper reimbursement processes are in place. Pre-monitoring reviews were conducted for all sub-recipients/lenders by the OCD-DRU staff. The reviews are to identify any problems with grantee files and to provide training on documenting low to moderate-income (LMI) beneficiaries. The appeals process for the first round of grants was set in place by LED and appeals are still being reviewed by LED&apos;s appointed board. In September, 35 grants were approved through the LED appeals process. Letters were sent out to recipients to notify them of the awards. Approximately 750 second disbursements for the grant program were processed for payment during September, which indicated that the grantee submitted the second disbursement application, receipts as proof of expenditures toward business operations, and LMI information. In August, LED and OCD-DRU staff closed reviews on 652 grant files, and in September, a plan wa..."],[104167,"Administrative costs incurred by Louisiana Economic Development related to the Small Firm Loan and Grant Program."],[104206,"See narrative for Small Loan - LMI (ESLG)"],[104299,"The Solicitation for Grant Application (SGA) process commenced in October, 2006. In January of 2007, a review board was initiated. In February, 19 sub-recipients were selected with whom LED met in March to go over program content and process. In April, LED established a contract template through collaboration with OCD-DRU. In May, a kick-off meeting was held for the program sub-recipients. The agenda consisted of training on LED&iquest;s Sage software system and program training by the OCD-DRU staff. Contracts with sub-recipients are still being negotiated with LED at this time. Training on CDBG regulations was provided to LED and their sub-recipients&iquest; by OCD-DRU during August. The purpose was on compliance of HUD requirements. OCD-DRU continues to provide technical assistance and oversight to the LED program manger regarding reporting, regulations, performance measures, and other issues."],[107407,"On June 25, the Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) board passed a resolution increasing the amount of total Road Home budget by moving $3 million from the infrastructure programs. For the entire Road Home program during this quarter 24,226 appointments were held, representing approximately 41.6% of the total appointments held (181,927). 45,475 applications were received. This is 24.6% of total applications received and recorded (184,653). 48,172 closings were held. This is approximately 82.7% of the total closing held (58,217) with each resulting in a disbursement amount greater than $0. Under the first appropriation specifically, approximately $560,699,813.39 has been dispersed to low to moderate income individuals in approximately 7,850 closings and approximately $358,960,695.25 has been dispersed to elderly homeowners in approximately 4,339 closings."],[107421,"On June 25, the Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) board passed a resolution increasing the amount of total Road Home budget by moving $3 million from the infrastructure programs. For the entire Road Home program during this quarter 24,226 appointments were held, representing approximately 41.6% of the total appointments held (181,927). 45,475 applications were received. This is 24.6% of total applications received and recorded (184,653). 48,172 closings were held. This is approximately 82.7% of the total closing held (58,217) with each resulting in a disbursement amount greater than $0. Under the first appropriation specifically, approximately $560,699,813.39 has been dispersed to low to moderate income individuals in approximately 7,850 closings and approximately $358,960,695.25 has been dispersed to elderly homeowners in approximately 4,339 closings."],[108333,"During the third quarter of 2007, the Louisiana Institute for Building Technology and Safety (LA IBTS) continued to deploy personnel to assist with code inspections, plan evaluations, code permitting, and training in the 11 parishes most impacted by Hurricanes Katrina and Rita: Orleans; Jefferson; St. Bernard; St. Tammany; Calcasieu; Plaquemines; Vermillion; Terrebonne; Cameron; Washington; and Iberia. Based on Memorandums of Understanding entered into by the Department of Public Safety (DPS), these personnel are currently deployed in ten of the eleven parishes and in 12 separate cities. Although Iberia Parish has yet to request assistance, one of its municipalities has. This was only a six month contract, but because the parishes and cities are requesting assistance for at least another year, this contract has been extended."],[108356,"During the third quarter of 2007, the Louisiana Institute for Building Technology and Safety (LA IBTS) continued to deploy personnel to assist with code inspections, plan evaluations, code permitting, and training in the 11 parishes most impacted by Hurricanes Katrina and Rita: Orleans; Jefferson; St. Bernard; St. Tammany; Calcasieu; Plaquemines; Vermillion; Terrebonne; Cameron; Washington; and Iberia. Based on Memorandums of Understanding entered into by the Department of Public Safety (DPS), these personnel are currently deployed in ten of the eleven parishes and in 12 separate cities. Although Iberia Parish has yet to request assistance, one of its municipalities has. This was only a six month contract, but because the parishes and cities are requesting assistance for at least another year, this contract has been extended."],[108411,"The ACORN Housing Corporation (AHC) contract was approved by the State Office of Contractual Review (OCR) on May 11, 2007. ACORN Housing Corporation has begun to provide services to support and encourage homeowners in need of technical assistance to utilize the Road Home program. The Southeast Louisiana Legal Services (SLLS) contract was approved in June. In September, ACORN provided housing counseling and outreach for the Road Home Program to 35 households for a total of 3,496 households. In the same time period, Southeast Louisiana Legal Services served 514 cases, which included 391 active cases and 123 closed cases."],[108437,"The ACORN Housing Corporation (AHC) contract was approved by the State Office of Contractual Review (OCR) on May 11, 2007. ACORN Housing Corporation has begun to provide services to support and encourage homeowners in need of technical assistance to utilize the Road Home program. The Southeast Louisiana Legal Services (SLLS) contract was approved in June. In September, ACORN provided housing counseling and outreach for the Road Home Program to 35 households for a total of 3,496 households. In the same time period, Southeast Louisiana Legal Services served 514 cases, which included 391 active cases and 123 closed cases."],[109099,"This program is combined contractually with the Housing Land Assembly Operations (HLAS) program. In April, OCD-DRU met with representatives of LISC and Enterprise Community Partners Inc. to discuss program implementation details including environmental reviews, acceptance, and review of applications and billing/reimbursement/draw-down procedures. In June, three training sessions were held (New Orleans, Baton Rouge, Lake Charles) to provide information to developers on the availability of the fund and the process to apply for funds. Applications are currently being accepted. Three HDEV applications were received for funding to enable property acquisition and other pre-development costs directly associated with the land purchase and essential to securing construction financing. One HLAS loan closed, and the other loans are expected to close next quarter. Both for profit and non-profit entities have applied for the funds, and the loan that closed in September of 2007 was with a non-profit developer. The number of total applications received was lower than anticipated due a several potential factors including the timing of tax credit awards not coinciding with the availability of the loan fund resources and requests for loans being less than the minimum dollar amount established for the program. Enterprise and LISC have identified these factors and are working to refine the program and increase the number of applicants. Reporting and monitoring processes and criteria were refined to ensure thorough tracking of program goals."],[109145,"A contract was signed with Environs was signed on May 8, 2007, but had to be revisited when it was determined that Environs could not purchase the needed IT equipment. Additional negotiations continued through June. From July through September, Environs was reviewing approximately 1,000 Small Rental properties for state historic preservation compliance. As a result of the additional negotiations, OCD-DRU purchased IT and other equipment for Environs. In September, this equipment arrived at OCD-DRU, and the first payment was issued to Environs on their environmental contract for $156,391.43 to cover administrative costs, GIS set-up expenses, and review of 1,000 Small Rental properties."],[110046,"This program is combined contractually with the Housing Development Loan Fund (HDEV) program. See HDEV for the update."],[110194,"The interagency agreement has been executed by OCD-DRU and Department of Social Services (DSS). The efforts to rehabilitate the 25 homeless shelters that were selected through the Request for Proposal (RFP) process are ongoing. All but one has cleared the environmental review process. A CDBG training for homeless shelter repair grant recipients was held at DSS on May 17, 2007. The RFP for Homeless Prevention and Rapid Re-Housing was published on April 26, 2007 and awards were announced August 29, 2007. A total of $6.2 million was awarded to 8 Continuums of Care in Orleans, Jefferson, Plaquemine, East Baton Rouge, Lafayette, Bossier, St. Bernard, Rapides, St. Tammany and Calcasieu Parishes. A program description has been drafted for the Interim Rental Assistance program and the Capital Incentive Grants program, which are currently under review. DSS is also designing its evaluation protocol for each program, the data collection requirements, HMIS data entry requirements and reporting requirements. DSS has agreed to allocate agency funds to be used in conjunction with CDBG funds to integrate fully the HMIS systems in the state that currently operate independently. A CDBG training workshop for local continuums of care was conducted for the Homeless Prevention and Rapid Re-housing program on July 19, 2007."],[112244,"An interagency agreement with the Department of Health and Hospitals (DHH) was executed in July. DHH will enter into sub-recipient agreements with the designated local lead agencies thereafter. Contracts between the providers of Permanent Supportive Housing (PSH) units and the local lead agencies will be entered into as the units are placed in service and have a PSH qualified tenant. CDBG training was conducted by ICF International for local lead agencies and DHH on July 13, 2007. DHH continues to meet with the local lead agencies on program compliance issues and negotiation of the cooperative endeavor agreements. The PSH set aside agreement was finalized for use when PSH units come on line."],[112382,"The Small Rental Property Program design was approved by LRA and OCD-DRU on December 28, 2006; the final scoring design was approved on January 5, 2007. The Small Rental application was approved by the LRA late in January, 2007. ICF International, which is administering the program, began accepting applications for the first of multiple competitive rounds on January 29, 2007, and continued receiving them through March 15, 2007, for the general pool, and through March 22, 2007 for the non-profit pool. The end of July marked the deadline for applicants to return their conditional award packages for Round 1 and the application deadline for Round 2. The total number of applications received was 6,734. Of all the applications received, 5,338 met basic eligibility requirements and were admitted to the Round 1 competition. The other 1,396 either were deferred or denied with the opportunity to re-apply and compete again in the next funding round. The total number of conditional award packages from Round 1 was 2,294. Of the award packages, 2,273 have been reviewed for completeness. Of this total, 941 were complete packages, meaning no information was missing. There were 462 withdrawn packages. During Round 1, $202,029,100 in federal funds was awarded to 2,693 applications, with an average award of $75,020 per project. The 2,645 eligible, but unfunded applicants may apply in Round 2. There have been 40 appeals and 38 have had determinations completed and resolved. Of the 2,693 awarded applications, 237 were owner-occupants of three- and four-unit properties. Non-profit organizations comprised 80 of the awarded applications. There were 2,376 awards made to applicants from a \"general pool\" of small owners of rental properties. For Round 1 awardees, 933 titles and 2,419 environme..."],[112722,"An activity narrative for this program can be found in the quarterly report for the 2nd Appropriation."],[114296,"This activity originally held some of the funds for Primary and Secondary Education, and the obligation above had been created for that purpose. It has since been broken out into the various activities."],[119185,"Action Plan One, Amendment 10, which includes $100,000,000 for match of Category A&B FEMA Public Assistance grants, was submitted to HUD on March 23 and approved by HUD on July 13, 2007. The application forms for PA match and FEMA-ineligible repairs to schools, along with instructions for completing them, were posted on the OCD-DRU website in May, allowing local governments to begin applying for CDBG funds to cover the 10% non-federal match on FEMA PA grants. E-mails were sent to each PA applicant in the state to let them know the application was available, and training sessions/input meetings were held with several potential applicants. On June 25, the LRA board passed a resolution reducing the amount of this program by $20,273,333 in order to provide additional funding to the Road Home program. On May 25, the President and Congress provided 100% federal funding for FEMA Public Assistance projects, reducing the required budget of this program to about $100 million. An amendment to Action Plan One will be completed to reallocate the excess funds to other recovery priorities."],[123366,"On February 9, 2007, HUD approved Action Plan One, Amendment 6 releasing funds for the implementation of this program. In March 2007, the contract was finalized between OCD-DRU and Entergy and $171,700,000 was transferred to Entergy New Orleans to mitigate rate increases in New Orleans. In May, Entergy submitted an additional request for payment for $5,092,614 for eligible activities performed during the month of March. In June, Entergy submitted an additional request for payment for $4,019,979 also for eligible activities performed during the month of March. Due to OCD-DRU staffing changes, monitoring of this project by the infrastructure and financial teams has been postponed. In the interim, OCD-DRU financial personnel are working with Entergy to establish financial monitoring protocols."],[137262,"Through the work of its various committees and task forces, the Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) has developed a series of strategic priorities and recovery plans in the areas of housing, infrastructure and economic development. The LRA has led the development of recovery policy and initiatives in the areas of housing, infrastructure, and economic development. These initiatives overlap with policy and planning in areas of health care, social services, education, and other subjects. The majority of LRA&apos;s work on the development, passage, submission and implementation of CDBG action plans has been tied to policies on housing, infrastructure and economic development -- the bedrocks of recovery. During 2006 and the first 2 quarters of 2007, the LRA focused heavily on economic and workforce development program development and implementation; infrastructure recovery programs and implementation issues, including education infrastructure design considerations; homeowner and rental housing program policy and implementation oversight issues; and community and regional planning efforts related to recovery priorities. Regarding the long-term planning process, the LRA collaborated with the American Institute of Architecture (AIA) and American Planning Association (APA) to develop rebuilding principles, upon which the LRA initiated a planning effort called Louisiana Speaks. This was a multifaceted planning initiative designed to address planning at every level. This extends from community planning such as the Unified New Orleans Plan (the LRA has supported UNOP through the recruitment of $3 million in private funding from the Rockefeller Foundation and the commitment of $2 million in CDBG funds for outreach, including communications and input with the New Orleans Diaspora)..."],[170198,"The Disaster Recovery Unit (DRU) of the Office of Community Development (OCD), Division of Administration (DOA), was created in the aftermath of the 2005 Hurricanes Katrina and Rita. The DRU administers the CDBG Disaster Recovery funds approved by Congress on December 23, 2005 and on June 30, 2007. Personnel were hired from within Louisiana and around the United States with experience in all aspects of CDBG programs. Managers and staff were retained in the areas of homeownership housing, multifamily/rental/supportive housing, infrastructure, economic development, policy and reporting, legal, and financial and auditing. Experienced employees of the Office of Community Development, CDBG Program, provide training as needed to new hires. In addition, the OCD continues to supply CDBG training in partnership with the Council of State Community Development Agencies and ICF International. The DOA&iquest;s resources are available to augment the DRU with expertise in budgeting, accounting, reporting, contract review and legal matters. The DRU is in close contact with the Louisiana Legislative Auditors. Currently, 8 auditors are assigned to the DRU. We are planning on adding 4 more auditors in the near future. At the close of this quarter, we had 55 people working in the DRU. The DRU received approval in September, 2006, to raise our total number of staff to 74. This does not include the 30 approved LRA positions. The administrative budget is used to fund salaries and related benefits, travel expenses, operating supplies and services, professional services, and inter-agency transfers. As mentioned last quarter, community meetings have been held throughout the impacted parishes and in cities with large numbers of evacuees. These meetings have raised citizen awareness of the reco..."],[105489,"An interagency agreement with the Department of Health and Hospitals (DHH) was executed in July. DHH will enter into sub-recipient agreements with the designated local lead agencies thereafter. Contracts between the providers of Permanent Supportive Housing (PSH) units and the local lead agencies will be entered into as the units are placed in service and have a PSH qualified tenant. CDBG training was conducted by ICF International for local lead agencies and DHH on July 13, 2007. DHH continues to meet with the local lead agencies on program compliance issues and negotiation of the cooperative endeavor agreements. The PSH set aside agreement was finalized for use when PSH units come on line."],[105614,"On June 25, the Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) board passed a resolution increasing the amount of total Road Home budget by moving $3 million from the infrastructure programs. For the entire Road Home program during this quarter 48,453 appointments were held, representing approximately 26.6% of the total appointments held (181,927). 45,475 applications were received. This is 24.6% of total applications received and recorded (184,653). 24,226 closings were held. This is approximately 41.6% of the total closing held (58,217) with each resulting in a disbursement amount greater than $0. Under the second appropriation specifically, $307,256,554.09 has been dispersed to low to moderate income individuals in 17,724 closings and $196,689,576.03 has been dispersed to elderly homeowners in 2,378 closings. Outcomes for this activity are reported in Katrina/Rita Grant 1 this quarter."],[105626,"On June 25, the Louisiana Recovery Authority (LRA) board passed a resolution increasing the amount of total Road Home budget by moving $3 million from the infrastructure programs. For the entire Road Home program during this quarter 48,453 appointments were held, representing approximately 26.6% of the total appointments held (181,927). 45,475 applications were received. This is 24.6% of total applications received and recorded (184,653). 24,226 closings were held. This is approximately 41.6% of the total closings held (58,217) with each resulting in a disbursement amount greater than $0. Under the second appropriation specifically, $51,990,363.87 was dispersed to non-LMI individuals and $196,689,576.03 was dispersed to elderly homeowners in 2,378 closings. Outcomes for this activity are reported in Katrina/Rita Grant 1 this quarter."],[105679,"The Small Rental Property Program design was approved by LRA and OCD-DRU December 28, 2006; the final scoring design was approved January 5, 2007. The Small Rental application was approved by the LRA late January, 2007. ICF International, who is administering the program, began accepting applications for the first of multiple competitive rounds January 29, 2007, and continued receiving them through March 15, 2007, for the general pool, and March 22, 2007 for the non-profit pool. The end of July marked the deadline for applicants to return their conditional award packages for Round 1 and the application deadline for Round 2. The total number of applications received was 6,734. Of all the applications received, 5,338 met basic eligibility requirements and were admitted to the Round 1 competition. The other 1,396 were either deferred or denied with the opportunity to re-apply and compete again in the next funding round. Total conditional award packages from Round 1 were 2,294. Of the award packages, 2,273 have been reviewed for completeness. Of this total, 941 were complete packages, meaning no information was missing. There were 462 withdrawn packages. During Round 1, $202,029,100 in federal funds were awarded to 2,693 applications, with the average award of $75,020 per project. The 2,645 eligible, but unfunded applicants may apply in Round 2. There have been 40 appeals and 38 have had determinations completed and resolved. Of the 2,693 awarded applications, 237 were owner-occupants of three and four-unit properties. Non-profit organizations comprised 80 of the awarded applications. 2,376 awards were made to applicants from a ¿general pool¿ of small owners of rental properties. For Round 1 awardees, 933 titles and 2,419 environmental reviews have been ordered. 12,050 a..."],[105814,"Due to the passage of the match waiver legislation, the \"match\" portion of this program is no longer necessary and the necessary amendment to Action Plan One will be submitted to reallocate those funds for other purposes. During the regular session of 2007, the Louisiana Legislature passed and the Governor signed an appropriations bill that provides funding for the \"FEMA-ineligible\" portion of this program. On June 25, the LRA board passed a resolution effectively removing funding from this program. As such, an amendment to Action Plan One will be completed and processed to eliminate funding for this program. The $9,395,767 already expended for match of FEMA PA was returned to the program by Facility Planning and Control."],[105848,"Tiffany Apartments will have 250 mixed-income units with total development costs at $42,000,000. Construction has not yet begun on this property, but the contract has been signed and the project cleared environmental review on 4/24/2007. At close, the funds leveraged detail is: Tax Credits 17,663,000.00 Perm Loan 3,775,000.00 Deferred Developer Fee 719,503.00"],[105864,"Lakeside Apartments are located in St. Tammany Parish on the North Shore of Lake Pontchartrain on Oak Harbor Blvd, Slidell, LA. The total development cost for this project is $39,000,000. The contract for this development has been signed and was cleared for environmental review, but construction has not yet begun. Detail of leveraged funds: First Mortgage Lender; $5,250,000.00 Tax Credit Equity; $17,039,000.00 Deferred Developer Fee; $1,655,500.00"],[105959,"The Preserve development has cleared environmental review and held a groundbreaking on August 24, 2007 to mark the beginning of the construction process. Total funds leveraged include the following: Net Investor Equity 16,466,000.00 First Mortgage Lender 8,500,000.00 Deferred Developer Fee 2,023,030.00 Interest Earnings 35,584.00"],[105975,"The project has cleared environmental review and ground breaking ceremonies were held on August 24, 2007 to mark the beginning of construction for the Crescent Club development project. The following details funds leveraged for this project at close: First Mortgage Lender; $10,740,000.00 Tax Credit Equity; $20,402,000.00 Deferred Developer Fee; $2,129,474.00 Interest Earnings; $39,969.00"],[106013,"The 200 Carondelet project was the first Piggyback project to close and is one of the few acquisition/rehabilitation projects in the Piggyback portfolio. The project has already cleared environmental review. The total development costs are $70,000,000. Other funds leveraged include: First Mortgage Lender; $4,950,000.00 Tax Credit Equity; $32,077,214.00 Deferred Developer Fee; $6,138,618.00"],[106278,"The Walnut Square Apartments represent the first project closed in New Orleans East. The environmental review for this project is still pending. The total development costs for this project are $37,000,000. Other funds leveraged include: Federal Investor Equity $13,748,007.00 Convetional 1st Mortgage $2,618,885.00 Loan from NHP - Bush/Clinton $600,000.00 Loan from NHPF - Neighborhood Works $250,000.00 Ford Foundation Funds $1,045,000.00"],[170142,"The Disaster Recovery Unit (DRU) of the Office of Community Development (OCD), Division of Administration (DOA), was created in the aftermath of the 2005 Hurricanes Katrina and Rita. The DRU administers the CDBG Disaster Recovery funds approved by Congress on December 23, 2005 and on June 30, 2007. Personnel were hired from within Louisiana and around the United States with experience in all aspects of CDBG programs. Managers and staff were retained in the areas of homeownership housing, multifamily/rental/supportive housing, infrastructure, economic development, policy and reporting, legal, and financial and auditing. Experienced employees of the Office of Community Development, CDBG Program, provide training as needed to new hires. In addition, the OCD continues to supply CDBG training in partnership with the Council of State Community Development Agencies and ICF International. The DOA¿s resources are available to augment the DRU with expertise in budgeting, accounting, reporting, contract review and legal matters. The DRU is in close contact with the Louisiana Legislative Auditors. Currently, 8 auditors are assigned to the DRU. We are planning on adding 4 more auditors in the near future. At the close of this quarter, we had 55 people working in the DRU. The DRU received approval in September, 2006, to raise our total number of staff to 74. The administrative budget is used to fund salaries and related benefits, travel expenses, operating supplies and services, professional services, and inter-agency transfers. As mentioned last quarter, community meetings have been held throughout the impacted parishes and in cities with large numbers of evacuees. These meetings have raised citizen awareness of the recovery programs as well as serving as forums for citizens to d..."],[2706,"For the current reporting period an adjusting entry of $540.00 was required to reverse a prior period entry."],[2727,"The City of Gulfport reported 2 businesses inspected during the current quarter but has not yet submitted an expenditure request to the Mississippi Development Authority."],[2739,"Hancock County submitted a request for reimbursement with the Mississippi Development Authority after the current quarter end. The County recently submitted a $318,696 request for reimbursement for salary, fringe benefit, travel and training expenses incurred between 7/14/06 and 6/1/07."],[2803,"The City of Pass Christian reported inspector activity during the current quarter. A request for expense reimbursement was not received during the current quarter. Subsequent to quarter end, the city requested a reimbursement of $6,111 for the period between 6/1/07 and 10/31/07."],[4361,"Note: Based on data supplied by the Mississippi Windstorm Underwriting Association, the Number of Households Benefitting and Number of Policies underwritten by the Association grew significantly more than original projections due to the private market&apos;s reluctance and/or refusal to offer affordable wind policies to residential customers on the Mississippi Gulf Coast. A final payment of $20 million is anticipated to be made in November, 2007."],[5978,"Note: Contruction to be completed in 3 to 5 years"],[7843,"$5 million of this activity was transferred to 18A-04 Economic Development - Tourism."],[10713,"A toll free number has been established and publicized for the general public to report suspected fraud. As of 9/30/07, 30 cases have been forwarded to authorities for possible prosecution."]];
